From 59642799fcbbd93ae43caf806e6cbefe7703dfb4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LiBr <me@nvme0n1p.dev>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 10:06:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/47] Z 6III: the loader, the image in DRAM, and both cores
 booting

smoke.sh now passes both checks: the RTOS runs 45 s with nothing stopping,
and Linux reaches a BusyBox prompt in the same machine (146 serial lines,
40,917 distinct blocks).

The largest piece was not a device model. The EG image is not fully flat
once the MMU is on: the firmware's own initial translation tables map three
windows of its text into DRAM off a base 0xa15c00000, and the functions that
would install those mappings and copy the text (sub_41189490,
sub_411893F8) are never executed on the way there. So the mapping is the
starting condition and the image is expected to be in DRAM already -- the
loader's "Program Load" step. Placing it there took undefined-instruction
exceptions from 102,715 per 30 s to zero. The content rule is measured, not
assumed: the firmware re-copies the first window itself and that DRAM comes
out 45/45 pages byte-identical to the image at the same VA.

The loader band itself (0x40000000-0x40160000) is now loadable with
-machine loader=<file>, which removed the stop at pc 0x40168a04. Two earlier
readings are withdrawn in the docs: that band is not missing from our
inputs, and 0x40168a04 is not a fault record -- it is the sync vector's own
STP X0,X1,[SP] with an SP that was never set.

Blocks modelled this round: EXS1780 and EXS1784 (the two windows
storage-registers.md section 8 had deliberately left unmapped), BLK-0x15D8,
the three DSP instances the driver's handle init names at 0x40c0a254,
DSP-0x1B800000, and the DSP local memory at 0x1b000000.

Two method findings, written up in register-windows.md:

  - A sixth base shape: an offset computed by a loop whose extent is an
    argument. No static set can enumerate it, so an enumerated set produces
    false stops. Z63RegWindow.dense is for exactly those windows.
  - A window reachable by a 64-bit access must put that width in
    MemoryRegionOps.valid and narrow it in impl. QEMU turns an access
    outside valid into a bus error instead of calling the handler, so the
    model refuses in a way the board never sees -- the RTOS's own console
    reported it as a data abort in task [STSQNOR] at FAR 0x16807F10.

Still open, both recorded in plan.md: the display has no frame (the scanout
refuses with output size 0x0 from +0x132c/+0x1328), and the Linux hand-off
is still a stand-in -- the firmware now gets far enough to release CPU3
itself and asks for it at 0x40160000, the RTOS image entry, so it is
claiming CPU3 as a fourth RTOS core rather than handing it to Linux.

