+# The 0x08000000 band: on-chip boot ROM, and 131 sites that are not addresses
+
+**Verdict: not a device, not shared SRAM, and not a register file. No model is
+warranted and none is supplied.**
+
+The band is the low end of **OCROM**, a 2 MiB on-chip mask ROM at
+0x08000000..0x08200000, declared by the image's own SOLID memory-region table
+(section 3). The firmware never forms an address in it, by any of the three
+means AArch64 has. The 131 code sites that a constant scan attributes to the
+page are all the single value 0x08000000 used as **bit 27 of a 32-bit data
+word** — the same false positive already documented and withdrawn for
+0x10000000 in [mmio-coverage.md](mmio-coverage.md), arising in part from the
+very same functions.
+
+Confidence: high on both halves. The negative (never addressed) rests on four
+exhaustive searches that each return zero while a control case returns the
+known-good answer. The positive (it is OCROM) rests on a self-validating table
+that independently reproduces nine addresses this board already models.
+
+This supersedes the `0x08000000 | mixed; conmgrle.c, A:NIKON001.DSC ... probably
+shared SRAM rather than one device` row in mmio-coverage.md's ranking.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Why it is not a device
+
+Five tests. Addresses are image/VA addresses as the rest of this tree cites
+them; "real text" means below the text segment end at 0x4184640C, which
+matters because IDA disassembles rodata past that point and invents
+instructions there.
+
+### 1.1 Only one value is ever materialised, and it is the page base
+
+Every immediate operand in 0x08000000..0x08009000 that lies in real text:
+
+| value | sites | distinct functions |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **0x08000000** | **131** | **102** |
+| 0x08000050 | 3 | 3 |
+| 0x08000001 | 3 | 3 |
+| 0x08008000 | 1 | 1 |
+| 0x08000800 | 1 | 1 |
+| 0x080007FE | 1 | 1 |
+| 0x08000002 | 1 | 1 |
+
+A real register block produces a spread of distinct offsets from one cluster of
+functions; the SD band is 631 materialisations of 124 addresses, the display
+band 462 of 211. This band is 131 materialisations of **one** address, which is
+the base itself, plus ten scattered singletons in ten unrelated functions
+(0x4052F780, 0x402ED1BC, 0x4120C544, 0x4089E924, 0x40348C3C, 0x40D52A6C and
+neighbours). 0x080007FE is not even 4-byte aligned.
+
+0x08000000 is 128 MiB, and a single set bit (bit 27). mmio-coverage.md's own
+rule applies without modification: *a page whose only referenced address is its
+own base, which is a round power of two, and which is referenced by hundreds of
+unrelated functions, is a constant.*
+
+### 1.2 The accesses are 32-bit and bitwise, not loads and stores
+
+The 131 sites break down by mnemonic as MOV 116, ORR 11, TST 2, EOR 1, AND 1,
+and **130 of the 131 are a `W` (32-bit) form**. A pointer cannot live in a `W`
+register, and a `TST`/`AND`/`EOR` against a base is not addressing. The single
+64-bit form is the bit-27 accumulation quoted below, so after it there are
+**zero** sites that could be an address. Two sites settle the meaning outright:
+
+**A bitfield tag.** At 0x402AC0C8, in the function at 0x402ABAD4:
+
+```
+    MOV     W1, #0x8000000
+    BFXIL   W1, W0, #0, #0x18      ; W1[23:0] = W0[23:0]
+```
+
+`BFXIL` inserts the low 24 bits of W0 into W1 and leaves W1[31:24] = 0x08.
+0x08000000 is the top byte of a packed word, written to be overwritten below
+bit 24. An address would not survive that.
+
+**A bit in a presence mask.** The *only* 64-bit form of this constant anywhere
+in the image is at 0x40CB15C4, in the function at 0x40CB1358, and it is the
+third of four consecutive accumulations:
+
+```
+    ORR     X20, X20, #0x2000000   ; bit 25
+    LDR     X1, [X19,#0x28] ; CBZ ; BL ...
+    ORR     X20, X20, #0x4000000   ; bit 26
+    LDR     X1, [X19,#0x30] ; CBZ ; BL ...
+    ORR     X20, X20, #0x8000000   ; bit 27   <-- the "band reference"
+    LDR     X1, [X19,#0x40] ; CBZ ; BL ...
+    ORR     X20, X20, #0x10000000  ; bit 28
+```
+
+X20 is a mask recording which optional callbacks in the record at X19 were
+present. Bit 27 is one flag in a run of four.
+
+The dominant shape of the remaining sites is an argument, not a dereference —
+`BL`, then `MOV W1, #0x8000000`, then `BL` into the thin accessor family at
+0x403EDDF0/0x403EDE4C/0x403EDEA8/0x403EDECC/0x403EDEDC (16–48 bytes each, each
+loading a global and tail-calling 0x40D32590/0x40D325CC/0x40D32668). Examples
+at 0x4039ADC4, 0x406634E4, 0x40571584. 0x403EDEDC's signature is
+`f(__int64 *a1, __int64 a2)` returning a `u32` by reference: 0x08000000 is its
+second argument, an id or mask, in a `W` register.
+
+The board already recorded this independently. `nikon-z63.c`, in the sequencer
+comment for offset 0x020: *"The first value the boot puts here is 0x08000000
+(bit 27)."* That is this constant, observed at runtime, written as data into a
+register at 0x0D158020.
+
+### 1.3 No ADRP reaches the band
+
+Zero `ADRP` instructions in the image target any page of 0x08000000..0x08009000.
+
+The control case proves the search works: `ADRP X16, #0x8201000@PAGE` at
+0x416684D0 is found — one of the six ROM-service veneers the board documents.
+When this firmware does address a page in the 0x08 decade, it looks like that,
+and nothing in my band does.
+
+### 1.4 No literal pool entry, and no `.data` pointer
+
+Lesson of the display bases: a base kept in a struct never appears as an
+immediate. So the image was scanned byte-wise for in-band values:
+
+- **8-byte aligned, whole 88,830,367-byte image: 29 hits.** Twenty-one are in
+  rodata and are 32-bit fields followed by a zero word (0x08000089, 0x0800011B,
+  0x080002C4, 0x080004E1 — non-recurring, not page-structured).
+- **Zero 64-bit in-band values inside `.text`**, so there is no literal pool
+  entry either.
+- **Eight are in `.data`, and not one is a pointer.** Two are entry 0's own
+  `start` and `start_again` fields in the region table of section 3. The other
+  six were each inspected in context, and all six are a 32-bit field in the low
+  half of an 8-byte-aligned slot with a zero high half:
+
+  | VA | content | what the neighbours show |
+  | --- | --- | --- |
+  | 0xA02B266C8 | 0x08000000 | **a bitmask table** — see below |
+  | 0xA02841308 | 0x08000000 | sits beside 0x02000000 in a list of sizes |
+  | 0xA02938C30 | 0x08000000 | flag word heading a descriptor whose next two fields are the text pointers 0x40E94A80 / 0x40E94AA4 |
+  | 0xA0280C9D0 | 0x08000000 | followed by 0x1BB, 0, 0xC9 — a parameter block |
+  | 0xA02823340 | 0x08000210 | a *value* in a `{pointer, value}` pair list; the neighbouring keys are 0x4035ED1C / 0x4035ED20 |
+  | 0xA02B26568 | 0x08000800 | amid 0x80008000, 0x80007FFF, 0x10001000 — packed 16-bit coefficient data |
+
+The first of those is the clearest single piece of evidence in this document.
+At 0xA02B266B0 there is a plain table of single bits:
+
+```
+    0xA02B266B0   0x01000000
+    0xA02B266B8   0x02000000
+    0xA02B266C0   0x04000000
+    0xA02B266C8   0x08000000   <-- this band's entire "reference"
+    0xA02B266D0   0x10000000   <-- the page already withdrawn as a constant
+    0xA02B266D8   0x20000000
+    0xA02B266E0   0x40000000
+```
+
+This band's value and 0x10000000 — the page mmio-coverage.md already withdrew
+after mis-calling it "Bluetooth / HCI" — are **adjacent entries in the same
+bitmask lookup table**. They are the same kind of object, and they were the
+same mistake.
+
+There is no hidden base, and no recurring page-aligned value of the kind the
+display bases did show (0x15480000 and 0x15C80000 recur in descriptors at
+0xA02808438). For completeness: `.data` sits at 0xA02808000, 39.0 GB from the
+text at 0x401xxxxx, so `ADRP` (±4 GB) cannot reach it; a 64-bit MOVZ/MOVK run
+or a literal load is the only way, and both were searched.
+
+### 1.5 The referencing functions share no subsystem
+
+The 102 functions span 0x40179EB8 to 0x41634950 — the entire 23 MB text, in
+fourteen separate megabyte-wide zones. Resolving every string each function
+builds through a contiguous MOVZ/MOVK run gives, for the whole set of 102:
+
+```
+  1  'A:NIKON001.DSC'      1  'CVALGDBG'   1  'CVLVPCAM'   1  'NP_VEH'
+  1  'CVALGMLT'            1  'CVLVPCMT'   1  'CVLVPMTX'   1  'NP_ANM'
+  1  'CVLVPEF0'            1  'CVLVPEF1'   1  'CVLVPMEF'   1  'TM_REG'
+  1  'mele.c'  1  'MeSendHciCommand(0x2024, 4) == 2'
+  1  'IsEntryAvailable(&(MEC(hciGenCmd).node))'
+```
+
+**No string is shared by even two of the 102 functions.** Each name cited as
+evidence for this band comes from exactly one function: `A:NIKON001.DSC` from
+0x40328660, the four `CV*` names all from the single function 0x40FD1ABC.
+
+And two of the functions are `mele.c` / `MeSendHciCommand` at 0x41237FEC —
+the precise string evidence that produced the "Bluetooth / HCI" identification
+of 0x10000000 which mmio-coverage.md records as spurious and withdrawn. The
+same mechanism, partly the same functions, and it should be withdrawn here for
+the same reason.
+
+(Method note: strings were resolved from contiguous MOVZ/MOVK runs inside each
+function body. `conmgrle.c` did not appear in the set by that method; it may be
+reached from a caller or by another form. The absence of any *shared* string is
+the finding, and it does not depend on that one name.)
+
+---
+
+## 2. What that leaves
+
+Four exhaustive searches — instruction immediates, `ADRP`, text literal pools,
+and 8-byte-aligned values across the whole image — return nothing that forms an
+address in 0x08000000..0x08009000. The `eg` module never reads or writes this
+band. Whatever is there, this firmware does not touch it.
+
+---
+
+## 3. What the band actually is: the SOLID memory-region table
+
+The band is nonetheless real hardware, and the image says what.
+
+At `.data` VA **0xA02BD2380** (image address **0x455C3380**, file offset
+0x05463380) there are **29 entries of 0x28 bytes**:
+
+```c
+struct solid_region {
+    uint64_t start;
+    uint64_t size;
+    uint64_t attr;
+    const char *name;    /* into the blob at 0xA02BD29A8 = image 0x455C39A8 */
+    uint64_t start_again; /* equals .start in all 29 entries */
+};
+```
+
+The field assignment is not a guess. `start + size` equals the next entry's
+`start` in **21 of the 28 adjacencies**, the other seven being deliberate gaps;
+the name pointers are exactly 0x28 apart across all 29 entries; and the name
+blob is 29 contiguous NUL-terminated strings with nothing left over. Entries 15
+and 16 reproduce this board's own `Z63_IMAGE_WINDOW_BASE` 0x40000000 and
+`Z63_IMAGE_LOAD_BASE` 0x40160000 as a region boundary.
+
+| # | start | size | end | attr | name |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 0 | **0x08000000** | **0x00200000** | **0x08200000** | **0x00000004** | **OCROM** |
+| 1 | 0x08200000 | 0x00200000 | 0x08400000 | 0x80000000 | WorkSRAM |
+| 2 | 0x08400000 | 0x00200000 | 0x08600000 | 0x80000000 | PCIe_mem |
+| 3 | 0x0C000000 | 0x00800000 | 0x0C800000 | 0x80000000 | CoreSight |
+| 4 | 0x0C800000 | 0x00200000 | 0x0CA00000 | 0x80000000 | SNAP |
+| 5 | 0x0D150000 | 0x00010000 | 0x0D160000 | 0x80000000 | TOP |
+| 6 | 0x0E000000 | 0x01000000 | 0x0F000000 | 0x80000000 | PERI |
+| 7 | 0x0F000000 | 0x00200000 | 0x0F200000 | 0x80000000 | UART |
+| 8 | 0x10000000 | 0x04000000 | 0x14000000 | 0x80000000 | DDR |
+| 9 | 0x14000000 | 0x01000000 | 0x15000000 | 0x80000000 | NoC |
+| 10 | 0x15000000 | 0x00800000 | 0x15800000 | 0x80000000 | ISP_APB |
+| 11 | 0x15800000 | 0x00800000 | 0x16000000 | 0x80000000 | ISP_AHB_AXI |
+| 12 | 0x16000000 | 0x00800000 | 0x16800000 | 0x80000000 | CODEC |
+| 13 | 0x16800000 | 0x04800000 | 0x1B000000 | 0x80000000 | EXS |
+| 14 | 0x1B000000 | 0x05000000 | 0x20000000 | 0x80000000 | DSP |
+| 15 | 0x40000000 | 0x00160000 | 0x40160000 | 0x00000004 | LOADER |
+| 16 | 0x40160000 | 0x05820000 | 0x45980000 | 0x00000002 | SOLID |
+| 17 | 0x45980000 | 0x02680000 | 0x48000000 | 0x00000004 | SNOR_USER |
+| 18 | 0xE0000000 | 0x20000000 | 0x100000000 | 0x80000000 | CSRAM |
+| 19 | 0x100000000 | 0x100000000 | 0x200000000 | 0x80000000 | Comp_AXI0 |
+| 20 | 0x200000000 | 0x200000000 | 0x400000000 | 0x80000000 | Comp_AXI1 |
+| 21 | 0x400000000 | 0x200000000 | 0x600000000 | 0x80000000 | PCIe_mem_AXI0 |
+| 22 | 0x600000000 | 0x200000000 | 0x800000000 | 0x80000000 | PCIe_mem_AXI1 |
+| 23 | 0x840000000 | 0x0C0000000 | 0x900000000 | 0x00000000 | BUFFER_EXT |
+| 24 | 0xA00000000 | 0x002800000 | 0xA02800000 | 0x00000000 | SUB_PROC |
+| 25 | 0xA02800000 | 0x002E00000 | 0xA05600000 | 0x00000000 | SOLID_RWDATA |
+| 26 | 0xA05600000 | 0x000C00000 | 0xA06200000 | 0x00000000 | SOLID_HEAP |
+| 27 | 0xA06200000 | 0x001200000 | 0xA07400000 | 0x00000000 | OSSTACK |
+| 28 | 0xA07400000 | 0x038C00000 | 0xA40000000 | 0x00000000 | BUFFER |
+
+**Entry 0 is the answer: 0x08000000, 2 MiB, "OCROM" — on-chip ROM.** The
+0x08000000..0x08009000 band is the first 36 KiB of the SoC's mask boot ROM.
+
+It is **not** SRAM. The SRAM is entry 1, "WorkSRAM", a *different* declared
+region beginning at 0x08200000 — and that is precisely where both already-modelled
+objects live: the ROM service page at 0x08201000 and the boot mailbox at
+0x0821F000. The brief's two "known nearby" objects are in WorkSRAM, not in this
+band, so the shared-SRAM hypothesis attaches to the wrong region.
+
+It is also **not CSRAM**, which entry 18 puts at 0xE0000000 with a size of
+512 MiB.
+
+### 3.1 The table validates against what the board already models
+
+Every base this board declares falls inside a named region, and in several
+cases the board independently chose the same name:
+
+| board symbol | value | region |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `Z63_ROMPAGE_BASE`, `Z63_MAILBOX_BASE` | 0x08201000, 0x0821F000 | WorkSRAM |
+| `Z63_AUX_BASE`, `Z63_PAIR_A_BASE` | 0x0C86E000, 0x0C850000 | SNAP |
+| `Z63_TOP_BASE` and the 0x0D15xxxx block | 0x0D150000..0x0D158000 | **TOP** |
+| `Z63_EMIO_E0/E1/E2_BASE`, `Z63_SPI_BASE` | 0x0E000000..0x0E700000 | PERI |
+| `Z63_UART_BASE` | 0x0F000000 | **UART** (exact base) |
+| `Z63_BLK_BASE` | 0x11800000 | DDR |
+| `Z63_VPIPE_BASE`, `Z63_UPK_BASE` | 0x14000000, 0x14C00000 | NoC (exact base) |
+| `Z63_DISP_*` | 0x15480000, 0x15496000, 0x1548C000 | ISP_APB |
+| `Z63_DISP_WIN2_BASE` | 0x15C80000 | ISP_AHB_AXI |
+| `Z63_PAIR_B_BASE`, `Z63_SDIO_BASE`, `Z63_UHS2L/SDH40` | 0x16807000, 0x1782xxxx | **EXS** |
+| `Z63_IMAGE_WINDOW_BASE` / `Z63_IMAGE_LOAD_BASE` | 0x40000000 / 0x40160000 | LOADER / SOLID |
+| `Z63_DRAM_BASE` | 0xA00000000 | SUB_PROC |
+| `Z63_LI_MEM_BASE` | 0xA07800000 | BUFFER |
+
+The SD controllers landing in **EXS** matches their interrupt names
+`EXS_SDIO_*` / `EXS_UHS2_*` exactly, so the region names and the
+interrupt-source names at rodata 0x41847000 are the same namespace. This table
+is therefore a second naming key for unmodelled blocks, and a coarser but more
+reliable one: it covers address space rather than interrupt lines.
+
+Two modelled bases fall in *gaps*: the GIC at 0x0D000000/0x0D080000, and
+`Z63_INT_LATCH_BASE` 0x0D100004 / `Z63_COUNTER_BASE` 0x0D140000. The table
+describes what SOLID maps, not everything that answers.
+
+### 3.2 The attribute column
+
+Grouping the 29 values — **this is inference from the grouping, not measured
+hardware behaviour**, and nothing downstream should depend on it:
+
+- `0x80000000` — all nineteen peripheral/MMIO regions. Device memory.
+- `0x00000000` — all six RAM regions. Normal cacheable.
+- `0x00000002` — SOLID alone, the executing RTOS image.
+- `0x00000004` — OCROM, LOADER, SNOR_USER: the three read-only, non-RAM
+  backing stores (mask ROM, loader stage, serial NOR user area — the last
+  matching `SERIAL_NOR` in the interrupt table).
+
+OCROM being grouped with ROM and flash rather than with the device regions is a
+second, independent reason not to model it as a register file.
+
+### 3.3 The second table, and the two modelled objects
+
+Immediately after, at 0xA02BD2808 (image 0x455C3808), thirteen entries of 0x20
+bytes, `{u32 type, u32 attr, u64 start, u64 end, u64 extra}`:
+
+| # | type | attr | start | end | extra |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 0 | 1 | 0x0F | 0x08201000 | 0x08202000 | — |
+| 1 | 1 | 0x0F | 0x0821E000 | 0x0821F000 | — |
+| 2 | 1 | 0x0F | 0xA02BDA000 | 0xA02BDD000 | — |
+| 3 | 1 | 0x0F | 0xA02BDD000 | 0xA02BDD000 | — |
+| 4 | 1 | 0x28 | 0xA00000000 | 0xA02800000 | — |
+| 5 | 1 | 0x4C | 0xA07800000 | 0xA15C00000 | — |
+| 6 | 1 | 0x28 | 0x840000000 | 0x900000000 | — |
+| 7 | 1 | 0x28 | 0xA19800000 | 0xA40000000 | — |
+| 8 | 2 | 0x0C | 0xA02BDE000 | 0xA02C26190 | 0xA02BDE000 |
+| 9 | 2 | 0x0D | 0x455CF000 | 0x45617190 | 0x455CF000 |
+| 10 | 2 | 0x0D | 0x40195000 | 0x40196000 | 0x40195000 |
+| 11 | 2 | 0x0C | 0xC40000000 | 0xD00000000 | 0x840000000 |
+| 12 | 2 | 0x0C | 0xE00000000 | 0xE40000000 | 0xA00000000 |
+
+Entries 0 and 1 are the two objects already modelled, and they are fine-grained
+4 KiB windows inside WorkSRAM — not inside this band. Entries 11 and 12 have a
+`start` differing from `extra`, so type 2 is plausibly a translated (aliased)
+mapping while type 1 is identity; that is **not established** and nothing here
+rests on it.
+
+One discrepancy worth recording: the board places the boot mailbox at
+0x0821F000, while entry 1 declares `[0x0821E000, 0x0821F000)` — the mailbox
+address is this window's *exclusive end*, not its start. The board's comment
+already notes it as "the page below the boot mailbox". The mailbox model is
+driven by observed guest behaviour (magic at +0xFC0, entries at +0xFD8) and is
+not in question; the open question is only whether this table uses inclusive
+end pages, which one entry cannot settle.
+
+---
+
+## 4. The other pages named in the brief
+
+| address | finding |
+| --- | --- |
+| 0x08400000 | Inside **PCIe_mem** (entry 2). Its only 8-byte-aligned occurrences in the image are two fields of the region table itself, plus one rodata word. No driver keeps it. |
+| 0x08495000 | **Appears nowhere in the image, at any width or alignment.** Zero 4-byte and zero 8-byte occurrences in 88,830,367 bytes. It did not come from this image. |
+| 0x08C00000 | 27 four-byte occurrences, **zero** eight-byte. Falls in the undeclared gap 0x08600000..0x0C000000. 0x08C00000 is 140 MiB — a size, not a page. |
+| 0x08D00000 | 14 four-byte, zero eight-byte, same gap. 141 MiB. |
+| 0x08F70000 | One occurrence in all 88 MB. Noise. |
+
+None belongs with this band, and none is a device base. For contrast,
+0x08200000 itself has 85 eight-byte occurrences — overwhelmingly a *size*: the
+boot header declares the image window as 0x08200000 bytes (136 MiB), which the
+board's own `Z63_IMAGE_WINDOW_SIZE` comment records.
+
+---
+
+## 5. What the board should do
+
+**Leave 0x08000000..0x08200000 unmapped.** No change to `nikon-z63.c` is
+needed, and the honest reasoning is the one the board already applies to the
+ROM service page:
+
+- The firmware never accesses it (section 2), so mapping it changes no observed
+  behaviour.
+- It is mask ROM whose contents are in no firmware image — the same argument
+  the board makes for 0x08201000: *"the contents come from a stage that runs
+  before `eg`"*. Mapping it as RAM would serve zeros to an instruction fetch;
+  mapping it as a register file would mean inventing registers that do not
+  exist.
+- Unmapped is a live tripwire. If a future run with the module lifecycle driven
+  does reach OCROM, QEMU stops with the offending PC, which is new information.
+  A region returning 0 would hide exactly that event.
+
+Should a labelled stub ever be wanted, the precedent is `rom-page-stub`:
+off by default, `z63_stop_read`/`z63_stop_write` on every offset, and a comment
+citing the firmware address that justifies each one. There is at present no
+firmware address to cite, which is the whole finding.
+
+The one change worth making is to the **ranking in mmio-coverage.md**: this
+band should leave the "largest uncovered blocks" table, because it is not a
+block. That drops the third-ranked entry and promotes USB (0x1A000000, 110
+sites, `EXS_USB3_TYPE_C_CONTROLLER`) to third.
+
+---
+
+## 6. What I could not determine
+
+- **Who consumes the region table.** Nothing in this image forms its address:
+  no contiguous MOVZ/MOVK run in either the bootstrap segment (file 0..0x8000)
+  or the 23 MB text produces 0xA02BD2380, 0xA02BD2808 or 0xA02BD29A8; there are
+  no 64-bit literals pointing at them; and `ADRP` cannot span the 39 GB to
+  `.data`. The only pointer to the name blob anywhere in the image is entry 0's
+  own `name` field. So the table is build-time data read either by a stage
+  outside this module — plausibly the `LOADER` region the table itself declares
+  at 0x40000000, which is not part of `eg` — or through pointer arithmetic from
+  the adjacent config block at 0xA02BD2300, which *is* referenced (62 runs from
+  low text, run starts 0x4017A434..0x4019180C). **I could not distinguish these,
+  and I did not establish who writes the MMU from it.**
+- **What is in OCROM.** The contents are not in this image and cannot be
+  recovered from it. The 2 MiB extent is the table's declaration, not a
+  measurement.
+- **Whether OCROM has internal structure.** The second table declares
+  sub-windows inside WorkSRAM but none inside OCROM, so nothing in the image
+  distinguishes offsets within the band.
+- **The attr encoding** (section 3.2) is inferred from grouping only.
+- **What 0x08000000 means as a flag.** It is bit 27 of something, and in at
+  least three distinct roles: a packed word's top byte (0x402AC0CC), one of a
+  run of four presence bits (0x40CB15C4), and an argument to the 0x403EDExx
+  accessors. I did not identify the enum behind the third, and 0x08000050 /
+  0x08000800 / 0x08008000 in their ten functions were not chased individually.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Where this brief was wrong
+
+Recorded because the ranking that produced it is still in use:
+
+1. **"Probably shared SRAM"** — no. OCROM is ROM; the SRAM is the adjacent
+   region. The hypothesis attached to the wrong one of two neighbouring 2 MiB
+   regions.
+2. **"126 code sites across 122 functions"** — 131 sites in 102 functions, and
+   all 131 are one non-address constant. The count is sound; its
+   interpretation was not.
+3. **"0x08400000, 0x08495000, 0x08C00000, 0x08D00000, 0x08F70000 — all reached
+   only through pointers in `.data`"** — none of these is reached through a
+   `.data` pointer. 0x08C00000, 0x08D00000 and 0x08F70000 have **zero**
+   eight-byte-aligned occurrences anywhere in the image, and **0x08495000 does
+   not occur in the image at all**.
+4. **"`CSRAM` and the several IPCU families are worth particular attention
+   given what your band looks like"** — CSRAM is 512 MiB at 0xE0000000
+   (entry 18), nowhere near this band.
+5. **"Access is uniformly 32-bit ... if your band is SRAM it very likely will
+   not be, and that is itself evidence"** — the band is uniformly 32-bit, and
+   that turned out to be evidence of something else entirely: not a narrow
+   register file, but a value that was never an address.
+
+The brief's own lesson 3 named the trap exactly, including the instruction to
+apply it to 0x08000000 first. It was the right call: the band fails that test,
+and this document is the result.
