+# The display band, 0x15400000 – 0x15700000
+
+Source: `analysis/Z6III_0200/extracted/components/eg2010_0200i1.bi`,
+sha256 `736ceaacdd63dc233e51d68beb85378242788e2467773e762b038b2f41c47fda`.
+All firmware addresses below are VAs in that image's text segment
+(0x40168000 – 0x4184640C); file offset = VA − 0x40160000.
+
+Every statement here is followed by the address that establishes it. Where
+something is an inference, it says so. Where it was not determined, it says
+that instead of guessing.
+
+---
+
+## 0. How the band was enumerated, and why the brief's census is misleading
+
+The band's addresses were collected by decoding every `MOVZ`/`MOVK` chain in
+the text segment whose high half lands in 0x1540–0x157F, resolving each pair
+to a full constant. That yields **462 materialisation sites building 211
+distinct constants in 325 functions**. The function count matches the brief
+exactly; the site count is one third of the brief's 1341 because the brief
+appears to count the `MOVZ` + `MOVK` + `LDR`/`STR` triple per site.
+
+Four of the 211 constants are not addresses at all and must be discarded:
+`0x1555554A` (0x40302b78), `0x15555555` (0x408c290c), `0x15555556`
+(0x40812e8c) and `0x15752618` (0x4076222c) are division-by-constant
+reciprocal multipliers, not MMIO.
+
+**The brief's central assumption is wrong in a way that matters.** It says
+the Z 8's display-controller base 0x15480000 is "nearly unreferenced" here
+(21 sites) and its LUT base 0x15c80000 is unreferenced (0 sites), so the
+Z 8 model should be treated as a semantic hint only. In fact *both bases are
+live in this image*: they are not reached as immediates but through pointers
+in `.data`, which no constant search can see. The initialised table at
+`0xA02808438` (file offset 0x5099438) holds three instance descriptors of
+0x38 bytes:
+
+| object | +0x00 id | +0x08 window 0 | +0x10 window 1 | +0x18 window 2 | +0x30 index |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 0xA02808438 | 0x1C7 (455) | **0x15480000** | 0x15490800 | **0x15C80000** | 1 |
+| 0xA02808470 | 0x1C8 (456) | **0x15484000** | 0x15490900 | **0x15C84000** | 2 |
+| 0xA028084A8 | 0x1C9 (457) | 0 | 0 | 0 | — |
+
+So there are two populated display units at 0x15480000 and 0x15484000 (0x4000
+apart, four 0x1000 sub-blocks each), a third descriptor with no windows of its
+own, and a second window per unit at 0x15C80000/0x15C84000 — i.e. exactly the
+Z 8's "display controller" and "LUT" bases. The Z 8 board's bases transfer;
+only its *offsets* remain unverified here.
+
+The pointers are consumed at 0x402b0600 (`0xA02808438`) and
+0x402b0614 / 0x402b0628 (`0xA02808470`), and the window fields are
+dereferenced throughout 0x402b0300 – 0x402b0d60 and 0x402b64bc.
+
+**A second correction.** Three of the brief's "others" — 0x155C0000,
+0x15600000, 0x15680000 — are not display blocks. They are the three banks of
+the main DMA engine the board **already models** (`z63_dma_bases` in
+`hw/arm/nikon-z63.c`, header comment line 22). Their base table is at rodata
+0x4184B610 and their driver is 0x40c18798 – 0x40c18fac with six instance
+objects at `.data` 0xA0280CD60 + n*0x30. This model does not touch them.
+
+### What names the blocks
+
+Rodata 0x41847000 holds the SoC's interrupt-source name table, 0x20 bytes per
+entry, which is what identifies the silicon. Entries 67–88:
+
+```
+41847860 DISP_0        418478E0 HDMI_MASTER   41847980 IDMAC0_0 .. 41847A20 IDMAC2_1
+41847880 DISP_1        41847900 HDMI_CEC      41847A40 RDMA_0 .. 41847A80 RDMA_2
+418478A0 DISP_1B       41847920 MIPI_DSI_TX   41847AA0 YUV_COMP
+418478C0 DISP_DWCH     41847940 RSZ           41847AC0 YUV_DECOMP
+                       41847960 MPO           41847AE0 CODEC_DECOMP
+```
+
+The rest of the table (`PERI_XDMAC_n`, `IPCU_n`, `NOC_MAIN`, `NOC_DDR`,
+`SDRAMC_n`, `EXS_UHS2_1_F_SDH30/40`, `EXS_NETSEC_A/B`, `W521C`) is Socionext
+Milbeaut naming, so EXPEED 7 is a Milbeaut-family part and the display blocks
+are the usual Milbeaut set: two or three DISP units, a DesignWare HDMI
+("DWCH"), and a MIPI DSI transmitter. Three instance ids 455/456/457 against
+three names DISP_0 / DISP_1 / DISP_1B is consistent with the third descriptor
+having no windows of its own (DISP_1B being the second half of DISP_1), but
+that pairing is an inference, not something the image states.
+
+No display block has a register-name table and no function in the band
+references a string, so no register names below come from the image. The
+only name table in this area (0x41852604, `[+] RDMA REG Dump`) belongs to the
+Chips&Media WAVE video codec, not to the display.
+
+---
+
+## 1. What the band contains
+
+Six groups, by driver:
+
+| # | span | driver VAs | what it is | state here |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | 0x1548C000 – 0x15492FFF | 0x402b0300–0x402b6600, 0x402b4688–0x402b6200 | DSI/display control complex, seven 4 KiB pages | **reversed** |
+| 2 | 0x15480000 – 0x15487FFF (+ 0x15C80000 – 0x15C87FFF) | same driver, via `.data` pointers | the two DISP units' own register windows | **reversed structurally** (offsets partly) |
+| 3 | 0x15496000 – 0x15498FFF | 0x402b6d14–0x402bb100 | large packet-oriented transmitter, 399 registers | **mapped, not reversed** |
+| 4 | 0x15440000 – 0x1544033F | 0x40c25340–0x40c25c90 | 48 registers, write-mostly | **not reversed** |
+| 5 | 0x154C0000 | 0x40c2f7d0–0x40c3006c | unknown | **not reversed** |
+| 6 | 0x15648000/0x15649000(+0x800), 0x15660000/0x15661000(+0x800) | 0x40c71b80–0x40c7500c, base table 0x4185B620 | two instances × two halves | **not reversed** |
+
+Plus the already-modelled main DMA at 0x155C0000 / 0x15600000 / 0x15680000
+(see §0).
+
+**Access width is uniformly 32 bits.** Every MMIO access in all 325 functions
+is `LDR Wn` / `STR Wn`; verified by disassembling the widest reader
+(0x402b4ec0 — 0x402b4ed4 onwards is `LDR W9` throughout, with the narrowing
+`STRB`/`STRH` applied to the *destination struct*, not the register). The
+byte-offset addresses the decompiler shows (`MEMORY[0x1548E00A]`,
+`MEMORY[0x1548E202]`, `MEMORY[0x1549701F]`, `MEMORY[0x15497402]`, …) are
+Hex-Rays rendering sub-fields of a single 32-bit load and are **not**
+registers; 0x402b5108/0x402b511c is the clearest example. They are excluded
+from every table below.
+
+### The sub-block index map
+
+0x402b64bc is a dispatcher that reads one register from one of 18 sub-blocks:
+`idx` (0..0x11) selects a base through the byte jump table at rodata
+0x4184B354 (`00 02 04 06 3f 08 0d 12 17 1c 21 26 2b 30 35 37 3a 3d`), then
+`LDR W0, [X8, W2, UXTW]` at 0x402b65f4 reads `base + offset`. The resulting
+map is the band's own statement of its layout:
+
+```
+idx  0..3   window0 + n*0x1000      (0x15480000 / 0x15484000 + n*0x1000)
+idx  4      0x1548C000
+idx  5      0x1548D000
+idx  6      0x1548E000
+idx  7      0x1548F000
+idx  8      0x15491000
+idx  9      0x15492000
+idx 10..13  0x15490000 + n*0x100
+idx 14..17  window2 + n*0x1000      (0x15C80000 / 0x15C84000 + n*0x1000)
+```
+
+Two consequences for a model. First, `idx 4..13` are *shared* between the two
+instances, while `idx 0..3` and `idx 14..17` are per-instance — so the DSI
+host at 0x1548D000 is a single instance serving both DISP units. Second, this
+path reads an **arbitrary** offset in any sub-block (as does 0x402b4f38,
+`return *(unsigned int *)(a1 + 0x1548D000)`), so no offset whitelist can be
+complete for reads. An offset not in the tables below is still a real
+register; it has simply never been named by an immediate.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Block 1a — MIPI DSI host at 0x1548D000
+
+**Identification: a Synopsys DesignWare MIPI DSI Host, v1.3x register map.**
+This is not a guess from the block's neighbourhood; it is eleven consecutive
+exact field-width matches plus four independent behavioural matches.
+
+The field widths written at 0x402b48fc:
+
+| offset | width written | DWC register |
+|---|---|---|
+| 0x03C | [13:0] | VID_PKT_SIZE |
+| 0x040 | [12:0] | VID_NUM_CHUNKS |
+| 0x044 | [12:0] | VID_NULL_SIZE |
+| 0x048 | [11:0] | VID_HSA_TIME |
+| 0x04C | [11:0] | VID_HBP_TIME |
+| 0x050 | [14:0] | VID_HLINE_TIME |
+| 0x054 | [9:0] | VID_VSA_LINES |
+| 0x058 | [9:0] | VID_VBP_LINES |
+| 0x05C | [9:0] | VID_VFP_LINES |
+| 0x060 | [13:0] | VID_VACTIVE_LINES |
+| 0x098/0x09C | [9:0] + [25:16] | PHY_TMR_LPCLK_CFG / PHY_TMR_CFG |
