+### 2.2 PHY_STATUS (0x0B0) — the band's main state machine
+
+This is the one place a model *must* answer correctly, because two call sites
+spin on it with interrupts on and no timeout.
+
+Four mask tables in rodata drive it, all indexed by `PHY_IF_CFG & 3`:
+
+```
+0x4184B3A0 (u16[4])  3C 00  BC 01  BC 07  BC 1F   -> 0x003C 0x01BC 0x07BC 0x1FBC
+0x4184B3E0 (u16[4])  80 0A  00 0A  00 08  00 00   -> 0x0A80 0x0A00 0x0800 0x0000
+0x4184B3E8 (u16[4])  80 1F  00 1E  00 18  00 00   -> 0x1F80 0x1E00 0x1800 0x0000
+0x4184B3F0 (u16[4])  00 15  00 14  00 10  00 00   -> 0x1500 0x1400 0x1000 0x0000
+```
+
+The union of every bit any of them touches is 0x1FBC = bits
+2,3,4,5,7,8,9,10,11,12, which group as five pairs {2,3} {4,5} {7,8} {9,10}
+{11,12}, and the index selects how many pairs are in play (2, 3, 4, 5). That
+is the DWC PHY_STATUS layout exactly: stopstate/ulpsactivenot for the clock
+lane and up to four data lanes, with PHY_IF_CFG[1:0] as the lane count. The
+even bit of each pair is the stopstate bit, the odd bit the ulpsactivenot bit.
+
+The four sites, with the exact condition each waits for:
+
+**a) Enable the HS clock — 0x402b4c8c**
+```
+if (~(tbl_3E8[n] | PHY_STATUS) & 0x1FBC) return 2;   // PHY_IF_CFG 0x402b4cb0
+                                                     // PHY_STATUS 0x402b4cc0
+                                                     // test       0x402b4ccc
+LPCLK_CTRL |= 1;                                     // 0x402b4cd4 / 0x402b4cdc
+```
+`~(tbl_3E8[n] | status) & 0x1FBC == 0` means status must contain every bit of
+`0x1FBC & ~tbl_3E8[n]`, which equals `tbl_3A0[n]`: 0x003C, 0x01BC, 0x07BC,
+0x1FBC for n = 0..3. So **before the HS clock may be enabled, the clock lane
+and all data lanes in use must read both stopstate and ulpsactivenot = 1.**
+If not, the call returns 2 (busy) — it does not loop.
+
+**b) Disable the HS clock — 0x402b4cf8**
+```
+if (~(tbl_3E0[n] | PHY_STATUS) & 0xA90) return 2;    // PHY_IF_CFG 0x402b4d1c
+                                                     // PHY_STATUS 0x402b4d2c
+                                                     // test       0x402b4d38
+LPCLK_CTRL &= ~1;                                    // 0x402b4d40 / 0x402b4d48
+```
+Required bits = `0xA90 & ~tbl_3E0[n]` = 0x0010, 0x0090, 0x0290, 0x0A90 —
+bits 4, 7, 9, 11, i.e. **the data lanes' stopstate bits only** (not the clock
+lane's, and not any ulpsactivenot). Returns 2 if unmet; no loop.
+
+**c) Enter ULPS — 0x402b4d64 (spins)**
+```
+if ((PHY_ULPS_CTRL | PHY_TX_TRIGGERS) != 0) return 2; // 0x0A8 at 0x402b4d74
+                                                      // 0x0AC at 0x402b4d7c
+                                                      // test   0x402b4d84
+if (~(tbl_3E8[n] | PHY_STATUS) & 0x1FBC) return 2;    // 0x0B0 at 0x402b4db0
+                                                      // test   0x402b4dbc
+v1 = tbl_3A0[n];
+PHY_ULPS_CTRL = 5;                                    // 0x402b4dec
+while (PHY_STATUS & v1) ;                             // 0x402b4df0 / 0x402b4df4
+                                                      // branch 0x402b4df8
+```
+**After `PHY_ULPS_CTRL = 5` (request ULPS on the clock lane and the data
+lanes), the model must clear every bit of `tbl_3A0[n]` in PHY_STATUS** —
+0x003C for one lane, up to 0x1FBC for four. Until it does, the CPU spins.
+
+**d) Exit ULPS — 0x402b4e0c (spins)**
+```
+(void)PHY_ULPS_CTRL;                                 // discarded read, 0x402b4e30
+if (tbl_3A0[n] & PHY_STATUS) return 2;               // 0x0B0 at 0x402b4e38
+                                                     // test   0x402b4e48
+v2 = tbl_3F0[n];
+PHY_ULPS_CTRL = 15;                                  // 0x402b4e94
+while (~(v2 | PHY_STATUS) & 0x1528) ;                // 0x402b4e98 / 0x402b4ea0
+                                                     // branch 0x402b4ea4
+sub_40D33F20(1);
+PHY_ULPS_CTRL = 0;                                   // 0x402b4eb4
+```
+The gate requires `tbl_3A0[n] & status == 0` — the inverse of (c), so this is
+only callable while actually in ULPS. Required bits to leave the loop =
+`0x1528 & ~tbl_3F0[n]` = **0x0028, 0x0128, 0x0528, 0x1528 for n = 0..3** —
+bits 3, 5, 8, 10, 12, the *ulpsactivenot* bits of the clock lane and the data
+lanes in use. The stopstate bits are not waited on here.
+
+A PHY_STATUS that satisfies all four sites is:
+
+```
+status = (PHY_RSTZ & 1) && !in_ulps ? tbl_3A0[PHY_IF_CFG & 3] : 0
+in_ulps = PHY_ULPS_CTRL == 5
+```
+because `0xA90 & ~tbl_3E0[n]` and `0x1528 & ~tbl_3F0[n]` are both subsets of
+`tbl_3A0[n]`. Bits 0 (phylock), 1 (direction) and 6 are in none of the four
+masks, so **nothing in this image observes them** and no value for them is
+asserted. Only the values 0, 5 and 15 are ever written to PHY_ULPS_CTRL, so
+treating anything else as "not in ULPS" is an extrapolation, flagged as such
+in the model.
+
+### 2.3 VID_SHADOW_CTRL (0x100) and the `*_ACT` readbacks
+
+0x402b48fc takes a flag `a3`. With `a3 & 1` clear it writes the eleven VID_*
+registers and sets bits 0/8/16 of 0x100 from a struct. With `a3 & 1` set it
+takes a different path:
+
+```
+if (MEMORY[0x1548D100] & 0x100) { result = 4; goto write_back; }  // read  0x402b493c
+                                                                  // TBNZ  0x402b4944
+                                                                  // ret 4 0x402b4a3c
+v14 = MEMORY[0x1548D100] | 1;                                     // enable
+...
+v14 |= 0x100;                                                     // request
+MEMORY[0x1548D100] = v14;                                         // 0x402b4a6c
+```
+
+So **bit 8 is a one-shot request that the hardware clears**: if the firmware
+ever reads it still set, it gives up with error 4. A model must clear bit 8
+on the write, otherwise every commit after the first fails.
+
+The ten read-only registers the firmware reads back are 0x140, 0x144, 0x148,
+0x14C, 0x150, 0x154, 0x158, 0x15C, 0x160 (as halfwords into the status
+struct) and 0x190 (full word) — all from 0x402b4ec0. They sit exactly
+`live + 0x100` from the nine registers 0x040–0x060 and from 0x090, and their
+field widths match. The model latches live → ACT when bit 8 is written.
+
+**This pairing is an inference.** Nothing in the image ever compares an ACT
+value against what it wrote, so the firmware does not prove the mapping.
+Returning 0 for all ten would be equally consistent with the image; the
+mirror is implemented because it is more useful and cannot be observed to be
+wrong by this firmware.
+
+### 2.4 What the DSI block does *not* tell us
+
+No panel geometry or pixel format is stated here as fact. The eleven VID_*
+registers are written entirely from a caller-supplied struct at 0x402b48fc —
+the image contains no initialised instance of that struct in the band's
+rodata, so **no resolution, no timing and no colour coding can be recovered
+from this block.** 0x1548D010 (DPI_COLOR_CODING, [3:0]) is likewise written
+only from a struct field. Anyone wanting the real panel timing has to find
+the caller that fills that struct; it was not traced.
+
+---
