+- 0x15497600 has both set (`|= bit`, 0x402b7a74 ff.) and clear
+  (`&= ~bit`, 0x402b7cd4 ff.) accessors per bit, for bits 17, 18/19, 20, 21,
+  24–31, with 0x15497610 bits [3:0] as its extension. 0x15497604 has only
+  set accessors, for bits 0–7 (0x402b7f34 ff.), with 0x15497610 bits [11:8] as *its*
+  extension, and 0x402ba458 reads 0x15497010, 0x15497604 and 0x15497610 and
+  scatters single bits into a struct. That is the shape of an
+  enable/status pair — **but whether the set-only registers are
+  write-1-to-clear status or plain enables could not be decided**: 0x15498004
+  has the same set-only API (`|= 1/2/4` at 0x402badb0/0x402bae38/0x402bae78)
+  *and* getters that read those exact bits back (0x402badd0/0x402bae58/
+  0x402bae98), which only makes sense if it is a plain RW enable, while a
+  W1C status would make the paired getter useless. Both readings fit.
+- The 0x20-stride groups at 0x15497620/640/660/680/6A0, 0x15497720/740/760/
+  780/7A0/7C0 and 0x15497820/840/860/880/8A0/8E0/920 are descriptor arrays;
+  0x402b8cbc and 0x402b8d1c pack three bytes plus 4-bit fields into them.
+  With the 256-byte window at 0x15497C00 (filled at 0x402b7284, read back at
+  0x402b706c) and the 28-byte window at 0x15497844 (filled at 0x402b8d94),
+  that is the shape of packet slots and a payload buffer.
+- 0x15496000 holds a byte-swapped 32-bit write at +0x08C
+  (`_byteswap_ulong`, 0x402b74b4) and a ten-entry block of 40 bytes written
+  from a struct at +0x2B0..+0x2D4 (0x402b9e44), plus a generic
+  `*(_DWORD *)(a2 + 0x15496000) = a3` accessor at 0x402babbc — so this page
+  too takes a runtime offset and no whitelist is complete for it.
+
+### Identity: probable, not proven
+
+The interrupt table's neighbours (`DISP_DWCH`, `HDMI_MASTER`, `HDMI_CEC`),
+the packet-slot/payload structure, the read-only packed id word and the
+56-bit value assembled from 0x154974A0/A4 at 0x402bab5c all point at an HDMI
+transmitter. **It is not proven.** No string, symbol or table in the image
+names this block, and I did not reverse enough of its semantics to claim it.
+Nothing here should be used as if the identification were settled.
+
+### Why the model refuses it rather than storing writes
+
+A plain register file would not hang anything (there is no poll loop), but
+twelve of these registers are read-only and the firmware's expectation for
+every one of them is unestablished — a read returning 0 would be an invented device
+response, which is exactly what this project does not do. So the three pages
+stop with the offset and the PC. If the integrator decides a scratch register
+file is the more useful trade for a particular bring-up run, the change is
+one function body in the header and it should be labelled when made.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Not reversed at all
+
+| span | driver | what is known |
+|---|---|---|
+| 0x15440000 – 0x1544033F | 0x40c25340 – 0x40c25c90 | 48 registers at 0x000, 0x004, 0x020, 0x02C (read-only), 0x040–0x0A0, 0x124–0x134, 0x300–0x33C. Write-mostly; 0x02C read three times, 0x060 read once. Two 0x300-apart halves suggests two channels. |
+| 0x154C0000 | 0x40c2f7d0 – 0x40c3006c | 13 sites on the base alone; offsets all runtime. Nothing established. |
+| 0x15648000/0x15649000/0x15649800 and 0x15660000/0x15661000/0x15661800 | 0x40c71b80 – 0x40c7500c | Base table at rodata 0x4185B620 (0x15648000, 0x15648000, 0x15660000, 0x15660000, 0x15660000) with +0x70 variants at 0x4185B648. Two instances, each with a +0x000 and a +0x800 half; offsets 0x010, 0x020, 0x028, 0x050, 0x064 on the 0x…8000 bases and 0x000, 0x004, 0x008, 0x00C, 0x020 on the 0x…9000/0x…1000 bases. |
+| 0x15400000, 0x15420000, 0x15540000 | 0x40c247fc, 0x40c25c90, 0x4026b484 | one site each; not enough to call them blocks. 0x15540000 is the Z 8's GPU base and the single site is a bare base with no offset. |
+| 0x15C80000 / 0x15C84000 | via `.data` pointers | window 2 of each display unit; four 0x1000 sub-blocks; no offset established. Outside the assigned band. |
+
+None of these are mapped by the model. They will hit the board's
+`z63_unmapped` trap, which already reports the address and the PC.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Summary of what a model has to answer
+
+Exactly three behaviours in this band are load-bearing:
+
+1. **PHY_STATUS (0x1548D0B0)** must return
+   `(PHY_RSTZ & 1) && PHY_ULPS_CTRL != 5 ? {0x003C,0x01BC,0x07BC,0x1FBC}[PHY_IF_CFG & 3] : 0`.
+   Two call sites spin on it forever otherwise (0x402b4df8, 0x402b4ea4).
+2. **VID_SHADOW_CTRL (0x1548D100) bit 8** must self-clear on write, or every
+   shadow commit after the first returns error 4 (0x402b4944).
+3. **0x1548E004 bit 1** must read 1 after bit 0 is written, or the predicate
+   at 0x402b503c is false forever. Whether anything loops on it is unknown.
+
+Everything else in the modelled region is a plain register that reads back
+what was written.
+
+All three were checked by replay: the four PHY_STATUS call sites and the
+shadow commit were transcribed from the disassembly, run against the model's
+logic with the rodata tables above read verbatim, for all four lane counts.
+Both spin loops exit on their first status read, both HS-clock gates pass,
+the HS clock can be re-enabled after an ULPS round trip, and three
+back-to-back shadow commits all succeed (which is what bit 8's self-clear
+buys). The replay harness is not part of the tree.
