+# The HDMI transmitter at 0x15496000 – 0x15498FFF
+
+(In the SoC's own physical-region table this address lies inside the
+aperture it calls `ISP_APB`, 0x15000000 – 0x15800000 — see §1.6, which
+explains why that is an aperture name and not a block name.)
+
+Source: `analysis/Z6III_0200/extracted/components/eg2010_0200i1.bi`,
+sha256 `736ceaacdd63dc233e51d68beb85378242788e2467773e762b038b2f41c47fda`.
+All firmware addresses are VAs in that image's text segment
+(0x40168000 – 0x4184640C); file offset = VA − 0x40160000. `.data` VAs
+(0xA028xxxxx) map to file offset 0x5099000 + (VA − 0xA02808000).
+
+Every statement is followed by the address that establishes it. Where
+something is an inference it says so. Where it could not be determined it
+says that instead of guessing.
+
+This continues `docs/display-registers.md` §5, which enumerated the block's
+399 registers and deliberately refused to model it because it could not name
+it. The name is established below. The offset census in that document is
+correct and is reproduced here with reachability added; its two structural
+conclusions that turned out to be wrong are corrected in §8.
+
+---
+
+## 1. What the block is
+
+**It is the HDMI transmitter — the source side: its DDC/E-DDC master, its
+packet (InfoFrame) generator, its video/audio front end and a PHY page.**
+
+The identification is protocol-level, not shape-level. Four independent
+proofs:
+
+### 1.1 The firmware drives the HDMI SCDC over this block's I2C master
+
+`0x15497A80` is written by `0x402B7034` as
+
+```
+0x15497A80 = (cmd << 24) | ((len - 1) << 16) | addr16
+```
+
+from an 8-byte descriptor `{u32 cmd; u16 addr16; u16 len}`. `addr16` is
+`slave | (offset << 8)`. The firmware's static request table at `.data`
+**0xA0280FDF8**, 19 entries of `{u32 op; u8 slave; u8 offset; u16 len; void *buf}`
+on a 0x10 stride, is consumed by `0x402C36CC` and reads:
+
+| .data | op | slave | offset | len | HDMI 2.0/2.1 SCDC register |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 0xA0280FDF8 | 0 rd | **0xA8** | 0x01 | 1 | Sink Version |
+| 0xA0280FE08 | 1 wr | 0xA8 | 0x02 | 1 | Source Version |
+| 0xA0280FE18 | 1 wr | 0xA8 | 0x20 | 1 | TMDS_Config |
+| 0xA0280FE28 | 1 wr | 0xA8 | 0x20 | 1 | TMDS_Config |
+| 0xA0280FE38 | 0 rd | 0xA8 | 0x21 | 1 | Scrambler_Status |
+| 0xA0280FE48 | 1 wr | 0xA8 | 0x30 | 1 | Config_0 |
+| 0xA0280FE58 | 0 rd | 0xA8 | 0x40 | 1 | Status_Flags_0 |
+| 0xA0280FE68 | 1 wr | 0xA8 | 0x31 | 1 | Config_1 |
+| 0xA0280FE78 | 0 rd | 0xA8 | 0x10 | 1 | Update_0 |
+| 0xA0280FE88 | 0 rd | 0xA8 | 0x41 | 2 | Status_Flags_1/2 |
+| 0xA0280FE98 | 0 rd | 0xA8 | 0x35 | 1 | — |
+| 0xA0280FEA8/B8/C8/E8/F08/F18 | 1 wr | 0xA8 | 0x10 | 1 | Update_0 (flag clears) |
+| 0xA0280FED8 | 0 rd | 0xA8 | 0x59 | 2 | — |
+| 0xA0280FEF8 | 0 rd | 0xA8 | 0x50 | **9** | CED block 0x50–0x58 |
+
+`0xA8` is the HDMI DDC slave address of the **SCDC**, and 0x01/0x02/0x10/
+0x20/0x21/0x30/0x31/0x40/0x41/0x50..0x58 is the SCDC register map verbatim,
+including the 9-byte character-error-detection block at 0x50. Nothing else
+uses that address with those offsets. The driver *writes* Source Version
+(0x02) and TMDS_Config (0x20) and *reads* Sink Version (0x01), so this is
+the **source**, not a sink.
+
+The wrappers confirm the semantics: `0x402C3CC8` reads SCDC 0x41 as two
+bytes and splits them into four 4-bit nibbles (one per lane); `0x402C3D48`,
+`0x402C3D7C`, `0x402C3DB0`, `0x402C3E04`, `0x402C3E5C`, `0x402C3EB0` each
+write a single bit (8, 0x20, 0x10, 0x40, 2, 1) to SCDC Update_0.
+
+### 1.2 The payload window is two EDID blocks, read with E-DDC segment addressing
+
+`0x402C1940` reads EDID block *n*:
+
+```
+0x402c19a4  LSL W8, W20, #7     ; offset  = (n << 7) & 0xff   -> addr16[15:8]
+0x402c19a8  LSR W9, W20, #1     ; segment = n >> 1            -> addr16[7:0]
+0x402c19b8  STRH #0x80          ; length  = 128
+0x402c39d4  MOV W8, #2          ; op = 2  -> command byte 0x48
+```
+
+Segment = block/2, offset = 0x00 or 0x80, 128 bytes: that is E-DDC
+(VESA Enhanced DDC) segmented EDID addressing, and command 0x48 is the
+dedicated segmented-read opcode — which is why op 2 exists separately from
+the plain "slave + offset" read op 0. The read-back window at `0x15497C00`
+is 256 bytes (`0x402B706C`), i.e. two EDID blocks, and its two size
+thresholds are 5 and 0x81 — it copies 4 bytes, then up to 128, then up to
+256.
+
+### 1.3 The 0x20-stride slots are HDMI packet slots
+
+Every slot writer has the same first word (`0x402B81D0`, `0x402B82F4`,
+`0x402B841C`, `0x402B84DC`, `0x402B8578`, `0x402B8660`, `0x402B8740`,
+`0x402B8820`, `0x402B8900`, `0x402B89E0`, `0x402B8AEC`, `0x402B8CBC`,
+`0x402B8D1C`, `0x402B8D94`):
+
+```
+slot+0x00 = (hdr[0] << 16) | (hdr[1] << 8) | hdr[2]     (mask 0xFF000000 kept)
+```
+
+and these generated setters take their struct fields most-significant-first
+(see `0x402B7548`, which writes `a2[0]`→bit 6 down to `a2[6]`→bit 0). So the
+word is **HB0 at [7:0], HB1 at [15:8], HB2 at [23:16]** and the struct is
+`{length, version, type, …}` — the three HDMI packet header bytes, with HB2
+(= length) used by the driver to size the body copy.
+
+The body is 28 bytes at `slot+0x04 .. slot+0x1F`, written little-endian
+byte-by-byte by the packer `0x402B730C`: its main loop at `0x402B73FC`–
+`0x402B743C` stores byte *k* of each word with `STRB W13,[X0+word*4+k]` for
+k = 0..3, i.e. byte *i* of the stream lands at bits 8*i*+7:8*i*, and its
+`a4 = 1/2/3` entry cases at `0x402B7354`, `0x402B7348` and `0x402B73A8`
+agree. **3 header bytes + 28 body bytes is exactly the HDMI packet:
+four subpackets of seven bytes.** `0x402B8D94` writes a full one — header to
+`0x15497840`, then 28 bytes to `0x15497844..0x1549785F`.
+
+`0x402B8660` is the clincher for the byte lane: it writes PB1/PB2/PB3 to
+bits [15:8]/[23:16]/[31:24] of `slot+0x04`, copies `length-3` further bytes
+from `slot+0x08`, and **never writes bits [7:0] of `slot+0x04`** — PB0, the
+InfoFrame checksum, which the hardware computes.
+
+### 1.4 The service objects are named for HDMI
+
+`0x402C3EE4` creates the block's RTOS objects with the names
+`DisSvH00`…`DisSvH10` (rodata 0x41876898…0x418768F2). The display
+controller's own objects are `DisSvD00`/`DisSvD01` (0x41876886), and the
+image also has `DisSvF00`, `DisSvO00`, `DisSvT00..13`, `DisSvV00`. "DisSv" +
+one letter per display sub-service; this block owns the "H" set.
+
+### 1.5 Confidence
+
+**Conclusive for "HDMI transmitter".** §1.1 alone cannot be anything else.
+
+### 1.6 What it is *not*, and what the address space says
+
+- **Not the Synopsys DesignWare HDMI TX register map.** The SoC interrupt
+  table (rodata 0x41847000, entry 70) has `DISP_DWCH`, and the DSI host next
+  door *is* a DesignWare part, so DWC HDMI was the obvious guess. It is
+  wrong for this map: DWC HDMI has four separate byte registers
+  DESIGN_ID/REVISION_ID/PRODUCT_ID0/PRODUCT_ID1 at 0x0000–0x0003 and its
+  frame composer at 0x1000+, while this block has **one packed id word** at
+  +0x000 (`0x402B6D14` splits it into a byte [23:16] and a halfword [15:0])
+  and fits entirely in three pages of 32-bit registers. Neither a 1-byte nor
+  a 4-byte DWC stride lands its registers here. If `DISP_DWCH` names a
+  DesignWare HDMI, it is not this address.
+- **It is inside `ISP_APB`.** The SoC's own physical-region table at `.data`
+  0xA02BD2380 (29 entries, 0x28 stride, name pointers into the blob at
+  0xA02BD29A8) calls 0x15000000–0x15800000 `ISP_APB` — I re-decoded it
+  independently and entry 10 is exactly that. That is an **aperture name,
+  not a block name**: the same 8 MB window also holds the two DISP units at
+  0x15480000/0x15484000, the DesignWare MIPI DSI host at 0x1548D000 and the
+  three main DMA banks at 0x155C0000/0x15600000/0x15680000, none of which is
+  an ISP. So the region table does not name this block and does not bear on
+  §1.1–§1.4. The doc records it because it is a fact: **this transmitter is
+  reached over the ISP APB bus.**
+- Which SoC interrupt line it raises (`DISP_DWCH` = SPI 70, `HDMI_MASTER` =
+  SPI 71, `HDMI_CEC` = SPI 72) **is not established** — see §5.3.
+- No HDMI-naming string exists anywhere in the image: the only hits for
+  `HDMI`/`DWC`/`SCDC`/`EDID`/`TMDS`/`HDCP` outside the interrupt table are
+  UI text and `DSID_Setting_HdmiExternalRecordingControl` (0x4426A554).
+
+---
+
+## 2. Shape, and which registers this firmware can actually reach
+
+One driver, **0x402B6D14 – 0x402BB100**, 204 functions, almost all generated
+one-register accessors. **399 distinct 32-bit registers** (37 in 0x15496000,
+343 in 0x15497000, 19 in 0x15498000) are named by an immediate; the
+walking-pointer loops add 127 more words (the slot bodies and the DDC
+payload window) for **526 words** in all.
+
+Access is uniformly 32-bit: every MMIO instruction in the driver is
+`LDR Wn` / `STR Wn`. The byte addresses Hex-Rays prints — `MEMORY[0x1549701F]`,
+`MEMORY[0x15497095]`, `MEMORY[0x15497812]`, `MEMORY[0x15497A85]`,
+`MEMORY[0x15497402]` — are sub-fields of one word; `0x402B7458` is the clear
+case (`LDR W8,[X8]; UBFX W0,W8,#8,#8`). They are not registers.
+
+**114 of the 204 accessors have no caller anywhere in the image** (no code
+xref, and no data xref either — nothing in text, rodata or .data holds a
+pointer into the driver, checked by byte search). So **279 of the 399
+registers are unreachable in this firmware build** and only **120 are
+reachable**. The reachable set is listed in §6 and is what the model is
+tuned for; the rest are whitelisted too (reads of them would otherwise be
+invented), but the doc flags them so a stop on one is recognised as either a
+new firmware path or a wrong reachability call.
+
+### Page roles
+
+| page | what it holds |
+|---|---|
+| 0x15496000 | 37 registers, write-mostly. Small bitfield groups plus a byte-swapped 32-bit word at +0x08C and two 10/9-word blocks at +0x2B0 and +0x2D8. **Not identified** beyond "configuration". |
+| 0x15497000 | the transmitter proper: id, control, interrupt status/enable, video and audio timing, 18 packet slots, the DDC master, the 256-byte DDC payload window. |
+| 0x15498000 | 19 registers: 16 single bits + a 5×4-bit lane group + 21 six-bit coefficients at +0x028..+0x058 and three read-only status words. **Shape of a PHY page** (lane count, drive/equaliser coefficients, lock status) — an inference, and entirely unreachable in this build. |
+
+---
+
+## 3. The DDC / E-DDC master — fully reversed
+
+This is the one register file with a real state machine and the one the
+model implements properly.
+
+### 3.1 Registers
+
+| off (abs) | dir | what the driver does | firmware address |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `0x15497A80` | W, R[31:24] | `(cmd<<24) | ((len-1)<<16) | addr16`. `addr16` = `slave | offset<<8` for cmd 0x40/0x44; = `segment | offset<<8` for cmd 0x48. `len` 1..256. The store is preceded by `LDR WZR,[X8]` — a discarded read, i.e. a pure write. | write `0x402B7034`; read `0x402B7010` |
+| `0x15497A84` | R | `[7:0]` transfer state — the client treats **1 as busy**; `[15:8]` result code, 0 = OK, 0x20 and 0x40 are the two failures the client handles. | `0x402B7474` (`AND W0,W8,#0xFF`), `0x402B7458` (`UBFX W0,W8,#8,#8`) |
+| `0x15497A88` | RMW[7:0] | the literal **0xF9** is written to bits [7:0], and only as part of recovery from result 0x20. Meaning **not determined**; the bit pattern 0b11111001 is consistent with clearing six error flags but nothing establishes that. | `0x402B7490` |
+| `0x15497A9C` | RMW[2],[1:0] | `(cfg0&1)<<2 | (cfg1&3)`. The config is the constant pair at rodata **0x4184D320** = `{0, 1}`, so the firmware always writes **bit 2 = 0, bits [1:0] = 1**; for op 2 (segmented EDID read) the first word is zeroed first, which changes nothing. Meaning not determined. | `0x402B6FE4`, caller `0x402C37B0`, constant load `0x402C3714` |
+| `0x154970D0` | RMW[31:28],[27:24] | two 4-bit fields, written immediately before every EDID block read from two bytes in `.data` (0xA02F9151C, 0xA02F91520 — both in BSS, no initialiser in the image, so the values are **not recoverable**). Shape of a DDC clock divider; an inference. | `0x402B6FB8`, caller `0x402C1998` |
+| `0x15497C00` – `0x15497CFC` | RW | 256-byte payload window, 64 words. Filled for a write by the walking-pointer loop at `0x402B72D4` (`v8 = 0x15497C00; *(_DWORD*)v8 = …; v8 += 4`), read back for a read at `0x402B706C`. | `0x402B7284`, `0x402B706C` |
+
+### 3.2 The command byte
+
+| cmd | op | what | established by |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 0x40 | 0 | read `len` bytes from `slave`, register `offset` | `0x402C3744` |
+| 0x44 | 1 | write `len` bytes (payload already in the window) | `0x402C3770`, fill at `0x402C3768` |
+| 0x48 | 2 | **E-DDC segmented read**: `[7:0]` is the segment pointer, `[15:8]` the offset; the slave is implicit | `0x402C377C`, caller `0x402C19BC` |
+| 0x49 | 3 | recovery issued after result **0x40** | `0x402C378C`, `0x402C3800` |
+| 0x4A | 4 | recovery issued after result **0x20**, preceded by the 0xF9 write to 0x15497A88 | `0x402C3794`, `0x402C3818` |
+
+Bit 6 of the command byte is set in all five, i.e. it is the go/enable bit;
+bit 2 distinguishes write from read; bit 3 with bits 1:0 selects the
+segmented read and the two recoveries. Reading that as a bit layout is an
+inference — only the five literal values are measured.
+
+### 3.3 The state machine the model must answer
+
+Transcribed from `0x402C3870` (the start-and-wait routine) and
+`0x402C36CC`/`0x402C392C`:
+
+```
+0x402c389c  w = DDC_STAT[7:0]                   ; 0x15497A84 & 0xff
+0x402c38a0  if (w == 1) {                       ; BUSY
+0x402c38ac      for (i = 0; i <= 0xC8; i++) {   ; at most 201 iterations
+0x402c38c0          delay(1 ms)
+0x402c38c8          if (DDC_STAT[7:0] != 1) break
+0x402c3914      }  if still busy: return false
+            }
+0x402c38ec  clr_flg(DisSvD01 flag, ~1)
+0x402c38f8  DDC_CMD = (cmd<<24)|((len-1)<<16)|addr16   ; start
+0x402c390c  twai_flg(flag, 1, TWF_ANDW, &ptn, 1000)    ; W3 = 0x3E8 = 1000 ms
+            return (twai_flg == E_OK)
+```
+
+then
+
+```
+0x402c37c4  r = DDC_STAT[15:8]
+            r == 0x40 -> 0x402C392C(1, desc): re-issue with cmd 0x49
+            r == 0x20 -> 0x402C392C(0, desc): write 0xF9 to 0x15497A88,
+                                               re-issue with cmd 0x4A
+            r == 0    -> success; if op is 0 or 2, read the payload window
+            else      -> fail
+```
+
+So the three things a model has to get right are:
+
+1. **The command byte must read back 0 once the transfer is over.**
+   `0x402B7010` is `LDR W8,[0x15497A80]; LSR W8,W8,#0x18; CMP W8,#0; CSET` —
+   "idle iff bits [31:24] are zero". (That getter has no caller in this
+   build, so the requirement is established by the register's documented
+   meaning rather than by a live call; the model honours it anyway because
+   it costs nothing.)
+2. **`0x15497A84[7:0]` must not be 1 when a transfer is started**, or the
+   client burns 201 ms and returns failure without ever writing the command.
+3. **Something must set the completion event flag within 1000 ms**, or every
+   transfer fails. §5.3 says what that something is and why the model does
+   not provide it.
+
+### 3.4 Replay against the model
+
+The three firmware routines above — `0x402C36CC`, `0x402C3870`,
+`0x402C392C` — plus the payload stage/fetch (`0x402B7284`, `0x402B706C`) and
+the four register accessors (`0x402B7010`, `0x402B7034`, `0x402B7458`,
+`0x402B7474`) were transcribed instruction by instruction and run against
+the model's actual read/write handlers, with the real descriptors from
+`.data` (SCDC write of Source Version, SCDC read of Sink Version, EDID block
+0 via op 2) and with the firmware's own branch structure. Results:
+
+- *idle pre-check*: after reset `0x15497A84` reads 0, so `0x402C38A0` sees
+  `!= 1` and the 201-iteration loop runs **0 times**; it also runs 0 times on
+  every subsequent transfer, because the model finishes inside the store and
+  leaves state `[7:0] = 0`. ✔
+- *go bit*: `0x15497A80[31:24]` reads **0** after the transfer, so
+  `0x402B7010` reports idle. ✔
+- *as shipped (no interrupt, §5.3)*: `0x402C390C`'s `twai_flg` times out
+  after 1000 ms, `0x402C37BC` branches to `0x402C37F8`, and `0x402C36CC`
+  returns 0 — **without** reading the result code or the payload. So the
+  result code below only matters once the interrupt is wired. ✔
+- *with the interrupt hypothetically wired*: result 0x40 sends the client to
+  `0x402C3800`, which issues command 0x49, gets 0x40 again, restores the
+  original command, gets 0x40 again, and `0x402C392C` returns false, so
+  `0x402C36CC` returns 0. "No device", in finite time, with the caller's
+  buffer untouched. ✔
+- *result 0 instead*: `0x402C392C` would return true and `0x402C36CC` would
+  copy 256 bytes out of the payload window and call them an EDID. That is
+  why the model does not answer 0.
+- *write path*: the staged payload is still in the window after a failed
+  write, as it must be — nothing consumed it. ✔
+- *0x402D0434*: writing 1s into `0x15497010[15:8]` through `0x402B8174` and
+  then reading them back makes the 501-iteration loop run **once**. ✔
+- *refusals*: a read of the hole at 0x15497818, a read of 0x15496000 (not in
+  the census) and a write to the read-only id at 0x15497000 each stop with
+  the address and the PC rather than returning or storing anything. ✔
+
+The replay found two real errors in an earlier draft of the model: the
+command word was being stored verbatim, leaving `0x15497A80[31:24]`
+non-zero, which contradicts `0x402B7010`; and the payload window still held
+the *previous* transfer's bytes after a failed read, so a failed EDID read
+would have handed the firmware the body of the last SCDC write. Both are
+fixed. The harness is not part of the tree.
+
