+## 4. Interrupt status and enable — reversed
+
+### 4.1 The two pairs
+
+| register | role | established by |
+|---|---|---|
+| `0x15497600` + `0x15497610[3:0]` | **enable / mask**, read-modify-write | every bit has a matching set (`|=`) and clear (`&= ~`) accessor: bits 17,18,19,20,21,24–31 at `0x402B7A74`…`0x402B7EB4`, and `0x15497610` bits 3,2,1,0 at `0x402B7B54`/`0x402B7B74`/`0x402B7B94`/`0x402B7BB4` vs `0x402B7DB4`/`0x402B7DD4`/`0x402B7DF4`/`0x402B7E14` |
+| `0x15497604[7:0]` + `0x15497610[11:8]` | **status, write-1 does not stick** | set-only accessors `0x402B7F34` (`|=0x80`), `0x402B7F74` (0x40), `0x402B7F94` (0x20), `0x402B7FD4` (0x10), `0x402B7FF4` (8), `0x402B8034` (4), `0x402B8054` (2), `0x402B80F4` (1); `0x402B8074`/`0x402B8094`/`0x402B80B4`/`0x402B80D4` for 0x800/0x400/0x200/0x100; read back by `0x402BA458` |
+| `0x15497010[15:8]` | **status, write-1 clears — proven** | see §4.2 |
+| `0x15497090` / `0x15497094` | **status, read-and-write-back to clear** | `0x402BA2D8` reads both, scatters ~26 bits into a 104-byte struct, then writes the values it read straight back |
+| `0x15497098` / `0x1549709C` | **enable / mask for 0x15497090/94** | `0x402B6D78` writes bit collections at exactly the bit positions `0x402BA2D8` reads: 0x15497098 mask 0xFF80F780 (bits 22:16, 11, 6:0), 0x1549709C mask 0xFF00FF1F (bits 23:16, 7:5) |
+| `0x15497614` | plain RW, ten single-bit setters, bits 16–25 | `0x402BA95C`…`0x402BAB0C` |
+
+### 4.2 The one proof, and the 501-ms loop the previous pass missed
+
+`docs/display-registers.md` §5 says "no polling loop exists anywhere on
+these three pages", having checked the 325 band functions. That is true of
+the *driver*; it is **not true of the block**. The loops are one layer up,
+in the HDMI service, spinning on driver getters:
+
+```
+sub_402D0434(a1):
+  v12..v19 = { 1, a1!=3, a1!=3, 1, a1!=3, 1, a1!=3, 1 }
+  sub_402B8174(&v12)        ; 0x15497010 = (0x15497010 & 0xFFFF00FF) | bits 15..8
+  i = 0
+  do {
+      sub_402BA458(v11)     ; v11[0..7] = 0x15497010 bits 15,14,13,12,11,10,9,8
+      delay(1 ms)
+      if (i > 0x1F4) break  ; 501 iterations
+      i++
+  } while ((v11[0] | … | v11[7]) != 0)
+```
+
+It writes 1s into `0x15497010[15:8]` and then waits for those same bits to
+read **0**. That settles the question `display-registers.md` left open:
+**these are write-1-to-clear status bits, not enables.** The shift tables are
+rodata 0x4184B460 = `{15,14,13,12}` and 0x4184B470 = `{11,10,9,8}`.
+
+For `0x15497604[7:0]` and `0x15497610[11:8]` the same accessor shape is
+present but no write-then-wait proof is: they are either W1C status or
+self-clearing one-shot requests (two of them, `0x402B7F94` and `0x402B8054`,
+are called right after a packet slot is filled — `0x402CFE00`→`0x402CFE08`
+and `0x402CFE98`→`0x402CFEA0` — which reads like "send this packet"). **Both
+readings produce the same observable behaviour — a 1 written to such a bit
+must read back 0** — so one implementation covers both, and the model uses
+it. Which of the two it physically is was not determined.
+
+### 4.3 The other loop
+
+`0x402C3870`, the DDC busy pre-check, at most 201 iterations of 1 ms (§3.3).
+
+Those two are the only loops in the whole block's call graph: every getter
+in the driver was listed, every caller of every getter was disassembled, and
+every backward branch in those callers was checked against the call site.
+Both loops are bounded, so **nothing in this block can hang the guest.**
+
