+## 5. What the model answers, and what it refuses
+
+### 5.1 Plain register file
+
+The 526 whitelisted words read back what was written. For the ~380
+registers the driver touches with a read-modify-write that is not an
+invented response — it is the only answer consistent with the instruction
+stream. Unwritten words read 0, which is **not** a measured reset value;
+no reset value anywhere in this block was measured.
+
+### 5.2 Modelled behaviour (all traceable)
+
+| what | behaviour | why |
+|---|---|---|
+| `0x15497A80` write with `[31:24] != 0` | run the transfer, then store the word with `[31:24] = 0` | `0x402B7010` |
+| `0x15497A84` | `(state) | (result << 8)`; state is always 0 because transfers complete inside the store | `0x402B7474`, `0x402B7458`, `0x402C38A0`, `0x402C37C8` |
+| DDC result | **0x40** — *declared stand-in.* 0x40 and 0x20 are the only two failure codes the client recognises (`0x402C37C8`, `0x402C37D0`); **which physical condition each one means is not established.** 0x40 is used because there is no HDMI sink in this machine and a sink-less DDC transfer must fail. As shipped the client never gets this far — it gives up at the 1000 ms event timeout first (§5.3) — so the value only matters once the interrupt is wired; it is set correctly now so that wiring the interrupt is a one-line change and not a second investigation. Change `Z63_HDMI_DDC_RESULT_NO_SINK` to 0 to make transfers "succeed". | §3.4 |
+| failed read: payload window | zeroed — *declared choice.* Leaving the previous transfer's bytes there would hand the firmware a fake EDID; zeroing says "nothing came back". | §3.4 |
+| `0x15497094` bit 6 | set on DDC completion | `0x402BA2D8` puts `(0x15497094 >> 6) & 1` at struct+96 and `(>> 5) & 1` at struct+100, and `0x402C34D4` sets the DDC completion event flag when either is 1. **Which of bits 6 and 5 is "done" and which is "error" is not established**; the model sets 6. |
+| `0x15497010[15:8]`, `0x15497604[7:0]`, `0x15497610[11:8]`, `0x15497090`, `0x15497094` | a written 1 does not stick | §4.2 |
+| `0x15497008[4:0]` | read-only status, reads 0; writes to those bits are dropped (every writer preserves them: `0x402B763C` writes with mask 0xFFFF3CFF, `0x402B8D1C` with 0xFFFFBFFF, `0x402BA5C0` does `|= 0x2000`) | bit 3 is the cable-present input the HDMI service debounces at `0x402C13E4`; 0 = no sink. See §5.4. |
+
+### 5.3 Refused: the interrupt
+
+`0x402B6BE8` is the status-dispatch routine: it calls `0x402BA2D8`
+(read-and-clear `0x15497090`/`0x15497094`) and hands the resulting 104-byte
+struct to `MEMORY[0xA028084B0](103, …)`, and that callback chain reaches
+`0x402C34D4`, which sets the DDC completion flag. **`0x402B6BE8` has no code
+xref and no pointer to it exists anywhere in the image** (byte-searched as
+4- and 8-byte LE in text, rodata and .data), so it is installed through a
+table built at run time that I could not locate, and **the SoC interrupt
+line this block raises is not established.** The candidates from rodata
+0x41847000 are SPI 70 `DISP_DWCH`, 71 `HDMI_MASTER`, 72 `HDMI_CEC`; the
+board already uses this table's index as its SPI number (SPI 173 UART_0_RX,
+176 UART_0_TX, `Z63_GIC_SPI` in `hw/arm/nikon-z63.c`), so wiring is a
+one-liner once the number is known.
+
+Consequence, stated plainly: **the model raises no interrupt, so every DDC
+transfer costs the guest the firmware's full 1000 ms `twai_flg` timeout and
+then reports failure.** It cannot hang. The header has the single place to
+add the IRQ if the number is ever established.
+
+### 5.4 Not a problem in practice
+
+The HPD branch that reads `0x15497008` bit 3 is **dead on this product**.
+`0x402C13E4` reads cable presence from `0x15497008` bit 3 only when
+`MEMORY[0xA0280FDF0] != 1`; that word is 1 in `.data` (file offset
+0x50A0DF0) and **nothing in the image writes it** (searched every
+instruction that materialises it). So the live path takes
+`sub_40C15BFC(MEMORY[0xA02F91370])` — another driver, presumably a GPIO —
+and this block's HPD bit is never consulted. Therefore **no register in this
+block is a hardware input that gates the firmware's progress**, which is why
+a plain register file plus the DDC engine is enough.
+
+### 5.5 Read-only registers the model answers with 0, labelled
+
+All of these are read only by accessors with no caller in this build, so
+nothing can observe the answer; 0 is a labelled stand-in, not a measurement.
+
+| register | what the firmware does with it | reader |
+|---|---|---|
+| `0x15497000` | one word split into byte [23:16] and halfword [15:0] into a struct — a packed id/revision. Never compared. | `0x402B6D14` (no caller) |
+| `0x15497400`–`0x15497410` | 14-bit + 13-bit at 0x400, 9-bit + 16-bit at 0x404, 14-bit + 13-bit at 0x408, 11-bit + byte [23:16] at 0x40C, three single bits at 0x410, into a struct. Two 14/13-bit pairs look like live H/V position but **nothing confirms that**. | `0x402B7598` (no caller) |
+| `0x154974A0` / `0x154974A4` | assembled into one 56-bit value (`A0 | (A4 & 0xFFFFFF) << 32`). 56 bits = one HDMI subpacket, but that is a guess; **not determined.** | `0x402BAB5C` (no caller) |
+| `0x15498014` | bits 25, 24, bytes [23:16] and [15:8], nibbles [7:4] and [3:0] into a struct | `0x402BADF0` (no caller) |
+| `0x15498020` | bits 0, 1, 2 | `0x402BAF28` (no caller) |
+| `0x15498024` | bits 11, 12, 13, 15 | `0x402BAF5C` (no caller) |
+
