+| 0x15497BF4–0x15497BFC | `0x402BA184` | |
+| the whole 0x15498000 page | `0x402BAC4C`, `0x402BAD20`, `0x402BAD80`, `0x402BADB0`…`0x402BAF94` | §2 "page roles" |
+
+---
+
+## 8. Corrections to `docs/display-registers.md` §5
+
+1. **"No polling loop exists anywhere on these three pages" is wrong at the
+   block level.** It is right about the driver; both loops are in the HDMI
+   service that calls it — `0x402C3870` (201 ms, DDC busy) and `0x402D0434`
+   (501 ms, waiting for `0x15497010[15:8]` to clear). Both are bounded.
+2. **"Whether the set-only registers are write-1-to-clear status or plain
+   enables could not be decided" is decided for `0x15497010[15:8]`:** W1C,
+   proven by `0x402D0434` writing 1s and then waiting for 0 (§4.2). For
+   `0x15497604` and `0x15497610[11:8]` it is still undecided, but the two
+   readings are observationally identical.
+3. The block is named (§1), and `0x15497A84` is not simply read-only — it is
+   the DDC status register, and `0x15497A80[31:24]` self-clears.
+4. The census of 399 registers is confirmed exactly, and extended with the
+   127 words the walking-pointer loops reach.
+
+Both headers map 0x15496000+0x3000. `nikon-z63-display.h` maps it as a
+refuse-only region (`Z63_DISP_TXQ_BASE`); **that block must be removed when
+this header is wired in** — see the wiring note at the end of
+`hw/arm/nikon-z63-hdmi.h`.
+
+---
+
+## 9. Where this stopped
+
+- The DDC/E-DDC master (§3) and the interrupt block (§4) are reversed and
+  modelled.
+- The packet-slot *structure* (§1.3, §6.4) is reversed; the *meaning* of each
+  slot — which InfoFrame or packet type each one carries — is not, because
+  the firmware supplies every header byte from a caller-side struct and this
+  image contains no initialised instance of one.
+- The video/audio front end (§6.3) is enumerated field by field but not
+  interpreted. No display timing, pixel format, colorimetry or audio format
+  is asserted anywhere in this document.
+- The 0x15496000 page and the 0x15498000 page are enumerated and not
+  identified.
+- The interrupt line number is not established (§5.3), and that is the one
+  missing piece that stops DDC from working end to end in the model.
