@@ -0,0 +1,161 @@
+# What is modelled, what is not, and how that was measured
+
+This answers one question with data rather than guesswork: given a goal of
+modelling as much of the Z 6III RTOS platform as possible, what should be
+modelled next? Two measurements, two tools, and one result that changes the
+order of the work.
+
+## The tools
+
+`z63-tools/mmio_scan.py` decodes the EG image's text linearly for the three
+ways an AArch64 build materialises a device address -- `MOVZ`/`MOVK`
+sequences, `ADRP`, and 64-bit literal-pool loads -- and counts, per 4 KiB
+page, how many distinct code sites build an address in it. It parses this
+board's own `Z63_*BASE` constants so the gap list stays correct as regions are
+added.
+
+    z63-tools/mmio_scan.py <eg2010_0200i1.bi>
+
+`z63-tools/trace_report.py` aggregates a runtime trace. QEMU's own
+tracepoints give every MMIO access with no board change at all:
+
+    qemu-system-aarch64 -machine nikon-z63,rom-page-stub=on -bios <eg> \
+        --trace enable=memory_region_ops_read \
+        --trace enable=memory_region_ops_write -D /tmp/z63-trace.log
+    z63-tools/trace_report.py /tmp/z63-trace.log
+
+Its useful column is how many distinct values a register answered. A register
+read many times that always answers the same value is a guest polling a model
+that cannot change, and is the next thing to give behaviour to.
+
+## Static result
+
+490 pages referenced from the instruction stream, 85 of them covered by a
+declared board region, plus **91 more reachable only through an initialised
+`.data` pointer** — see the correction below for why that second number
+exists.
+
+The largest uncovered blocks, by the number of code sites that materialise an
+address in them:
+
+| sites | block | what it is |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| 546 | 0x17820000 + 0x17828200 | **two SD host controllers**, published SD Host Controller Standard layout, one with the UHS-II extension. Named by the image's own interrupt-source table: `EXS_SDIO_*`, `EXS_UHS2_1_F_SDH30_*`, `EXS_UHS2_1_F_SDH40_*`. Modelled in `nikon-z63-storage.h`; see [storage-registers.md](storage-registers.md) |
+| 191 | 0x15496000 + 0x15497000 | a packet-oriented transmitter, most likely the **HDMI** one the IRQ table names (`HDMI_MASTER`, `HDMI_CEC`) — not proven. 399 registers enumerated in [display-registers.md](display-registers.md), deliberately not modelled |
+| ~~126~~ | ~~0x08000000~~ | **Withdrawn — not a block.** All 131 sites are the one value 0x08000000 used as bit 27 of a 32-bit data word, never as an address; nothing in the image forms an address in the page. The page itself is real: the image's region table names it `OCROM`, 2 MiB of on-chip mask ROM, and the SRAM is the *adjacent* region `WorkSRAM` at 0x08200000. See [band-0x08000000.md](band-0x08000000.md), which also decodes that 29-entry region table — a second naming key for the blocks below |
+| 110 | 0x1a000000..0x1ac00000 | **USB** — `EXS_USB3_TYPE_C_CONTROLLER` in the IRQ table, `EF_UVC` / `MTX_UAC` / `IAP2CTLSVC` in the referencing functions, and 0x1788C110 is the DesignWare USB 3 `GCTL` offset |
+
+
+**The SoC is a Socionext Milbeaut-family part.** The interrupt-source name
+table at rodata 0x41847000 (0x20 per entry) names `DISP_0/1/1B`, `DISP_DWCH`,
+`HDMI_MASTER`, `HDMI_CEC`, `MIPI_DSI_TX`, `RSZ`, `MPO`, `IDMAC0_0..IDMAC2_1`,
+`RDMA_0..2`, `YUV_COMP/DECOMP`, `PERI_XDMAC_n`, `IPCU_n`, `NOC_MAIN`,
+`SDRAMC_n`, `W521C` and the SD host names above. Two independent passes over
+the band found that table and agree on it. It is the single most useful object
+in the image for naming hardware, and it should be the first stop for any
+further block.
+
+**A second, stronger naming key: the SoC's own region table.** The image
+carries 29 named physical regions at `.data` VA 0xA02BD2380 —
+`z63-tools/region_table.py` decodes them and will label this page list with
+them (`--pages`). It covers address space rather than interrupt lines, and it
+self-validates: `TOP`, `UART` and `NoC` land exactly on bases this board had
+chosen independently. Every page above resolves: the SD controllers are in
+`EXS`, 0x15490000/0x15497000 are in `ISP_APB`, 0x1c73e000 is in `DSP`,
+0x0efd0000 is in `PERI`, and 0x10000000 is the `DDR` controller — a real
+region whose registers the text never addresses by immediate, which is why the
+constant there fooled an earlier version of this document.
+
+Naming a block from the firmware also works bottom-up: take the functions that
+reference a page and read the string literals *those* functions reference
+(`z63-tools/block_identify.py`). It fails on low-level register drivers, which
+are leaf functions with nothing to log.
+
+## Two defects in the first version of this measurement
+
+Both were caught by reverse-engineering the blocks properly, which is the only
+reason to trust the numbers above over the ones this document first carried.
+
+**Counts were about 3x too high.** A 64-bit address is built as `MOVZ` +
+`MOVK` x3, and the value is already in the peripheral band after the second
+instruction, so counting every instruction that leaves it in band counted one
+materialisation three times. 1928 sites for the SD band was really 631
+materialisations of 124 addresses; 1341 for the display band was really 462 of
+211. `mmio_scan.py` now keys on where a run starts. Rankings were unaffected.
+
+**"The Z 8 board's addresses do not transfer" was wrong.** This document
+previously argued that, because against this image the Z 8's display base
+0x15480000 shows 21 sites and its LUT base 0x15C80000 shows none. Both are in
+fact live here, reached through initialised descriptors in `.data` — at VA
+0xA02808438, id 0x1C7 maps to (0x15480000, 0x15490800, 0x15C80000) and id
+0x1C8 to (0x15484000, 0x15490900, 0x15C84000). A scan of instruction
+immediates cannot see a base a driver keeps in a struct; the tool's own
+docstring said so and the conclusion ignored it. **The Z 8 bases transfer; only
+its register offsets remain unverified here.** `mmio_scan.py` now also scans
+`.data` for band-valued pointers, which finds 91 pages the text scan misses,
+and it recognises bases declared in an array (the main DMA banks were being
+reported as gaps although the board models them).
+
+**Values that are not addresses ranked near the top.** The scan had no
+alignment filter, so plain integers that happen to fall in the peripheral band
+were counted as hardware. 0x10624DD3 ranked third with 245 sites and is a
+division reciprocal; 0x15555555 and 0x1555554A are two more; 0x1AA00002 and
+0x1AA00006 are byte offsets, not registers. Requiring a 4-byte-aligned value
+removes them and drops the referenced-page count from 490 to 387. It cannot
+remove **0x10000000**, which is 256 MiB and therefore aligned: that page still
+appears with 172 sites across 354 functions and is a size constant, not a
+device. An earlier version of this document called it "Bluetooth / HCI" on the
+strength of `mele.c` and `MeSendHciCommand` appearing in functions that use the
+constant; that identification was spurious and is withdrawn. A page whose only
+referenced address is its own base, which is a round power of two, and which is
+referenced by hundreds of unrelated functions, is a constant.
+
+**0x08000000 was the same trap, and caught the same way.** It is 128 MiB, and
+also bit 27; 131 sites across 102 unrelated functions all build that one value
+and nothing else in the page. Two of those functions are the same `mele.c` /
+`MeSendHciCommand` pair that produced the withdrawn Bluetooth call above, and
+across all 102 functions no string literal is shared by even two of them. The
+test that settles these cases in one query is **register width**: a 64-bit
+address cannot live in a `W` register, and 130 of the 131 sites are a 32-bit
+`MOV`/`ORR`/`AND`/`EOR`/`TST` (the one 64-bit form is an `ORR` setting bit 27
+in a run with bits 25, 26 and 28). Worth running before the string pass,
+because the string pass is what misled both times.
+
+## Runtime result, and why it reorders the work
+
+Booted with `rom-page-stub=on` and no LI payload, the whole run makes **4073**
+MMIO accesses and then stops changing:
+
+| region | accesses |
+| --- | --- |
+| `gicv3_dist` | 1867 |
+| `z63-video-pipeline` | 1409 |
+| `z63-upload-window` | 214 |
+| `z63-top` | 153 |
+| `z63-gpio-top` | 94 |
+| `gicv3_redist_region[0]` | 89 |
+| `z63-sibling-blocks` | 63 |
+| `z63-main-dma` | 40 |
+| sequencer, SPI, CGN, UART, EMIO, … | < 30 each |
+
+**No register is polled.** Nothing is read repeatedly with an unchanging
+answer, and no access falls outside a modelled region. An execution trace
+agrees: 4890 distinct translation blocks run, covering the kernel, the BSP
+driver layer at 0x40c00000-0x40cc0000, platform code at 0x40d20000-0x40d70000,
+some service-layer code at 0x40e30000-0x40f50000 and the card HAL area at
+0x402b0000, and the hottest blocks are all kernel infrastructure -- a
+`MPIDR_EL1` reader, a `CNTVCT_EL0` reader, and the TOPPERS kernel's
+`LDAXR`/`WFE` spinlock. All three cores end in the kernel idle task.
+
+So the platform bring-up this board provides is already sufficient for the
+RTOS to boot, schedule and idle. It is not blocked on a missing device, and
+adding device models cannot change its behaviour until something starts the
+camera application, which on EXPEED 7 does not happen by itself: the module
+constructor/entry/start rounds are normally sequenced by `SystemControl`,
+which waits on results no inert register model produces. The Z 8 project
+reached the same wall and drives those rounds over GDB instead.
+
+The order that follows from this: drive the module lifecycle first, and let it
+be what makes a device's absence observable; then this page list says which
+device, and the trace report says which register inside it. Modelling ahead of
+that is modelling without a test.
