@@ -0,0 +1,769 @@
+# Register windows: turning a stop into a modelled block
+
+Phase 2 of `plan.md` is "turn the crank": the machine stops on an access it
+does not understand, and the job is to work out what the firmware expects
+there, model it, and run again. This document is the method that emerged,
+because the first few turns were done the slow way and the difference is
+large: a turn now costs a harvest and a build instead of a reversing session.
+
+## The shape of the problem
+
+Most EXPEED 7 peripherals are not reached by a constant. The driver is a
+family of very small accessors, each of which loads the device base out of a
+descriptor and applies one offset:
+
+```
+LDR Xn, [X0,#8]          ; the device base
+LDR/STR Wt, [Xn,#imm]    ; one register
+```
+
+No address scan of the text finds such a block, and nothing about it is
+visible until the firmware touches it and the machine stops. For a block like
+this there is nothing to model but the register file, so the model is:
+
+* an offset the firmware is known to name is **storage**;
+* an offset it is not **stops the machine with its PC**.
+
+That is `hw/arm/nikon-z63-regwindow.h`. It does not model behaviour and must
+not grow to. A block that needs a side effect -- an interrupt, a completion
+bit, a FIFO -- needs its own `MemoryRegionOps`, with the firmware address
+that demanded the side effect in the comment above it.
+
+Storage is not a shortcut. Where the firmware read-modify-writes a register,
+reading back what was written is the firmware's own stated expectation.
+Where it only ever reads, storage is a **declared choice**, and each block's
+header says so and lists which offsets those are. A poll on a bit real
+silicon would raise hangs rather than stops, which is what
+`z63-tools/where_blocked.py` is for.
+
+## Three base shapes
+
+`z63-tools/handle_offsets.py` reports every constant offset a function range
+applies to a device base, in whichever of three shapes the driver uses.
+
+| shape | how the base arrives | flag |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| descriptor field | `LDR Xn,[X0,#8]` | default, `--field` |
+| constant + index | `MOVK` the window base, then `ADD Xd, Xbase, Xidx, LSL#k` | `--window-base` |
+| runtime pointer | `LDR Xd,[Xn]` or `[Xn,Xi,LSL#3]` where `Xn` is a `.data` slot | `--array-base` |
+| materialised constant | the whole register address is a `MOV`/`MOVK` pair | `--const-window` |
+| an argument | one generic accessor pair, offset passed in | `--call-arg` |
+
+The last two have opposite failure modes and both are stated in the headers
+they generate. `--const-window` counts a constant the firmware computes but
+never dereferences, so it over-approximates. `--call-arg` only sees calls
+whose argument it can pin down, so it under-approximates: the next stop in
+such a window is expected to be a computed offset rather than a missing
+constant. Tail calls count as calls -- `sub_40C5B368` writes +0x7f8 with a
+`BL` and +0x7fc with a `B`, and matching only `BL` found the first and missed
+the second.
+
+The third is the one no static scan can follow, and it is the common one.
+The slots are in the device descriptor table described below, so the base is
+usually in the image after all -- just not in the instruction stream.
+
+`z63-tools/make_window.py` runs the harvest and writes the header.
+
+## It has to be a CFG analysis
+
+The first version walked instructions in address order. That is not a weaker
+analysis, it is a wrong one. These accessors pick an offset with a branch or
+a CSEL chain, and the chosen offset is applied in a block that an
+address-order walk reaches after the branch has already overwritten the
+register. `sub_40C2BD0C` is the case that forced the rewrite:
+
+```
+LDR  X8, [X0,#8]
+CMP  W1, #2
+B.EQ 0x40c2bd38
+ADD  X9,  X8, #0x16c      <- false path
+ADD  X10, X8, #0x22c
+ADD  X8,  X8, #0x10c
+CSEL X8, X10, X8, NE
+CSEL X8, X9,  X8, EQ
+B    0x40c2bd3c
+ADD  X8,  X8, #0x1cc      <- true path, X8 is still the base here
+STR  W2, [X8]             <- +0x10c, +0x16c, +0x1cc or +0x22c
+```
+
+For the sensor interface the address-order walk reported **33 offsets that do
+not exist** and missed **79 that do**. Worse, the phantom offsets looked
+meaningful: fifteen tidy register pairs at +0x450, +0x968, +0xe10 and so on,
+which invited the reading that they were per-lane status, fifteen of them for
+the fifteen records in the driver's table. They were an artefact of the walk.
+
+The tool is now a forward worklist over the basic-block graph, a register
+holds the *set* of offsets it may hold, and a join is a union.
+
+Two idasql pitfalls cost real time here and are guarded against in the tool:
+
+* `cfg_edges` is a virtual table and only yields rows for an **equality**
+  constraint on `func_addr`. A range predicate returns nothing, with no
+  error. The tool now refuses to report offsets if branching functions came
+  back with no edges, rather than silently degrading to the linear scan.
+* Several columns come back as text, so a bare numeric comparison matches
+  nothing. Everything is `CAST(... AS INTEGER)`.
+
+## Which IDA database
+
+**`z63_eg.elf`, md5 `0fedcc0523da1dac8127a8010ccf978b`** matches
+`eg2010_0200i1.bi` (md5 `b024ec7148e24ec0493e27d4d0f20b0e`), the image this
+board loads. It is on port 8084.
+
+`vr2070.elf` on port 8082 is a **different image**. Its register layout is
+nearly identical -- 195 of 197 offsets agreed for the sensor interface -- and
+most functions sit at the same addresses, which is exactly what makes it
+dangerous: reading this image's PCs through it produces plausible, wrong
+answers. It puts the sensor interface's record table at 0x4164bf18, where the
+shipped image has code.
+
+The test that settles it: `SELECT key,value FROM binary WHERE key='md5'`.
+`docs/symbols.md` was checked against both and lines up with 8084 (86 exact
+function-start matches against 10), so it is sound.
+
+## The device descriptor table
+
+The firmware ships its own id-to-base map in `.data`, around flat
+`0x451fe400`-`0x451fe900` (runtime `0xa0280d400`, using the measured `.data`
+delta `0x9bd60f000`). Entries are a device id followed by its MMIO base, in
+groups that share a block of driver entry points:
+
+```
+flat 0x451fe588   0x40c1b704 0x40c1b848 0x40c1b89c 0x40c1b8a0   <- the ops
+flat 0x451fe5a8   0x192  0x150ce000
+flat 0x451fe5c8   0x193  0x150ce400
+...
+flat 0x451fe7f0   0x185  0x15340000   0xa02dc4900
+flat 0x451fe810   0x186  0x15380000   0xa02dc5100
+flat 0x451fe8c8   0x18e  0x15080000
+```
+
+This is the same shape as the display descriptor at `0xa02808438`
+(`{0x1c7, 0x15480000, ...}`). It is the fastest route from a stop to a model:
+the stopping address is usually in this table under an id, its siblings are
+named next to it, and the group's entry points bound the driver whose code
+`handle_offsets.py` should be pointed at. `z63-tools/window_pointers.py`
+searches the image for pointers landing in a given window and is the other
+half of the same idea.
+
+## Blocks modelled this way so far
+
+| base | name | offsets | header |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 0x0c86e000 ×2 | AUX | 123 | `nikon-z63-aux.h` |
+| 0x0e700000 ×12 | serial bus | 36 | `nikon-z63-spibus.h` |
+| 0x15000000, 0x15040000 | the 0xa02dc47xx pointer-array family | 680 | `nikon-z63-ispband.h` |
+| 0x15080000 | sensor interface | 508 | `nikon-z63-sensorif.h` |
+| 0x150c0000 | ISP_APB device 0x1a0 | 209 | `nikon-z63-isp-apb.h` |
+| 0x15200000 | the 0xa02e7b0c0 block | 152 | `nikon-z63-vpipe-ctl.h` |
+| 0x15100000 ×2, 0x40000 apart | devices 0x1cf, 0x1d0 | 588 | `nikon-z63-dev1cf.h` |
+| 0x15180000, 0x15182000, 0x151c0000 | devices 0x1cc, 0x1cd, 0x1ce | 90 | `nikon-z63-dev1cc.h` |
+| 0x15240000 | the 0xa02dc3d88 pointer slot | 143 | `nikon-z63-blk1524.h` |
+| 0x15340000 ×5, 0x40000 apart | devices 0x185, 0x186 and their siblings | 680 | `nikon-z63-blkfam.h` |
+| 0x16020000 | CODEC | 74 | `nikon-z63-codec.h` |
+
+Two of these replaced hand-built models that listed only the offsets that had
+happened to be seen (`SPI` had three of thirty-six, `AUX` three of a hundred
+and twenty-three). Both were stopping the machine on the first register their
+driver reached that nobody had yet watched it reach. Expect the same of any
+block still modelled the old way.
+
+Three of them started as separate windows and were merged once the pattern
+showed. That is the move worth watching for:
+
+* **0x15340000, 0x15380000, 0x15440000** were three windows until a fourth
+  stop landed on 0x153c0044 and made the 0x40000 stride visible. One strided
+  window of five instances replaced them, and it cleared 0x153c0000 and
+  0x15400000 without either ever being investigated. The upper bound is not
+  a guess: 0x15340000 + 5 × 0x40000 is exactly 0x15480000, the display
+  controller base.
+* **0x15000000 and 0x15040000** are reached through slots 0x18 apart in a
+  pointer array in BSS. That array cannot be read out of the image, so there
+  is no static enumeration to be had and the header is a base *list* that
+  grows by one line per stop. The register set is already known for all of
+  them: a harvest seeded at any one slot returns the union over every device
+  the array serves, and two independent harvests agreed exactly.
+
+The lesson both times: a stop is worth a moment's arithmetic against the
+blocks already modelled before it is worth a reversing session. Several of
+these turns cost two tool calls.
+
+## The frontier
+
+`z63-tools/frontier` records the address the RTOS currently stops on, and
+`smoke.sh` checks that it stops *there* and nowhere earlier. A stop at any
+other address is news and needs a person: either the crank moved forward, or
+a model that used to work has stopped working. Nothing else distinguishes
+those two, which is why the file exists.
+
+While that file holds an address, `smoke.sh` skips the Linux check rather
+than failing it. A refused access stops the whole machine, so CPU3 never gets
+far enough to print; Linux reaching a shell is the exit condition of this
+phase, not a gate on it. The file's history section is also the shortest
+account of what this phase has actually done.
+
+## After the crank: the blocker class changes
+
+With every stop cleared, the RTOS runs 45 seconds without an unmodelled
+access and `smoke.sh` reaches a BusyBox prompt on CPU3 in the same run. What
+stops progress after that is a different kind of thing, and it needs
+different instruments.
+
+**A stuck interrupt line costs 92% of the machine.** The first measurement
+after the last stop was cleared looked like a guest hung in a handler: CPU1
+sat at 0x40c16ae8 in EL1h and one register, 0x0e110104, was written three
+and a third million times in a twenty-five second run while the two other
+cores idled in `WFI`. `-d int` settled what it was: **2,259,027 IRQs taken
+in 45 seconds**.
+
+The cause was this board asserting a line it did not own. The DMA model at
+0x155c0000 was wired to `HDMAC0_0 + 8 * (ch / 2) + (ch % 2)`. But HDMAC is
+three units of eight channels, SPI 149-172, and the firmware describes
+exactly those twenty-four channels in an array at runtime 0xa0280c438 whose
+`+0x10` field holds INTIDs 181 to 204 -- SPI 149 to 172, HDMAC0_0 to
+HDMAC2_7 in order -- and whose `+0x08` field holds each channel's register
+base, in 0x0e11xxxx. So the lines belong to the controller in the PERI band;
+the one at 0x155c0000 is a different controller whose own descriptors (flat
+0x451fdd58, ids 0x17a..0x17d) carry no interrupt number at all.
+
+The model raised the line on its first completion, the firmware acked the
+controller it actually owns, the line stayed high, and the guest spent the
+run re-entering the handler. Unwiring it took the interrupt count to **5,707**
+and the block-execution count from 32.8M to 2.65M -- the storm had been
+burning 92% of the machine. Distinct blocks went 18421 -> 19095 and the app
+and UI region's reach went 15.0% -> 19.7%.
+
+Three things worth keeping from that:
+
+* **Asserting the wrong line is much worse than asserting none.** A missing
+  interrupt shows up as a wait, which is easy to read. A spurious one shows
+  up as a guest apparently stuck for no reason, which is not. The DMA model
+  now reports once if it would have raised a line it does not have, so the
+  missing interrupt stays visible instead of becoming silent.
+* **`-d int` is the instrument for this class**, and it is cheap. Counting
+  `Taking exception 5 [IRQ]` separates "spinning in a loop" from "re-entering
+  a handler", which no amount of reading the disassembly settles. `z63_set_irq`
+  in the board reports the first assert and first deassert of each named
+  line, so a line this board leaves high is named rather than inferred.
+* **A core's PC and PSTATE over QMP is the first thing to look at**, not the
+  last. `info registers -a` gave CPU0 and CPU2 at 0x40190bd8 -- the idle
+  task's `WFI`, already in `symbols.md` -- and CPU1 somewhere else entirely.
+  Note the monitor reads the GIC as non-secure, so `GICD_ISPENDR` reads back
+  zero for the RTOS's secure interrupts; that route does not work here.
+
+**What is next, and what it needs.** The display is not being programmed:
+`DISP-UNIT0` took 5 register writes in a 25-second run and `DISP-UNIT1` took
+12, none of them a framebuffer address or a geometry value. So Phase 3 is not
+blocked on the display model, it is blocked on the firmware not getting that
+far. The remaining known gaps on the path are HDMAC (a real controller at
+0x0e11xxxx behind a storage-only window, with twenty-four interrupt lines
+nobody raises) and the interrupt the 0x155c0000 DMA cannot deliver. Both are
+"model the device" work rather than "find the stop" work, which is why the
+crank has stopped producing turns.
+
+## What the machine looks like once nothing stops it
+
+Every number here was measured after the last stop was cleared, so this is
+the state the next round starts from rather than an impression.
+
+* **The module lifecycle completes.** `sub_40D6FB64` runs three times and the
+  round bodies at 0x40d6fd50, 0x40d6fea8 and **0x40d70000** each execute once:
+  constructor, entry and **start**. The start round had never run before.
+  z8-qemu drives these rounds over GDB; this board no longer needs to.
+* **189 tasks exist, named.** Walking the TCB array at 0xa04cf8078 (stride
+  0x480, `+0x10` is `p_tinib`, and the TINIB's `+0x08` exinf carries the
+  name) gives `DISPVDSR`, `DISPOFF`, `TFTDIR`, `TFTEVFSR`, `HDMISRV`,
+  `HDMIOBS`, `EYEDEV`, `TEMPSRV`, `FILESRV`, `FILESVP`, `FILEWM`, `FILEWP`,
+  `CARDPS0/1`, `FILSVEV3/4`, `DSPS0/1/2`, `EXPRTSND`, `EXPRTRCV`,
+  `IMPFRCTL`, `IMPFRCTM`, `IPUS` and the rest.
+* **They are idle, not deadlocked.** State low byte: 88 waiting on a data
+  queue, 48 on a mailbox, 40 dormant, 4 runnable, 3 delayed, 2 on an event
+  flag. All three RTOS cores sit in the idle task's `WFI` at 0x40190bd8. The
+  whole machine makes 4,661 MMIO reads in 40 seconds. Nothing is spinning;
+  there is simply nothing to do.
+* **The display is not programmed.** `DISP-UNIT0` takes 5 register writes in
+  a 25-second run and `DISP-UNIT1` takes 12, none of them a framebuffer
+  address or a geometry value. Phase 3 is not blocked on the display model,
+  it is blocked on the firmware not getting there.
+
+### A hypothesis that was tested and is wrong
+
+Every GPIO pin reads 0. `sub_40C15BFC` is the generic pin read -- it takes a
+bank base out of the runtime pointer array at 0xa02dc41b8, indexed by the
+descriptor's `+0x04`, and returns `(*base >> pin[+0x08]) & 1` -- and bank 0's
+input register at 0x0d151010 was read 161 times in a 40-second idle run,
+always answering 0. Camera switches are usually active low, so all-zero could
+plausibly have been telling the application that every switch was closed,
+which would explain a display that never turns on.
+
+It does not. With `-machine gpio-input-level=0xffffffff` every pin reads 1 and
+nothing changes: 17 DISP-UNIT writes, 5 to the display complex, 3,783 MMIO
+writes in all, the same as with every pin at 0. GPIO polarity is not the gate.
+
+The knob stays, labelled as what it is -- a measurement, not a model, unset by
+default -- because the question it answers comes up for every input this
+board holds at a constant.
+
+### What the next round has to do
+
+The remaining work is no longer "find the stop". The machine answers every
+access the firmware makes; what it does not do is **generate events**. A
+camera at rest is waiting for its sensor to produce a frame, its display to
+report vertical blank, and a person to press something. None of those exist
+here, and no register model produces them.
+
+So the next lever is a device that originates something, and the cheapest
+honest one to find is the message each display task is waiting for. Read
+`TCB+0x08` for `TFTDIR`, `DISPVDSR` and `DISPOFF` to get the queue object
+each is blocked on, then find what sends to it; that names the subsystem that
+has to produce the first event. The `-d int` and MMIO-trace instruments above
+are the right ones, and the task walk that produced the list is three
+`pmemsave` calls.
+
+Two known device gaps sit on that path and are worth modelling once something
+needs them, not before: HDMAC -- a real controller whose unit bases are
+0x0e100000, 0x0e110000 and 0x0e120000 with channel *c* at `unit + 0x100 +
+c * 0x20`, confirmed by sub_40C1643C which fills each descriptor's base from
+`qword_4184B578[unit + 3] + 256` in steps of 32 and installs the handler for
+the INTID at `+0x10` -- and the 0x155c0000 DMA's completion interrupt, which
+the firmware's descriptors do not name.
+
+### What each named task is waiting on
+
+Walked from the TCB array with three `pmemsave` calls and no guesswork:
+`TCB+0x08` is the object the task is blocked on, `TCB+0x10` is its `p_tinib`,
+and the TINIB's `+0x08` exinf carries the name.
+
+| task | state | wait object | reading |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| DISPVDSR, DISPOFF, CARDPS0/1, DSPS0/1/2, FILESRV, FILESVP, EXPRTSND/RCV, IPUS | 0x2a | one object each in 0xa04d1b..0xa04d1d | each server idle on its own request queue |
+| TFTEVFSR, HDMISRV, FILEWM, FILEWP, IMPFRCTL, IMPFRCTM | 0x42 | one object each in 0xa04d244.. | idle on a mailbox |
+| EYEDEV | 0x52 | 0xa04d28560 | the one event flag with a queue, shared with one unnamed task |
+| TEMPSRV | 0x01 | - | runnable |
+| **TFTDIR**, **HDMIOBS** | **0x02** | 0xa04cf5108 | neither a queue nor a mailbox -- the object is inside the TCB array, so this is the plain "waiting to be woken" state |
+
+That last row is the interesting one. Every other task is a server idling on
+its own inbox, which is what an idle camera looks like. `TFTDIR` -- the TFT
+director, the task the display services hang off -- is not waiting for a
+message at all; it is waiting to be woken, and so is `HDMIOBS`. Finding what
+wakes it is a smaller question than modelling a sensor, and it is the next
+thing to answer.
+
+Its task index is 12 (TCB array base 0xa04cf8078, stride 0x80) and its
+descriptor is at 0xa02fadbf0, which is the handle the waking code is likely
+to hold.
+
+### The display's first event, traced to one function
+
+`TFTDIR` turned out not to be blocked at all. Its body, `sub_402DF860`, is a
+100 ms polling loop with a two-sample debounce:
+
+```
+while (1) {
+    if (!MEMORY[0xa02fadc41]) {              /* an inhibit flag */
+        v = sub_402DFA04();                  /* sample the position */
+        if (MEMORY[0xa028104e4] == v) MEMORY[0xa02fadc40] = 0;
+        else if (++MEMORY[0xa02fadc40] >= 2) {
+            MEMORY[0xa02fadc40] = 0;
+            MEMORY[0xa028104e4] = v;
+            if (MEMORY[0xa02fadc50])         /* the subscriber */
+                MEMORY[0xa02fadc50](0x42000000, v);
+        }
+    }
+    sub_40D33F20(100);
+}
+```
+
+`sub_402DFA04` is the **monitor-position sensor**. It reads three GPIO pins
+through `sub_40C15BFC` and maps them to a position code:
+
+| pin C | pin A | pin B | result |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 1 | 0 | - | 3 |
+| 1 | 1 | 0 | 4 |
+| 0 | 1 | 0 | 5 |
+| 0 | 1 | 1 | 6 |
+| 1 | 1 | 1 | 7 |
+| 0 | 0 | - | 0 |
+
+The three pin descriptors are static, at `.data` 0xa0280ba88, 0xa0280bab0 and
+0xa0280bad8 (flat 0x451fca88 and on), and each is fully configured: ids
+0x12a-0x12c, **bank 22, bits 4, 5 and 6**, with the "configured" byte at
++0x0c set. So with every pin reading 0 the sensor returns 0, and with every
+pin reading 1 it returns 7.
+
+**This is why the `gpio-input-level` experiment showed nothing, and the
+reason is not the one it was testing.** At 0xffffffff the sensor does change
+from 0 to 7 and the debounce does fire -- but `MEMORY[0xa02fadc50]` is
+**zero**, so the loop publishes to nobody. The display does not come up
+because nothing has subscribed to the monitor position, not because the pins
+read wrong.
+
+The subscriber slot is `TFTDIR_object + 0x60` (the object is at 0xa02fadbf0,
+in BSS, so there is no constant to search for). Of the sites that build
+0xa02fadc50, two are the module's own (`sub_402DF860` reading it and
+`sub_402DF9B4` publishing on demand), one is `sub_402DFB6C` -- which forces a
+position, calls the subscriber with message 0x42070200 and then sets the
+inhibit flag at 0xa02fadc41, stopping the poll -- and one is in the
+application band: **`sub_407484D4`**, at 0x40748ff8.
+
+So the next question is a single one: does `sub_407484D4` ever run, and if
+not, what was supposed to call it? That is one `-d exec` grep away, and it is
+a far smaller question than modelling a sensor.
+
+Two more things found on the way, both worth keeping:
+
+* **The static task table is at va 0x454f4161**: 344 entries, stride 28,
+  big-endian fields with the priority at +0x08 and an eight-byte name at
+  +0x14. It names every task the firmware can create -- `TFTDIR` is entry
+  107, `DISPVDSR` 100, `DISPOFF` 101, `HDMISRV` 105, `HDMIOBS` 106,
+  `MPOMAIN` 108, `EYEDEV` 124, and the tail includes `BATTCHK`, `LENSCMDS`,
+  `SCN_DTCT`, `CO_AV` and `STOMAIN`. 189 of the 344 have live control blocks.
+* **The generic GPIO pin read is `sub_40C15BFC`**: it returns 0 unless the
+  descriptor's +0x0c is set, then takes the bank base from the runtime
+  pointer array at 0xa02dc41b8 indexed by the descriptor's +0x04, and
+  returns `(*base >> desc[+0x08]) & 1`.
+
+### Where the display chain actually breaks
+
+Traced to one function, with the trace to prove each step. In a 25-second run:
+
+| function | trace hits | what it is |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `sub_402DF860` | 1 block | `TFTDIR`'s 100 ms poll -- **running** |
+| `sub_402DFA04` | 2 blocks | the monitor-position sensor -- **running** |
+| `sub_40C15BFC` | 169 | the GPIO pin read -- **running** |
+| `sub_407484D4` | **0** | the candidate subscriber -- never entered |
+| `sub_40752504` | **0** | its only caller -- never entered |
+
+So the sensor works, the poll works, and the subscriber slot stays zero
+because the code that would fill it is never reached.
+
+`sub_40752504` is a phase handler: it takes a bitmask in W0 and dispatches
+with `TBNZ W0,#0` / `#1` / `#2` / `#3`, the same shape as `sub_40CC3F98` and
+the module round bodies. It appears nowhere in the image as a 64- or 32-bit
+pointer, aligned or otherwise, so it is reached through a module table built
+at run time rather than through a static vtable. The call to the subscriber
+sits inside one of those phase bodies and is itself conditional:
+
+```
+BL   sub_40CF581C
+TBZ  W0, #0, loc_407528B4     ; skips the registration
+BL   sub_407484D4             ; 0x4075287c -- the registration
+TBZ  W0, #0, loc_407528B4
+BL   sub_40CF5A2C
+```
+
+So there are two separate things to establish, in this order:
+
+1. **Why the phase handler is never called.** All three module rounds run --
+   `sub_40D6FB64` three times, bodies at 0x40d6fd50, 0x40d6fea8 and
+   0x40d70000 once each -- so the driver reaches the end. Either this module
+   is not in the table the driver walks, or its earlier phase failed and the
+   driver skipped it. Counting the modules each round actually visits, and
+   comparing against the component list, answers this.
+2. **Whether `sub_40CF581C` would then allow the registration.** It gates the
+   call, and nothing is known about it yet.
+
+That is the whole remaining distance to the first pixel being plausible, and
+neither step needs a new device model. Both are reading what the machine
+already does.
+
+**And one level further up.** The round driver `sub_40D6FB64(phase)` walks a
+static table at **0x4408D798 + (phase << 8)**, 32 pointers per group, groups
+0x308 apart, seven groups, stopping at the first NULL in each. The base is
+from the function's own prologue:
+
+```
+MOV  X19, X0                ; the phase
+MOV  X8,  #0x4408D798
+AND  X9,  X0, #0xffffffff
+ADD  X25, X8, X9,LSL#8      ; table = 0x4408d798 + (phase << 8)
+...
+LDR  X8, [X25,X23]
+MOV  W0, W19                ; each handler is called with the phase
+BLR  X8
+```
+
+An earlier version of this section said 0x44080C98 and reported 11 groups and
+48 handlers from it. That address was a transcription error -- the
+decompiler's literal 1141430168 is 0x4408D798 -- so those counts described
+whatever happens to sit at the wrong address and are withdrawn.
+
+So there are two module systems. The one this driver walks is the platform's,
+48 handlers, all in the BSP and service bands, and it runs to completion. The
+display subscriber's phase handler lives in the **application's** own
+component system in 0x40400000-0x40c00000, reached through a table built at
+run time, and that system is where the `app and UI` region stops at 19.7%.
+
+The next round's first question is therefore an application question, not a
+device one: find the table the application's component starter walks, and
+find why it does not reach the component that owns `sub_40752504`. The
+instruments are the same ones used here -- an `-d exec` grep to decide what
+ran, the exinf/TCB walk to name what is waiting, and the static tables the
+image already carries.
+
+### The chain closed: the display waits on the imaging pipeline, not the reverse
+
+Climbing from the subscriber slot to the first thing that actually runs, each
+step checked against a 25-second `-d exec` trace:
+
+```
+0xa02fadc50      the subscriber slot          stays 0
+  sub_407484D4   would fill it                NOT RUN
+  sub_40752504   its only caller              NOT RUN   (a task event handler)
+  sub_4075234C   registers that handler       NOT RUN
+                   -> sub_40C00204(obj 0xa048868b0, handler 0x40752504,
+                                   wait pattern 0xef)
+  sub_40746464   its only caller              NOT RUN
+  sub_4074B484   ran, but took the other path     RAN
+```
+
+and `sub_4074B484` is three instructions:
+
+```c
+__int64 sub_4074B484(int a1) { if (a1 == 4) sub_40746464(); return 1; }
+```
+
+So the whole display subscription hangs on this component being called with
+**4**, and it was called with something else.
+
+`sub_4074B484` is slot 0 of a four-method vtable at rodata **0x45387110**
+(0x4074b484, 0x4074b4a0, 0x4074b4bc, 0x4074b4cc; a second vtable of thunks
+sits just before it at 0x453870f0). Three of the four methods gate on exactly
+the same value:
+
+```c
+sub_4074B4A0(int a1) { if (a1 == 4) sub_407464F4(); return 1; }
+sub_4074B4BC(int a1) { if (a1 == 4) return sub_40746B34(); ... }
+sub_4074B4CC(int a1) { if (a1 & 1)  return sub_40746B48(); ... }
+```
+
+And immediately after the vtable, at 0x45387130, is the table that says what
+4 means -- pairs of (index, value):
+
+```
+0 -> 0x3c   1 -> 0x30   2 -> 0x1e   3 -> 0x18
+4 -> 0x0f   5 -> 0x0c   4 -> 0x0f   7 -> 0x06
+```
+
+60, 48, 30, 24, **15**, 12, 15, 6. Frame rates. Index 4 is 15 fps.
+
+**So this reverses the plan's ordering.** The display subscriber is registered
+when a display-mode callback arrives with mode 4, and a frame-rate mode is
+something the imaging pipeline sets when live view starts. The display is not
+waiting for the display: it is waiting for the sensor. Phase 6 (ISP) and the
+sensor are a prerequisite for Phase 3 (pixels), not the other way round, and
+chasing the display controller's registers first would have been chasing a
+block that is correctly idle.
+
+What to do with that is a choice worth making deliberately rather than
+drifting into:
+
+* **Model the sensor and the ISP far enough to produce a frame.** Honest, and
+  the only route to a real picture, but it is the largest piece of work in
+  the plan and the sensor interface's register semantics are mostly unknown.
+* **Find what sets the frame-rate mode and whether anything short of a real
+  frame will do it.** Much cheaper, and the answer is one more climb: find
+  the caller of the vtable at 0x45387110 and what supplies its argument. If
+  the mode is set from configuration rather than from a frame arriving, the
+  display may come up on a still pipeline, which would make every later
+  display-register question testable without a sensor.
+
+The second is the next step, because it is cheap and because its answer
+decides whether the first is necessary.
+
+### The gate's argument, measured
+
+Static reading could not settle what the display gate is called with, because
+every step is an indirect call through a table built at run time. QEMU's
+gdbstub does settle it, and `z63-tools/rsp.py` is a 60-line GDB-remote client
+that does nothing but set breakpoints and print argument registers. Start the
+machine with `-gdb tcp::<port> -S` so the breakpoints are in place before the
+first instruction, then:
+
+```
+rsp.py <port> 0x4074b484 --hits 4
+  hit 0: pc=0x4074b484  x0=0x3 x1=0x5f x2=0xa03d7bf70 x3=0x1
+         x19=0x3 x20=0x180 x21=0xa0292d3d0 lr=0x40d70b58
+```
+
+**x0 = 3, and the gate needs 4.** It is called repeatedly with 3 and never
+with anything else; x21 is the component list and lr is inside the component
+dispatcher, so the path is confirmed from the other end too.
+
+One level up, `sub_40D70B00` -- the dispatcher that calls all 59 components'
+slot 0 -- is itself entered with x0 = 3 and lr = 0x40d6fbec, which is the
+`BLR X8` return site in the round driver. And the round driver itself, broken
+at its own entry over 40 hits, is only ever called with **1 and 2**:
+
+```
+rsp.py <port> 0x40d6fb64 --hits 40
+  distinct x0 values seen: {'0x1': 27, '0x2': 13}
+```
+
+That looked like a contradiction -- the driver does `MOV W0, W19 ; BLR X8`,
+so a handler cannot see 3 when the phase is 2 -- and the resolution is a tail
+call. `sub_40D70000` is a phase-2 table entry (phase 2, group 1, slot 3) and
+reaches the lifecycle vtable without a `BL`, so LR still points into the
+driver while x0 has been replaced. The phase and the component step are
+different numbers: **phase 2 drives component step 3**.
+
+So the remaining question is one step wide: which phase-table entry reaches
+the 14-method lifecycle vtable at 0x4408ece0, and what would make it pass 4
+rather than 3. `sub_40D70B00` is slot 0 of that vtable and appears in no
+phase table, so it is reached through an object, and the vtable's other
+slots -- 0x40d70b74, 0x40d70be8, 0x40d70c58 ... 0x40d71444 -- are the same
+walk over different component lists (0xa0292d3d0, 59 entries; 0xa0292d5a8,
+43; 0xa0292d700, 4) calling different methods.
+
+**A correction to record.** The counts in the previous section came from
+reading the phase tables at 0x44080C98, which was a mis-transcription of the
+decompiler's literal 1141430168 = **0x4408D798**. Read at the right address
+the tables are: phases 0, 1 and 2, seven groups each 0x308 apart, 32 slots
+per group terminated by NULL -- **57 slots, 47 distinct handlers**, with
+phase 2 carrying almost all of them (1, 4, 4, 17, 15, 1, 1 per group). The
+four round bodies the earlier session found are phase 2 group 1:
+0x40d6fd38, 0x40d6fd50, 0x40d6fea8, 0x40d70000. Phase strides by 0x100 and
+groups by 0x308, so phases above 2 would overlap group 1 -- there are three
+phases, not more, which matches the driver only ever being called with 1
+and 2 (and 0 at boot).
+
+## Phase 3: the display is modelled, with scanout
+
+`nikon-z63-scanout.h` is the consumer the display register file never had.
+`nikon-z63-display.h` already stores the whole 0x4000 window per unit, so
+every register below already read back what the firmware wrote -- but nothing
+read the configured framebuffer, so a correctly programmed controller would
+still have shown nothing.
+
+**The register meanings did not have to be re-derived.** ../n-research/z8-qemu
+reversed this controller from the Z 8 3.11 image, with a reproducible
+extractor and per-register function provenance, in
+`docs/native-lcd-controller.md` and `docs/native-lcd-minimum-tuple.md`. It is
+the same block, and the two images agree independently: that work names
+resource 0x1c7 with register base 0x15480000, auxiliary reset 0x15490800 and
+LUT RAM 0x15c80000, and the Z 6III image's own descriptor table at `.data`
+0xa02808438 holds exactly `{0x1c7, 0x15480000, 0x15490800, 0x15c80000}`.
+
+What the scanout reads, relative to a unit base:
+
+| offset | meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| 0x15cc | output mode bit; selects which timing pair is live |
+| 0x132c / 0x1328 | mode 0 output width / height (1024 x 682 on the Z 8) |
+| 0x13ac / 0x13a8 | mode 1 output width / height (1280 x 960) |
+| 0x1530 | output LUT enable; nonzero is a stage this does not run |
+| 0x2200 & 7 | pixel format: 0 is R,G,B,A bytes; 2 is little-endian 0xABGR 4444 |
+| 0x2204 / 0x2208 | composite canvas extent / destination origin |
+| 0x2640 | ROI enable, bits 0..9 |
+| 0x2400 + 4*s | ROI source extent: width low 16, height bits 16..29 |
+| 0x2440 / 0x2444 | slot 0 primary DMA address, low and high |
+| 0x2480 / 0x2484 + 8*(s-1) | slots 1..9 primary DMA address |
+| 0x2580 + 4*s | primary byte stride, low 16 bits |
+| 0x2600 + 4*s | ROI placement relative to the destination origin |
+
+It runs off its own 30 Hz timer rather than the host display's refresh, which
+is both more faithful -- a real controller scans out continuously once
+enabled -- and means it behaves identically under `-display none`, so the
+diagnostics work headlessly. No interrupt is raised and no register changes,
+so the period is invisible to the guest.
+
+**It refuses rather than invents.** A frame is produced only for a
+configuration it can read honestly: nonzero output size, 0x1530 clear, at
+least one enabled slot with a nonzero address and stride, and a format the
+Z 8 work established. Anything else leaves the console blank and reports the
+reason once, naming the register. Scaling, the overlay LUT and matrix, alpha
+blending, indexed colour and the serializer at 0x1548d000 are unmodelled;
+slots are drawn in index order with no blending, and a run with more than one
+enabled slot says so once.
+
+### What it says today, and what that means
+
+```
+Z63_SCANOUT: no frame -- output size is 0x0 (mode 0, from +0x132c/+0x1328)
+```
+
+That is the whole current state of the display, at register granularity: the
+firmware has not written the output size. The moment it does, pixels appear.
+
+### Proving the model without the firmware
+
+The firmware not programming the controller makes the model untestable from a
+plain boot, so `z63-tools/scanout_check.sh` programs it by hand over the
+gdbstub with the minimum tuple from the Z 8 work -- mode 0, 1024x682, format
+0, one ROI slot, a DRAM address, stride 4096 -- and asserts a first frame:
+
+```
+ok -- 1024x682 mode 0 format 0, 1 slot(s), slot 0 at 0xa0a000000 stride 4096
+PASS
+```
+
+So the scanout reads the register file, accepts the documented layout, fetches
+1024 x 682 x 4 bytes from guest DRAM and composes a frame. This tests the
+model against documentation; it is deliberately **not** part of `smoke.sh`,
+because a frame made from registers a human wrote says nothing about the
+firmware. `z63-tools/rsp.py --write` is what makes it possible, and it goes
+through the guest address space so it reaches device models exactly as a
+guest store would.
+
+---
+
+## A sixth base shape, and where an enumerated set is wrong
+
+The five shapes above all end in an immediate the text names, which is what
+makes a static offset set complete. There is a sixth that does not:
+
+**The offset is computed by a loop whose extent is an argument.** At
+0x40c23974, writing the window at 0x15040000:
+
+    40c23978  MOV  W12, W1            ; the count, an argument
+    40c23980  MOV  W11, #0x28c
+    40c23990  ADD  X15, X8, X9,LSL#2  ; base + 4k
+    40c239c0  ADD  X15, X8, X11       ; base + 0x28c + 0x10k
+    40c239c8  ADD  X11, X11, #0x10
+    40c239dc  STUR W13, [X15,#-8]     ; base + 0x284 + 0x10k
+
+No dataflow over registers can enumerate that; the reachable offsets depend
+on a runtime value. A set derived from the immediates therefore has a hole
+at every k the immediates miss, and **each hole is a false stop** -- the
+machine halts on a register the firmware is plainly entitled to write, and
+the halt teaches nothing. 0x150402c4 is 0x284 + 0x10*4, and it stopped the
+crank for exactly this reason.
+
+`Z63RegWindow.dense` makes the whole span storage for such a window. It
+concedes the tripwire for that one window and nothing else: same storage,
+same declared choice. Prefer the enumerated set; set `dense` only with the
+loop that forces it quoted above the call.
+
+## Blocks modelled in the loader round
+
+| Window | Base | Shape | Header |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| EXS1780 | 0x17800000 | materialised constant | `nikon-z63-exs1780.h` |
+| EXS1784 | 0x17840000 | materialised constant | `nikon-z63-exs1784.h` |
+| BLK-0x15D8 | 0x15d80000 | materialised constant | `nikon-z63-blk15d8.h` |
+| DSP x3 | 0x1c800000, 0x1d800000, 0x1fc00000 | descriptor `[handle+8]`, bases named by the driver's own handle init at 0x40c0a254 | `nikon-z63-dsp1c80.h` |
+| DSP-0x1B800000 | 0x1b800000 | materialised constant | `nikon-z63-dsp1b80.h` |
+| DSP local memory | 0x1b000000 | RAM -- memcpy writes it, no register offsets | in the board |
+
+The first two were the ones `docs/storage-registers.md` section 8 recorded
+as "not storage" and deliberately left unmapped; the crank reached them.
+
+Two notes the method earned here:
+
+* **The RTOS's own console is a better instrument than a block count.** Once
+  it came up it printed `task [STSQNOR] ... EC = 00000025 : Data abort ...
+  WnR 1 ... DFSC 0x10 Synchronous External abort ... FAR = 0x16807F10`,
+  which named the block, the access and the task in one line. That abort was
+  not a stop: XDMAC's `MemoryRegionOps.valid` capped accesses at 4 bytes, and
+  QEMU turns an access outside `valid` into a bus error rather than calling
+  the handler. **A window that can be reached with a 64-bit access must put
+  the width in `valid` and narrow it in `impl`**, or the model refuses in a
+  way the board never sees.
+* **A gdb-remote breakpoint must be stepped off before continuing.** A bare
+  `c` from a PC that still carries the breakpoint re-reports the same stop
+  forever, which looks exactly like the guest calling one function in a
+  tight loop. It cost two wrong readings here ("the firmware is retrying the
+  DMA", "it is in a reset loop") before `z63-tools/..`/`bp.py` was fixed to
+  remove the breakpoint, single-step, and re-arm.
