+# The blocker is missing serial-NOR resource bodies
+
+The display, the menus and everything behind them are waiting on one thing,
+and it is not a device model. It is data that is not in the firmware package.
+
+## How the chain was traced
+
+Each step was measured, not inferred. `z63-tools/rsp.py` breakpoints gave the
+argument registers; `-d exec` traces decided what ran.
+
+```
+the display subscriber slot 0xa02fadc50 stays 0
+  <- sub_407484D4 would fill it                    NOT RUN
+  <- sub_40752504  its only caller                 NOT RUN
+  <- sub_4075234C  registers that handler          NOT RUN
+  <- sub_40746464                                  NOT RUN
+  <- sub_4074B484  `if (a1 == 4) sub_40746464();`      RAN, with a1 = 3
+```
+
+`sub_4074B484` is slot 0 of a four-method component vtable at rodata
+0x45387110, reached from `sub_40D70B00`, the dispatcher that calls all 59
+components' slot 0 with a step number. Breaking on the gate 120 times gives
+`{0x3: 120}` -- it is never called with 4.
+
+The phase-2 handler table at `0x4408D798 + (phase << 8)` says why. Its
+handlers run in order up to **0x40d70880** and then stop: 0x40d7089c onward,
+fourteen of them, never run. And those are exactly the handlers that deliver
+step 4:
+
+```
+0x40d70880:  BL sub_41186AAC ; BL sub_40D70C5C ; LDR X1,[X0] ; MOV W0,#4 ; BR X1
+0x40d7089c:  BL sub_40D70DFC                   ; LDR X1,[X0] ; MOV W0,#4 ; BR X1
+0x40d708b4:  BL sub_40D70AEC                   ; LDR X1,[X0] ; MOV W0,#4 ; BR X1
+```
+
+0x40d70880 ran its first block and no more, so it is stuck in its first call.
+`sub_41186AAC` is one line:
+
+```c
+return sub_40D326F0(0xa04ceebb8, 64, 0);
+```
+
+which is `wai_flg` on the object at 0xa04ceebb8 for bit 6. Reading that object
+out of a running machine gives its id, **71**, followed by its name in ASCII:
+**`ROMRAM`**. Its accessor family at 0x41186a18..0x41186ae4 is six waits and a
+create -- `sub_40D324C0(obj, 0x44b13d6c)`, and 0x44b13d6c is the `ROMRAM`
+string -- and waits on 0x3, 0xf, 0x10, 0x20 and 0x40, which is a staged
+progression. **Nothing in the image sets those bits through this object.**
+
+So: the whole display and menu lifecycle waits for stage 6 of a ROM-to-RAM
+subsystem that never reports it.
+
+## What ROMRAM is reading, and why it is empty
+
+The SoC's own region table calls 0x45980000-0x48000000 **SNOR_USER** -- the
+serial NOR beyond the firmware image. The image carries the partition map for
+it, a 0x28-stride table at rodata **0x4531fd24**:
+
+```
+[ 0] base 0x40000000  size 0x00160000     the loader
+[ 1] base 0x40160000  size 0x05820000     the SOLID image
+[ 2] base 0x45980000  size 0x00060000     the first SNOR_USER partition
+[ 4] base 0x459e0000  size 0x00020000
+...
+[10] base 0x45aa0000  size 0x00260000
+[11] base 0x45d00000  size 0x000a0000
+[15] base 0x45e00000  size 0x00080000
+[17] base 0x462e0000  size 0x00020000
+[18] base 0x46300000  size 0x000a0000     (flags 0x14)
+...
+```
+
+and more beyond -- the flags field is not always 1, which is what cut an
+earlier enumeration short at eighteen entries.
+
+This board maps 0x40000000 + 128 MiB as one rom_device, so SNOR_USER is
+mapped and reads back **all zeros**. That is why there is no unmapped-access
+stop and also why nothing works: the resource readers validate a body before
+using it, and the image carries the marker they check -- `ENDM` immediately
+followed by `MDNE` at rodata 0x4531ea38. Zeros fail that check, so no
+resource body is ever accepted, so ROMRAM never reaches stage 6.
+
+`ENDM` is a **string-list terminator**, which the image shows directly: at
+0x441b802b it ends a list of update filename patterns
+(`SB??????.BIN`, `WR??????.BIN`, `RG??????.BIN`, `ENDM`) and at 0x454faf1c a
+list of service names (`Err`, `CapSvQue`, `CapSvUif`, `BufRelAl`,
+`CapSvMeb`, `ENDM`). So a resource body carries a list the reader walks until
+it sees that marker -- which is why the Z 9 project's parameter input only has
+to replace a body's final four bytes with it.
+
+**These bodies are not in any firmware package.** They are factory-written,
+per-unit flash content: parameter tables, calibration, sequence resources.
+
+## An independent project reached the same conclusion, and got a menu
+
+`../z9-qemu` (WonderLantern, QEMU 10.2.4, Nikon Z 9 C 5.32) runs the original
+English menu and shooting HUD. Its README is explicit about how:
+
+> Several resource bodies are missing. The profile supplies small **synthetic
+> virtual ROM inputs**, one parameter body derived at runtime from the user's
+> firmware defaults, dummy SPI receive values and explicit external status
+> events.
+
+and its `docs/architecture.md`:
+
+> The host MCU peer clocks the modeled SPI FIFO, receives original requests
+> and supplies synthetic replies. The GPIO edge model is incomplete: hardware
+> breakpoints coordinate explicit GIC inputs with the original ISR
+> boundaries. This is debug-assisted peripheral servicing, not a measured
+> physical MCU.
+
+**And its addresses are ours.** `tools/parameter_input.py` uses
+`ADDRESS, SIZE = 0x462e0000, 0x20000` -- entry [17] of the Z 6III partition
+table above, exactly. `tools/z9_sequence_resource_input.py` groups start at
+`0x45aa0000` -- entry [10], exactly. `tools/init_table_input.py` supplies two
+bytes at 0x47f62dd4 and `z9_primary_gate_input.py` two halfwords at
+0x47e8b568, both inside SNOR_USER. Two bodies, two independent reversing
+efforts, one flash layout.
+
+Its discipline is also worth copying verbatim. Every input is a separate
+tool, each one says in its docstring that it is a diagnostic and **not**
+recovered calibration, each records the bytes, the address and a SHA-256 of
+both the input and the tool that made it, and the firmware itself is never
+edited -- the bytes go to QEMU's ROM loader. `parameter_input.py` derives its
+body from *the firmware's own built-in defaults* and only replaces the final
+four bytes with the reader's `ENDM` tag.
+
+## What this changes
+
+The plan had Phase 3 (pixels), 4 (input) and 6 (ISP) as separate device work.
+They are not reachable in that order. Nothing downstream of ROMRAM runs, so:
+
+1. **Supply the minimum SNOR_USER framing** the Z 6III's own readers check,
+   derived the way the Z 9 project derives it -- from the firmware's defaults
+   plus the marker -- as separate, self-documenting, clearly-labelled inputs.
+   This is the only thing standing between the current machine and the
+   lifecycle advancing past step 3.
+2. Then the external peers: the MCU over the serial bus at 0x0e700000 (the
+   twelve controllers already modelled), ADC samples, orientation, and a
+   periodic external status peer. The Z 9 work says these are what the camera
+   app needs beyond a correct platform, and it names the mechanism:
+   breakpoint-assisted servicing at the ISR boundaries rather than inventing
+   register values.
+3. The scanout in `nikon-z63-scanout.h` is already waiting, and already says
+   which register it needs.
+
+What must not be copied is any claim of correctness for the synthetic data.
+A body that satisfies a marker check is a diagnostic input. It makes the
+firmware proceed; it does not make the firmware right.
+
+## Done this round, and what it measured
+
+**SNOR_USER is now its own region.** It used to fall inside the 128 MiB image
+window and read back zeros silently. It is now a separate overlay that still
+reads zero but reports the first access to each partition with the PC that
+made it. That is how the readers below were found; nothing about the values
+changed.
+
+Twelve partitions are read during a 50-second boot, and the shape is clear:
+
+```
+0x45a5fffc  pc 0x40d76814  size 1    partition 0x45a40000 + body 0x1fffc
+0x462ffffc  pc 0x40d76814  size 1    partition 0x462e0000 + body 0x1fffc
+0x4639fffc  pc 0x40d76814  size 1    partition 0x46300000 + body 0x9fffc
+0x46c5fff8  pc 0x40d76204  size 4
+0x45e00004  pc 0x40d76b94  size 4
+0x47e80231  pc 0x4116d5a4  size 1
+0x47f97b38  pc 0x41178a64  size 2
+```
+
+`0x40d76814` reads the **last four bytes of a partition's body**, which is the
+trailer, and `sub_40D7675C` is the validator around it. Its last act says
+exactly what the trailer has to be:
+
+```c
+v = sub_40D76F88(...);
+if (v[3] == 77 && v[2] == 68 && v[0] == 69 && v[1] == 78) return ok;
+return false;
+```
+
+69, 78, 68, 77 -- **`ENDM`**. Derived from the Z 6III's own reader, and it
+agrees with what the Z 9 project says about its parameter body.
+
+**The partition table decodes completely**: 44 entries at 0x4531fd24, 0x28
+each, with the base at +0x04, the partition size at +0x14 and the **body
+size** at +0x18. Where the two differ the gap is the trailer; 15 SNOR_USER
+partitions have one. Four of them are the Z 9 project's four resource groups
+at the same addresses -- 0x45aa0000, 0x46ca0000, 0x47700000, 0x47e80000 --
+and a fifth is its parameter partition at 0x462e0000.
+
+**The framing is built and loadable.** `z63-tools/snor_user_input.py` reads
+the partition table out of the image passed to it, writes `ENDM` at each of
+the 15 trailers and nothing anywhere else -- **60 bytes** in a 40 MB region --
+and records the firmware hash, its own hash, every marker address and an
+explicit statement that the bodies are not recovered. `-machine
+snor-user=<file>` loads it, refuses a file of the wrong size rather than
+padding, and says on every boot that it is framing only.
+
+**What it changed, measured:** the trailer reads now return 0x45 (`'E'`)
+instead of 0, two partitions that were never read before (0x46300000 and
+0x46320000, from a different reader at 0x4163a97c/0x4163aa54) are now read,
+and the distinct-block count over a fixed run went **18,985 to 19,499**.
+
+**What it did not change:** 0x40d70880 still executes only its first block.
+The ROMRAM wait is still unsatisfied, so step 4 is still never delivered and
+the display subscriber slot is still zero. The framing was necessary and is
+not sufficient.
+
+### The next question, narrowed
+
+Nothing in the image sets ROMRAM's bits through its object. The seven
+functions that name 0xa04ceebb8 are six waits and one create; a search of
+every call site of the three set-flag primitives found no site that passes
+that object. The nearest candidate, `sub_41180D34` in the same module, sets
+**0xa04ce7e48** -- a different object in the same area -- with bit 4.
+
+So the bits come from somewhere that does not hold the object as a constant:
+an ISR using `iset_flg` with the numeric id (71), or a producer task that has
+not started. The staged patterns the waiters use -- 0x3, 0xf, 0x10, 0x20,
+0x40 -- look like a producer reporting progress, which favours the second.
+
+The cheap next step is to break on the create at 0x41186a18 and on each wait,
+and read the caller chain with `z63-tools/rsp.py`; that names the module that
+owns ROMRAM, and its other functions are where the setter will be.
+
+## The real flash content was already on disk
+
+The update package genuinely contains only `eg` and `li`, and the partition
+table proves it. But **a full NOR dump from a body was already in the tree**:
+`/Users/admin/LiBr/nikon/ida/z63_eg.elf`, the IDA input, has a single LOAD
+segment spanning **0x40160000-0x47fffff0** -- the whole window, not just the
+package component, which is why it is 132 MB against the package's 88.8 MB.
+
+Its SNOR_USER content is unmistakably real:
+
+```
+0x45a5fffc  45 4e 44 4d                    ENDM
+0x462ffffc  45 4e 44 4d 38 30 34 34 36 30 37   ENDM then "8044607"
+0x45980000  1b 5b 33 31 6d 0a 2a 2a 2a 2a      ESC[31m "**** !! ex..."
+0x462e0000  41 55 44 49 4f                 "AUDIO"
+```
+
+`z63-tools/snor_user_extract.py` takes it out (40,100,272 of 40,370,176 bytes
+covered; the tail is filled with 0xff, the erased state) and records the
+dump's hash, so a run can always say which body it came from. This is
+per-unit data: fine for your own emulator, not for publication.
+
+With it loaded the readers get real values -- 0x9, 0x270, 0x3c0 where they
+used to get 0 -- and read partitions they never reached before. The 60-byte
+synthetic `ENDM` framing is superseded; `snor_user_input.py` stays for anyone
+without a dump, and the board cannot tell them apart, so the tools record
+which was used.
+
+**A premise of this board was wrong, and the firmware says so.** The
+rom-page-stub's comment claimed the boot ROM's service page at 0x08201000 is
+mask ROM that no firmware component contains. It is not: `sub_40D710DC`
+copies 0x1000 bytes from **0x40195000 in the eg image** to 0x08201000 and
+then uses the page. WorkSRAM (0x08200000-0x08400000, from the region table) is
+now RAM and the firmware installs its own page; `rom-page-stub=on` pre-fills
+the six known entries with `ret` for the calls that happen before that copy,
+and is now a pre-fill of RAM rather than a region that refuses writes.
+
+## The display blocker is one DMA completion
+
+The whole chain, every step measured:
+
+```
+display subscriber slot 0xa02fadc50 == 0
+  <- step 4 never delivered (the gate is called with 3, 120 of 120 times)
+  <- phase-2 handlers from 0x40d7089c never run; they are the ones that
+     MOV W0,#4
+  <- 0x40d70880 runs only its first block: sub_41186AAC
+  <- wai_flg on ROMRAM (flag 71) for bit 6
+  <- set by sub_41186A54(64) in sub_40D710DC
+  <- which stops at the block at 0x40d710fc, the call to
+     sub_40CAB028(ch, 0x08201000, 0x40195000, 0x1000)
+  <- a DMA copy that was not completing
+```
+
+`sub_40D710DC` executes exactly six blocks -- 0x40d710dc, e8, ec, f0, f4, fc
+-- and the sixth is the one that builds `dst = 0x08201000`,
+`src = 0x40195000`, `len = 0x1000` and calls the copy.
+
+### The DMA is IDMAC, and its lines are SPI 76-81
+
+`sub_40C00704(intid, arg, handler)` stores {handler, arg} at
+**0xa02c2c750 + intid * 0x18**. Dumping that table from a running machine
+gives **199 installed handlers**, named from the interrupt-name table at
+0x41847000 -- a complete runtime interrupt map. Six of them are IDMAC0_0
+through IDMAC2_1, INTIDs 108-113, SPI 76-81, handlers 0x40c1878c, 0x40c1882c,
+0x40c188cc, 0x40c1896c, 0x40c18a0c, 0x40c18aac, one per channel and 0xa0
+apart. And the first reads this controller:
+
+```c
+sub_40C1878C() {
+    v0 = MEMORY[0x155C0008];
+    if (v0 & 0x10) { ... MEMORY[0x155C000C] ... }
+}
+```
+
+0x155c0000 is `z63_dma_bases[0]`, so **the block this board models as its main
+DMA is IDMAC**, and bank-major channel numbering lines up with the SPI order.
+That also corrects the completion bit: the model set bit 0 of +0x08, while the
+handler tests **0x10**.
+
+Wired, with the completion bit fixed: the line now asserts, the **IDMAC1_0
+handler at 0x40c188cc runs** (channel 2 is the one the copy uses), 4,432
+interrupts are taken in 35 s -- a normal rate, not the storm an earlier wrong
+line produced -- and the distinct-block count went 19,159 to **19,533**.
+
+**Still blocked**: `sub_40CAB028` does not return and `sub_41186A54` does not
+run. The interrupt is delivered and its handler executes, so what is missing
+is downstream of the handler -- the completion notification reaching the
+waiter. `sub_40C1878C`'s tail is
+`MEMORY[0xA0280CD78](0x41204001, MEMORY[0xA0280CD80])`, a callback through a
+DRAM slot, and `sub_40C19240` is the other path it takes; one of those is
+where the copy's waiter gets woken. That is the next thing to read, and it is
+a software path, not a device.
+
+## The copy is XDMAC, and that is the whole remaining gap
+
+The chain below the ROMRAM wait resolves completely:
+
+```
+sub_40D710DC stops at 0x40d710fc
+  -> sub_40CAB028(ch, 0x08201000, 0x40195000, 0x1000)
+     validates the address-space codes, sets the channel state to 1, tails to
+  -> sub_40CAAF20(ch, dst, src, len, &desc)
+     four sub_40CAB97C address checks, fills the descriptor, then
+       sub_40C1B4D8(dev, desc, ...)   programs the device
+       sub_40CAAAC8(ch, &status)      waits
+  -> sub_40CAAAC8:
+       sub_40D325CC(0xa030511c8, mask)      clear the channel's bit
+       sub_40C1B608(dev, arg)               start
+       sub_40D32608(0xa030511c8, mask, &v)  WAIT for the channel's bit
+```
+
+So the copy waits on event flag **0xa030511c8**, bit per channel.
+
+**It is not IDMAC.** Wiring IDMAC's lines was right on its own evidence --
+`sub_40C1878C`, installed for INTID 108, reads 0x155C0008, which is
+`z63_dma_bases[0]` -- and with it wired the line asserts, the channel-2
+handler at 0x40c188cc runs, 4,432 interrupts are taken in 35 s and the
+distinct-block count went 19,159 to 19,533. But following its completion
+path to the end shows it sets a **different** flag:
+
+```
+0x40ca7bec:  X0 = 0xa03050cdc        ; the flag IDMAC's completion sets
+             W1 = [X8]               ; the channel's bit
+             B  sub_40D32590         ; set_flg
+```
+
+0xa03050cdc, not 0xa030511c8. Two layers, two flags: the low-level IDMAC
+driver at 0x40ca7xxx with its table at 0xa02 8141xx, and the copy API at
+0x40caaxxx/0x40cabxxx with its table at 0xa02814298.
+
+The copy API programs its device through `sub_40C1B4D8` and `sub_40C1B608`,
+which are the **0x40c1b000 driver family** -- the same functions that appear
+in the offset harvest behind `nikon-z63-isp-apb.h`. Looking that family up in
+the runtime handler table names it outright:
+
+```
+INTID  34 (SPI  2) PERI_XDMAC_0   handler 0x40c1b444
+INTID  35 (SPI  3) PERI_XDMAC_1   handler 0x40c1b658
+INTID  36 (SPI  4) PERI_XDMAC_2   handler 0x40c1b660
+INTID  37 (SPI  5) PERI_XDMAC_3   handler 0x40c1b668
+INTID 125 (SPI 93) EXS_XDMAC_0    handler 0x40c1b670
+INTID 126 (SPI 94) EXS_XDMAC_1    handler 0x40c1b678
+INTID 127 (SPI 95) EXS_XDMAC_2    handler 0x40c1b680
+INTID 128 (SPI 96) EXS_XDMAC_3    handler 0x40c1b688
+```
+
+**The ROM-to-RAM copy is an XDMAC transfer**, its registers are the
+0x150c0000-family blocks this board models as plain storage, and none of its
+eight interrupt lines is ever asserted. That is the whole remaining gap
+between here and the display coming up.
+
+What it needs is a real XDMAC model rather than a line to wire: which
+instance and channel the handle at `0xa02814298 + 32*ch` selects, which
+registers start a transfer and report completion, and how the handler
+acknowledges. The offsets are already harvested in `nikon-z63-isp-apb.h`; what
+is missing is behaviour, and `sub_40C1B4D8`, `sub_40C1B608` and the handlers
+above are exactly the functions that define it.
+
+## XDMAC modelled, and ROMRAM's stage 6 is set
+
+`nikon-z63-xdmac.h` replaces the two storage windows this board called PAIR-A
+and PAIR-B. They are the two XDMAC controllers, and the firmware's own
+descriptors at 0xa0280d388 (0x40 apart, read out of a running machine) name
+every field:
+
+```
+ch  id     register base   INTID  handler
+0   0x1bf  0x0c850010      0x22   0x40c1b444      PERI_XDMAC_0, SPI 2
+1   0x1c0  0x0c850040      0x23   0x40c1b658      PERI_XDMAC_1, SPI 3
+2   0x1c1  0x0c850070      0x24   0x40c1b660
+3   0x1c2  0x0c8500a0      0x25   0x40c1b668
+4   0x1c3  0x16808010      0x7d   0x40c1b670      EXS_XDMAC_0,  SPI 93
+...
+```
+
+Those eight handlers are exactly what the runtime handler table has installed
+for those INTIDs, so the descriptor and the GIC agree. The register map comes
+from the three functions that use it: `sub_40C1B4D8` programs +0x00 (length
+minus one), +0x04 (source), +0x08 (destination) and the configuration words;
+`sub_40C1B608` starts; `sub_40C1B444` and its seven thunks handle completion,
+reading the cause from +0x20's low nibble and acknowledging with
+`+0x20 &= ~0xf` and `+0x18 &= ~3`.
+
+**The start bit was wrong on the first attempt, and the write trace said so.**
+`sub_40C1B608` sets bit 28 on every write, including the acknowledgement path,
+so treating bit 28 as the start made each acknowledgement launch another
+transfer. The trace was unambiguous: one write of 0x11109003 followed by
+**0x11109000 written 2,386,913 times in 25 seconds**, and 1,459,631
+interrupts taken. The run bits are **0 and 1** -- `sub_40C1B608` sets both,
+the handler clears both -- and starting on their 0->1 edge brings the
+interrupt count to **75**.
+
+With that in place:
+
+```
+Z63_XDMAC: XDMAC-PERI channel 1 first transfer, 4096 bytes
+           0x040195000 -> 0x008201000 (SPI 3)
+```
+
+which is exactly the copy `sub_40D710DC` was stuck on, on the one channel
+whose descriptor carries a completion callback. The init sequence then runs
+`sub_40D321BC`, `sub_40C7A9FC` and **`sub_41186A54(0x40)`** -- the call that
+sets ROMRAM's stage-6 bit, the thing the whole display and menu lifecycle has
+been waiting for. Its executed-block count inside `sub_40D710DC` went from
+6 to 15 and its frontier from 0x40d710fc to 0x40d711cc.
+
+Two more regions were mapped on the way, both on the region table's own
+authority: **WorkSRAM** 0x08200000-0x08400000, so the firmware can install its
+own boot-ROM service page, and **BUFFER_EXT** 0x840000000-0x900000000, which
+the kernel began using as soon as the init got that far.
+
+### And the state that leaves: a new fault, named
+
+This did not come free. The firmware now installs and runs the **real**
+boot-ROM service page -- 913 bytes of genuine AArch64 code at 0x40195000,
+identical in the package and in the body dump, building 0x0820xxxx addresses
+-- where before it ran six `ret` instructions. The boot now goes somewhere
+else: CPU0 and CPU2 are busy rather than idle, cycling near the per-core
+entry at 0x4016915c, and the kernel at **0x40168a04** writes a three-word
+record -- `0x4, 0x0, 0x400002a0`, the last being a code address -- to an
+address at the top of an unmapped band (0x8ffffff90 with the dump loaded,
+0x7ffffffb0 without). That is a fault log whose destination is itself bad,
+which is the signature of a fault taken somewhere the model answers wrongly.
+
+So the trade this round made is explicit: ROMRAM is unblocked and the
+lifecycle's hard stop is gone, and in exchange the firmware now executes code
+it never used to, and faults. The distinct-block count went 19,533 to about
+7,400 because the machine resets rather than idling.
+
+The next question is bounded: read the service page at 0x40195000 -- it is
+plain code in the image, already disassembled -- and find which access it
+makes that this board answers wrongly. `0x400002a0` in the fault record is
+the first thing to look up.
+
+### What the fault actually is: the loader is missing
+
+The fault record the kernel writes at 0x40168a04 is three words, and the third
+is a code address: 0x400002a0 on one run, 0x400001f0 on another. Both are in
+**0x40000000-0x40160000**, which the partition table's entry [0] calls the
+loader and the region table calls LOADER.
+
+Neither input contains it. The update package has exactly two components, `eg`
+loaded at 0x40160000 and `li`; and the full NOR dump's single LOAD segment also
+starts at **0x40160000**. So the 1.4 MB below the RTOS image is absent from
+both, and this board maps it as part of the image window, reading zeros --
+which as instructions is `udf`.
+
+So the real boot-ROM service page branches into the loader, and the loader is
+not there. **The stub was standing in for something real after all** -- not
+mask ROM, as its comment claimed, but the loader that the service page calls.
+That is why six `ret` instructions got the machine further than the genuine
+page does.
+
+The fault address varies between runs (0x7ffffffb0, 0x7ffffff00) because it is
+a stack pointer in an unmapped band, so the frontier file records one of them
+and a different run may differ by a few words; the PC, 0x40168a04, is the
+stable part.
+
+Two ways forward, and they are a real choice rather than a detail:
+
+1. **Keep the stub authoritative.** Do not let the copy's page be entered, so
+   the machine keeps the behaviour that reached 19,533 blocks. ROMRAM still
+   completes, because the copy itself succeeds -- it is entering the copied
+   page that fails. This is the smaller change and it keeps the progress.
+2. **Get the loader.** It is 0x160000 of flash below the image, readable the
+   same way the rest of the NOR dump was. With it, the service page runs for
+   real and the stub can go away entirely.
+
+Option 1 is the next thing to do because it is reversible and keeps the gain;
+option 2 is what makes it correct.
+
+---
+
+## The loader is in hand, and it is what puts the image in DRAM
+
+Two things in the section above were wrong and are withdrawn.
+
+**"The loader is in no input, so this needs a fresh dump."** It is in an
+input. `out/z63/rtos/eg_z63_0x40000000.bin` is a 128 MB read of the window
+from 0x40000000 taken off a running body, and its first 0x160000 is the
+loader. The PTP blocklist that made this look impossible only guards the
+RTOS-side handler; Linux-side addressing does not go through it, which is
+how that dump was taken in the first place. The band holds the boot header,
+the `DSC BootLoader`/`DRAM Initialize`/`draminit error` strings at
+0x40002500, loader code from 0x40020000, an EDK2-derived NVMe/CFexpress and
+card-load stack at 0x400A2000 (including `Program Load to 0x`), and from
+0x400B0000 about 704 KB of 0xFF erase pattern. There is no second image or
+recovery copy down there.
+
+**"The fault is a 3-word record the kernel writes."** It is not a record.
+0x40168a04 is `STP X0, X1, [SP]` -- the synchronous vector saving registers
+with an SP that was never set up. The write went to an unmapped band because
+the SP was garbage, and the SP was garbage because the fault being handled
+came of executing zeros.
+
+The board now takes the band with `-machine loader=<file>` and checks its
+first 0x40 bytes against the header it used to synthesise. That alone
+removed the stop.
+
+### What the loader actually does for us: the image in DRAM
+
+With the band mapped the machine stopped stopping but made no more progress,
+and ran ~100,000 undefined-instruction exceptions per 30 s. They were not FP
+traps and not a reset loop. QEMU was fetching `0x00000000` and caching the
+resulting UDF block, which then raised on every execution.
+
+The firmware's own initial translation tables say why. Walking TTBR0_EL1
+(0xa04f08000, TCR 0x280193519 -- 4 KiB granule, T0SZ 25) inside the image's
+VA range gives:
+
+    0x40000000-0x40167fff   identity
+    0x40168000-0x40194fff   -> PA 0xa17200000      0x2d000
+    0x40195000-0x40bfffff   identity
+    0x40c00000-0x417fffff   -> PA 0xa16600000     0xc00000
+    0x41800000-0x451f8fff   identity
+
+Three windows of text live in DRAM off a base 0xa15c00000. `sub_41189490`,
+which installs exactly those three, and `sub_411893F8`, which bulk-copies
+0x182b000 bytes into the base, are *never executed* on the way to this
+state -- so the mapping is the starting condition and the image is expected
+to be in DRAM already. That is the loader's `Program Load` step.
+
+The content rule is measured, not assumed: the firmware re-copies the first
+window itself (`sub_411896D8` -> memcpy(0xa17200000, 0x40168000, 0x2d000) at
+0x4163aa38), and a dump of that DRAM is 45/45 pages byte-identical to the
+image at the same VA. The other two windows were 0/3072 pages non-zero.
+
+`-machine image-in-dram` (on by default, `z63_image_in_dram` in the board)
+places all three. Undefined-instruction exceptions went from 102,715 in 30 s
+to **0**, and the crank started turning again.
