+# Z 6III storage band: 0x17800000 – 0x17900000
+
+Recovered from the decrypted RTOS component
+`eg2010_0200i1.bi` (sha256 `736ceaa…c47fda`, AArch64 LE, text
+0x40168000–0x4184640C). Every claim below cites the firmware address that
+produced it. Where a value or a behaviour could not be derived from the
+driver, this file says so instead of filling the gap.
+
+---
+
+## 1. What the block is
+
+It is a **pair of SD host controllers using the published SD Host
+Controller Standard register layout**, one of them SD-4.x with the
+**UHS-II** extension. The identification does not rest on the device tree
+or on the base address; it rests on the register semantics.
+
+Five independent confirmations, each from the code:
+
+1. **The interrupt-source name table** at VA 0x41847c20–0x41847d00 (file
+   offsets 0x16e7c20–0x16e7d00) names the blocks outright:
+
+   ```
+   0x41847c20  EXS_SDIO_ALL
+   0x41847c40  EXS_SDIO_SD_CARD
+   0x41847c60  EXS_SDIO_PME
+   0x41847c80  EXS_UHS2_1_F_SDH30_ALL
+   0x41847ca0  EXS_UHS2_1_F_SDH30_SD_CARD
+   0x41847cc0  EXS_UHS2_1_F_SDH30_WAKEUP
+   0x41847ce0  EXS_UHS2_1_F_SDH40_ALL
+   0x41847d00  EXS_UHS2_1_F_SDH40_SD_CARD
+   ```
+
+   `F_SDH30` and `F_SDH40` are the Fujitsu/Socionext SD Host Controller 3.0
+   and 4.0 IP names. `UHS2_1` is one UHS-II-capable slot carrying **both**
+   an F_SDH30 and an F_SDH40 register set, which is why there are two
+   SD-standard register files in the 0x17828000 window (§4, §5).
+
+2. **A literal SDHCI command word.** 0x40291254 writes 0x0CDB0000 to
+   0x1782000C. Split as the standard register pair {Transfer Mode[15:0],
+   Command[31:16]} that is Command = 0x0CDB: index 12, flags 0xDB =
+   ABORTCMD(0xC0) | INDEX_CHECK(0x10) | CRC_CHECK(0x08) |
+   RESP_SHORT_BUSY(0x03) — i.e. `MAKE_CMD(STOP_TRANSMISSION, R1b)`.
+   Transfer Mode = 0. Nothing but an SDHCI command register decodes that
+   way.
+
+3. **The DAT-line busy wait.** 0x4029122C ends with
+   `(~present_state & 0x00F00000) == 0`, i.e. it waits for DAT[3:0] signal
+   level all-high — the standard Present State `DATA_LVL_MASK` at bits
+   20–23.
+
+4. **SD R1 card-status masks applied to the response.** 0x4029122C tests
+   response0 (0x17820010) against 0x073E0000; the UHS-II path tests the
+   byte-swapped response (0x178282A4) against 0xC7380000 (0x4029EC34),
+   0xC7180000 (0x4029F0D0) and 0x21180000 (0x402A1E90). All are subsets of
+   the SD R1 error bits (OUT_OF_RANGE, ADDRESS_ERROR, WP_VIOLATION,
+   CARD_IS_LOCKED, LOCK_UNLOCK_FAILED, CARD_ECC_FAILED, CC_ERROR, ERROR).
+
+5. **The UHS-II bring-up sequence** in 0x4028ECD4 is the published one,
+   register for register: capability bit "UHS-II Supported", Host Control 2
+   `UHS2_INTERFACE_ENABLE` and `UHS_MODE_SELECT = 7`, VDD2 power at 1.8 V,
+   then Present State bit 31 "UHS-II interface detection" and bit 30 "lane
+   synchronization". See §6.
+
+### What matches the standard and what does not
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Matches | Offsets 0x00–0x58 and 0xFC of both files; Host Control 2 bit assignments; Capabilities bits 16–17 / 18–22 / 26 and bit 35; the UHS-II block at +0x80–0xCF; Normal/Error Interrupt Status write-1-to-clear; ADMA Error Status 3-bit field at 0x54 with the descriptor address at 0x58 |
+| Vendor extension | 0x100–0x2FF of the SDIO file and 0x300–0x3FF of the F_SDH40 file. Offsets 0x100 and 0x114 coincide with the published Socionext `F_SDH30_AHB_CONFIG` / `F_SDH30_IO_CONTROL2`; the rest (0x104, 0x108, 0x10C, 0x118, 0x1E0, 0x1E4, 0x200, 0x204, 0x210, 0x224, 0x230, 0x250) do not match anything I can cite and are documented only as observed writes |
+| Does **not** match | The 0x17828000–0x178281FF page: its five used registers (0x60, 0x78, 0x7C, 0x94, 0xFC) are not at SD-standard offsets except 0xFC. See §4 |
+| Absent | No legacy Argument/Command/Response traffic on the F_SDH40 file (it issues commands through the UHS-II command packet instead) and no DMA descriptor list is ever built — every transfer in this image is PIO through the Buffer Data Port |
