+## 6. State machines — what the model has to answer
+
+Each entry is: the write that starts it, the register and bit that is
+polled, the value waited for, and the timeout the firmware allows.
+
+### 6.1 Internal clock stable (both files)
+
+```
+write  Clock Control bit 0 = 1          0x4028EE34 (UHS2)   0x4028D784 (SDIO)
+poll   Clock Control bit 1 == 1         0x4028EE68          0x4028D7AC
+budget 10 ms  (sub_402A3D84 arg = 0xA)
+```
+A second variant at 0x4028FADC sets bit 0 and then polls **bit 0** itself
+for 1 with a 1500 ms budget. Both exist in the image.
+
+### 6.2 Command Inhibit before every command
+
+```
+write  Clock Control bit 2 = 1 (SD clock enable)
+poll   Present State bit 0 == 0
+retry  up to 9 attempts, then give up
+```
+Present in every UHS-II command function: 0x402A1E90, 0x4029EC34,
+0x4029F0D0, 0x4029F404, and 30 more.
+
+### 6.3 UHS-II link bring-up (0x4028ECD4) — the whole sequence
+
+```
+ 0x4028ED2C  SDIO vendor 0x200  = 0 ; delay ; = 0x10000 ; delay
+ 0x4028EE10  F_SDH40 0x3C      |= bit 24         UHS-II Interface Enable
+ 0x4028EE18  lower page 0x78    = 1
+ 0x4028EE34  F_SDH40 0x2C      |= bit 0          internal clock enable
+ 0x4028EE68  poll 0x2C bit 1 == 1               internal clock stable, 10 ms
+ 0x4028EE9C  read F_SDH40 0x44, require bit 3    UHS-II Supported
+             -> if clear, return failure and do nothing else
+ 0x4028EECC  F_SDH40 0x28 Power Control = 0x0F   VDD1 on, 3.3 V
+ 0x4028EEF8  F_SDH40 0x28     |= 0xB000          VDD2 on, 1.8 V
+ 0x4028EF18  F_SDH40 0x3C: UHS_MODE_SELECT = 7, Version 4 mode on
+ 0x4028EF40  F_SDH40 0x2C     |= bit 2           SD clock enable
+ 0x4028EF4C  read F_SDH40 0x24 bit 31            UHS-II interface detection
+   bit 31 set:
+ 0x4028F0B8    poll 0x24 bit 30 == 1             lane synchronization, 250 ms
+               then record "UHS-II active" and return success
+   bit 31 clear:
+ 0x4028EF64    lower page 0x94 = 0, = 0x4050, |= 1
+ 0x4028EFA8    poll lower page 0x60 bit 1 == 0   250 ms
+ 0x4028EFD4    lower page 0x78 = 0
+ 0x4028EFF4    F_SDH40 0x2C &= ~bit 2 ; &= ~bit 0
+ 0x4028F040    poll lower page 0x7C bit 0 == 1   250 ms
+ 0x4028F08C    F_SDH40 0x3C &= ~bit 24
+               return failure (caller falls back to the SDIO path)
+```
+
+### 6.4 Execute Tuning (SDIO file)
+
+```
+write  0x3C bit 22 = 1                  ORR #0xC00000
+poll   0x3C bit 22 == 0                 TBZ #0x16
+
+address built at 0x4029427C and at 0x40297B94; both tests follow in-line
+```
+The controller is expected to clear the bit itself when tuning finishes.
+
+### 6.5 Interrupt wait (0x4029B5FC) — the UHS-II completion engine
+
+Every UHS-II command goes through this loop:
+
+```
+loop while  F_SDH40 0x28 & 0x1100 != 0            (either supply still on)
+    if      F_SDH40 0xC0 & 2 == 0                 (no reset in progress)
+        accumulate  F_SDH40 0x30                  normal interrupt status
+        accumulate  F_SDH40 0xC4                  UHS-II error status
+        if error accumulator & (0x0003_0000 | 0xBF or 0x813F)  -> fail
+        if normal accumulator & wanted             -> success
+    timeout -> fail
+```
+The `wanted` masks, and what each caller is waiting for:
+
+| wanted | meaning | helper | callers |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 0x01 | Command Complete | 0x402A0D98 | every command function |
+| 0x02 | Transfer Complete | 0x402A1578, 0x402A189C | read/write completion |
+| 0x10 | Buffer Write Ready | 0x402A21F0, 0x402A1D50 | PIO write, then 0x402A3324 |
+| 0x20 | Buffer Read Ready | 0x402A13CC | PIO read, then 0x402A3670 |
+
+Timeout budget is chosen by mask: 1000 ms if `wanted & 0x0A` in DMA mode,
+2000 ms if `wanted & 0x32`, 10000 ms in one configured case, else 250 ms.
+
+After each wait the helper clears its own bit in 0x34 and 0x38 and masks
+the error enables in 0xC8/0xCC — so a model that never raises an interrupt
+makes the firmware time out and unwind cleanly, which is a real firmware
+path rather than a hang.
+
+### 6.6 PIO data movement
+
+```
+write path 0x402A3324:  0x38 &= ~0x10 ; 0x30 = 0x10 ;
+                        require 0x24 bit 10 ; then store words to 0x20
+read path  0x402A3670:  0x38 &= ~0x20 ; 0x30 = 0x20 ;
+                        require 0x24 bit 11 ; then load words from 0x20
+```
+Word count is `blocks * 128` dwords, i.e. a fixed 512-byte block. Both
+paths handle a misaligned host buffer by shifting across word boundaries;
+that is host-side only and says nothing about the device.
+The SDIO file does the same at 0x40299688 / 0x40298B98.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Host Controller Version is *constrained*, not free
+
+0x4028FF18 gates DMA selection on the version word:
+
+```
+ADMA2 path:  Capabilities bit 19 set  AND  (0x178200FC >> 24) == 1
+ADMA1 path:  Capabilities bit 20 set  AND  (0x178200FC & 0xFF0000) == 0x10000
+```
+
+Byte 0xFF is the Vendor Version and byte 0xFE is the Specification Version.
+So the firmware requires **Specification Version == 1 (SD Host Controller
+2.00) and Vendor Version == 1** before it will use either ADMA mode. The
+only value of the word that satisfies both tests is `0xZZZZ0101` in the
+upper half, i.e. 0x01010000 with no slot interrupts pending. That is a
+*derived* value, not a measured one — but it is the only one the firmware
+accepts.
+
+The UHS-II side's test is weaker: 0x4029B8C8 only requires
+`0x178280FC >= 0x10000`, so any non-zero version satisfies it.
+
+---
+
+## 8. The rest of the band is not storage
+
+The three other windows in 0x17800000–0x17900000 are driven by a code
+region that shares nothing with the SD HAL (0x4028c000–0x402a4000). The
+split is clean — not one function touches both:
+
+* **0x17800000–0x178010FF**, driven by 0x40c5deb4–0x40c5e934. Example:
+  0x40c5e230 writes 0x17800114 = 0x80000000, 0x17800130 = 0xFF,
+  0x17800154 = 0xFF, 0x17800440 = 0x0001005F and routes two bits of
+  0x17800814 into 0x1780043C. No identification attempted.
+* **0x17840000–0x17842840**, driven by 0x40c5ea9c–0x40c6023c. Example:
+  0x40c5ea9c decodes 0x17840600 bits 6,7,15,16,17 into a small state code
+  and writes 0x20000 back to clear one. The brief lists 0x17840058 and
+  0x17840094 as part of the storage block; they are not — they belong here.
+* **0x17880000**, driven by 0x40c717ec–0x40c7187c. 0x40C71848 is a
+  table-indexed register writer (`*(0x17880000 + table[i]) = value`, up to
+  0xA0 entries). The one absolute offset materialised in the image is
+  **0x1788C110**, which is the DesignWare USB 3 `GCTL` offset — so this is
+  almost certainly a USB 3 host, consistent with the
+  `EXS_USB3_TYPE_C_CONTROLLER` entry two rows below the SD entries in the
+  same interrupt-name table. Out of scope here.
+
+These are deliberately **left unmapped** by the companion model. The
+board's existing unmapped trap already stops with a PC, which is a better
+starting point for them than a storage model pretending to own them.
+
+---
+
+## 9. What I could not determine
+
+* **Whether the 0x17828000 page is the F_SDH30 standard register set or a
+  separate PHY page** (§4). The evidence is split and the firmware never
+  touches an offset that would settle it.
+* **The meaning of lower-page bits** 0x60 bit 1, 0x7C bit 0, 0x78's enable,
+  and the 0x94 configuration fields (0x4050 / 0x4440 / 0x4040 plus the bit-0
+  strobe). I know the order they are driven in and the value each poll waits
+  for; I do not know what they control.
+* **The Capabilities values of either controller.** Only five bits of
+  0x40 and one bit of 0x44 are ever read, and the firmware's behaviour tells
+  me what it *wants*, not what the silicon *reports*. Nothing in the image
+  contains a capabilities dump.
+* **UHS-II Response dword 0 bit 23.** Treated as a hard error by every
+  command function, but I cannot name it.
+* **Present State bit 29** (`TBNZ #0x1D` at 0x402A2C38, polled until set
+  before the SD clock is stopped). In the published standard that position
+  is "In Dormant State", which would fit a controlled entry into dormancy,
+  but the firmware's use of it was not traced far enough to confirm the name.
+* **The UHS-II command-packet header and argument encodings.** The constants
+  are 0x2A800010, 0x18800010, 0x19A00010, 0x56000010 in dword 0 with a
+  common low half 0x0010. Driver-side protocol, not device-side behaviour,
+  so the model does not need them — but they are not decoded here.
+* **Vendor registers** 0x104, 0x108, 0x10C, 0x118, 0x1E0, 0x1E4, 0x200,
+  0x204, 0x210, 0x224, 0x230, 0x250 of the SDIO file and 0x100, 0x114,
+  0x118 of the F_SDH40 file: observed values and write order only.
+* **Interrupt numbers.** The interrupt-name table gives eight names for this
+  band but the image's binding of name to SPI was not traced, so the model
+  wires no IRQ.
+* **Whether a card is present at power-on**, obviously, is not a property of
+  the firmware. The model declares an empty slot; see the header.
+
+---
+
+## 10. Where this contradicts the brief
+
+1. **`sdhci@0x17830000` is not in this component.** There is no flattened
+   device tree anywhere in `eg2010_0200i1.bi` (no `d00dfeed` magic, no
+   `sdhci` or `mmc@` string), and no code in the image references
+   0x17830000 at all. That node must come from the Linux-side tree. The
+   RTOS drives 0x17820000 and 0x17828000/0x17828200.
+2. **"1928 code sites" is about 3× too high.** The real count is **631
+   address materialisations across 124 distinct addresses**. The inflation
+   is an artefact: these addresses are built with the four-instruction
+   64-bit form
+
+   ```
+   MOV  X21, #0x8224
+   MOVK X21, #0x1782, LSL#16     <- value is already 0x17828224 here
+   MOVK X21, #0, LSL#32          <- and here
+   MOVK X21, #0, LSL#48          <- and here
+   ```
+
+   so a scan that samples the register after every MOVK counts each
+   materialisation three times. 631 × 3 ≈ 1893, which is where 1928 comes
+   from. The *ranking* is unaffected — this is still by far the
+   most-referenced unmodelled block.
+3. **The "0x17828000 file" offsets in the brief are really two files.**
+   0x220–0x2CC are offsets 0x20–0xCC of an SD-standard register set based
+   at **0x17828200**, which is what makes them interpretable at all. Read
+   as raw offsets from 0x17828000 they decode as nothing.
+4. **0x17840000 is not part of this block** (§8).
+5. The brief lists `0x17820000 +0x124` and `+0x200`; both are real, but
+   `+0x200` is also touched by the *UHS-II* entry path (0x4028ED2C), which
+   is the only place the two controllers' register files are driven from one
+   function.
