+# Nikon Z 6III (EXPEED 7) -- the USB complex
+
+Reversed from the decrypted factory RTOS image
+`eg2010_0200i1.bi` (sha256 `736ceaac...1c47fda`), 88,830,367 bytes, AArch64 LE.
+File offset = VA - 0x40160000, valid up to VA 0x451F8A4F.
+
+The model is `hw/arm/nikon-z63-usb.h`. Read this document before extending it.
+
+---
+
+## 0. Summary, and two corrections to the brief this work started from
+
+**What the complex is.** One Synopsys DesignWare USB 3 (DWC_usb3, "DWC3")
+controller at **0x17880000**, driven by this RTOS component *in device
+(peripheral) mode only*, plus four small glue register files around it:
+
+| base | size used | what it is | confidence |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `0x17880000` | 0x0000..0xD06C | DWC3: xHCI host block + DWC3 global + DWC3 device registers | **certain** |
+| `0x17A00000` | 0x000..0x1024 | EXS-subsystem control: 68 indexed registers + a 10-channel interrupt mux. **Shared with the PCIe driver** | certain it exists and is shared; its *name* is inferred |
+| `0x17A20000` | `+0x010` only | USB3 port 0 PHY / isolation control | role certain, fields partial |
+| `0x17A40000` | `+0x004,008,010,014,024,040` | "Type-C assist": an interrupt-driven helper engine | register roles certain, **function unknown** |
+| `0x17A60000` | `+0x010,018,020,028,038` | USB3 port 1 PHY / isolation control -- same layout as 0x17A20000 | role certain, fields partial |
+
+Three SPIs name it, derived as `index = (name_table_VA - 0x41847000) / 0x20`
+(the same arithmetic that yields the board's already-verified
+`Z63_UART_IRQ_SPI 173` from `UART_0_RX` at rodata 0x418485A0):
+
+* `EXS_USB3_TYPE_C_CONTROLLER`     rodata 0x41847D80 -> **SPI 108**
+* `EXS_USB3_TYPE_C_ASSIST`         rodata 0x41847DA0 -> **SPI 109**
+* `EXS_USB3_NOT_TYPE_C_CONTROLLER` rodata 0x41847DC0 -> **SPI 110**
+
+Two USB3 *ports*, then, matching the two PHY glue blocks 0x17A20000 and
+0x17A60000 -- and matching the camera: one USB-C data port, one further USB3
+instance. The RTOS only ever gadget-drives the Type-C one. Nothing in this
+image touches a second xHCI/DWC3 register window, so if the not-Type-C
+controller has one, only the Linux component knows where.
+
+### Correction 1: the 0x1A000000 / 0x1A400000 / 0x1A800000 / 0x1AC00000 quartet is PCI Express, not USB
+
+Short version: it is four Synopsys DesignWare PCIe root ports, and the
+windows are their **DBI** (Data Bus Interface) apertures. Section 8 has the
+proof, the register map and -- the thing that actually matters for the
+emulator -- how the firmware performs a PCI configuration access.
+
+It is four instances, selected by index, and the index space runs 0..7:
+
+```
+sub_40289CE4 @ 0x40289CE4        (the whole function; it is 232 bytes)
+  case 0: base = 0x1A000000      case 4: base = 0x400000000
+  case 1: base = 0x1A400000      case 5: base = 0x500000000
+  case 2: base = 0x1A800000      case 6: base = 0x600000000
+  case 3: base = 0x1AC00000      case 7: base = 0x700000000
+  v2 = *(u32 *)(base + 0x104);
+  if (v2 & 0x4000) *(u32 *)(base + 0x104) = 0x4000;
+  return (v2 & 0x4000) != 0;
+```
+
+The evidence that this is PCIe and not USB:
+
+* `*(u32 *)(base + 0x04) |= 0x147` at 0x40288xxx inside `sub_40287ED0`
+  (0x40287ED0, 6432 bytes). 0x04 is the **PCI Command/Status** register and
+  0x147 is IO Space | Memory Space | Bus Master | Parity Error Response |
+  SERR Enable -- the canonical bring-up value.
+* The other offsets `sub_40287ED0` touches are `+0x78, 0x7C, 0x80, 0x88,
+  0x8C, 0x90, 0x98, 0xA0` with field masks that are the **PCI Express
+  Capability** at config offset 0x70: 0x78 Device Control/Status (bits 0..4
+  = the four error-report enables + Relaxed Ordering, [7:5] MaxPayload,
+  [14:12] MaxReadReq, [19:16] the W1C Device Status bits -- `MEMORY[0x78]`
+  in the decompilation applies exactly that field set), 0x7C Link
+  Capabilities, 0x80 Link Control/Status, 0x88 Slot Control, 0x8C Root
+  Control, 0x90 Root Status, 0x98 Device Control 2, 0xA0 Link Control 2.
+* `+0x104` bit 14 is the **AER Uncorrectable Error Status** "Completion
+  Timeout" bit, with the AER capability at the usual extended-config 0x100.
+  `sub_40289CE4` is a completion-timeout check, and its callers
+  (`sub_40289BA0`, `sub_40289DCC`, `sub_4028A068`, ... -- a family of
+  byte/word/dword accessors) return error code 4 when it fires. That is a
+  textbook PCI config/MMIO read with abort detection.
+* The interrupt-source table names `EXS_PCIE00/01/10/11_PCIE_MISC`, `_MSI`
+  and `_DMA` (rodata 0x41847DE0..0x41847F40) -- **four** PCIe instances.
+* Indices 4..7 give 4 GiB-aligned 64-bit bases: four outbound memory
+  apertures, one per root complex.
+
+The 4 MiB spacing was real but it is a PCIe DBI/config stride, and the whole
+family lives in a different code cluster (0x40287064..0x4028B42C) from the
+USB driver (0x40C6C000..0x40C71CD8). The two clusters share nothing except
+the `0x17A00000` control block described below. **This model does not map
+the quartet**; it falls through to the board's `z63_unmapped` trap, which
+already reports address and PC. On this camera PCIe is almost certainly the
+CFexpress Type B slot.
+
+### Correction 2: "0x1AA00000" is DRAM, not a register file
+
+The brief listed a register file at `0x1aa00000` with offsets +0x000,
++0x004, +0x010, +0x410 and registers 0x1aa09410 / 0x1aa09820. Every one of
+those is a **64-bit DRAM address with the top byte truncated**. The
+disassembly at 0x40C6DEA8..0x40C6DF3C is explicit:
+
+```
+0x40c6deb4  MOVK  X9, #0x1AA0,LSL#16
+0x40c6debc  MOVK  X9, #0xA,LSL#32          <-- bits [39:32] = 0x0A
+0x40c6dec0  MOVK  X9, #0,LSL#48
+...
+0x40c6dedc  STR   XZR, [X9]                 -> 0xA1AA00000
+```
+
+0xA1AA00000 = `Z63_DRAM_BASE (0xA00000000) + 0x1AA00000`, i.e. 426 MiB into
+DRAM. `sub_40C6DE5C` (0x40C6DE5C, 528 bytes) memsets four spans there, and
+that is the DWC3 gadget's DMA area:
+
+```
+0xA1AA00000  +0x10      EP0 SETUP packet buffer (the DMA target of the
+                        EP0 setup TRB). 0xA1AA00002/4/6 that the brief
+                        flagged as "byte offsets" are wValue/wIndex/
+                        wLength of this packet -- correct, and they are
+                        DRAM, not MMIO.
+0xA1AA00010  +0x400     EP0 data buffer        (memset at 0x40c6dee8)
+0xA1AA00410  +0x9000    nine 4 KiB endpoint data buffers, index<<12
+                                               (memset at 0x40c6df04)
+0xA1AA09410  +0x2490    nine 1040-byte TRB rings, index*1040
+                                               (memset at 0x40c6df20)
+0xA1AA0C000  +0x1000    the DWC3 event buffer  (memset at 0x40c6df3c)
+0xA1AA0D000             a 4-byte flag cleared at 0x40c6e70c
+```
+
+Two independent confirmations that this reading is right: `sub_40C6E658`
+(0x40C6E658) builds a 16-byte TRB at 0xA1AA09410 whose buffer pointer is
+`{lo = 0x1AA00000, hi = 0xA}` and whose control word is 0x0823 =
+HWO|LST|IOC|TRBCTL(2)="Control-Setup"; and `sub_40C70F14` writes
+`GEVNTADRLO0 = 0x1AA0C000`, `GEVNTADRHI0 = 0xA` -- the device-visible
+address of exactly that memset span.
+
+The same truncation accounts for the rest of the brief's "0x1A band" site
+counts: `sub_4036CA58` (the one that references `IAP2CTLSVC`) touches
+0xA1AA1xxxx / 0xA1AA2xxxx / 0xA1AA4xxxx, `sub_4090C73C` touches
+0xA1AAAxxxx / 0xA1AEBxxxx / 0xA1AEDxxxx, `sub_4026EBBC` touches 0xA1AF4xxxx
+(see the `MOVK Xn, #0xA,LSL#32` at 0x4036CAF8, 0x4090C758, and the pair at
+0x4026EBE0). They are gadget-class buffers in DRAM. The genuine 32-bit
+0x1Axxxxxx constants are only the PCIe quartet, and those are emitted as a
+4-instruction `MOV Xn, #0x1A400000` with no `LSL#32` term (0x40288568,
+0x40289C20, ...).
+
+---
+
+## 1. Identification of 0x17880000 as a DWC3
+
+The driver does not name registers with immediates. It calls two accessors
+that index a table:
+
+```
+sub_40C71848 @ 0x40C71848  (write)
+    if ((u32)i <= 0x9F) *(u32 *)(word_4185B3D8[i] + 0x17880000) = v;
+        store site 0x40C71874
+sub_40C7187C @ 0x40C7187C  (read)
+    if (i > 0x9F) return 0;
+    return *(u32 *)(word_4185B3D8[i] + 0x17880000);
+        load site 0x40C718A8
+```
+
+`word_4185B3D8` is **160 x uint16** at rodata 0x4185B3D8 (file offset
+0x16FB3D8), all values distinct and monotonically increasing. Printed in
+full it *is* the published DWC3 register map:
+
+```
+idx   0.. 16  0000 0004 0008 000C 0010 0014 0018 001C 0020 0024 0028
+              0034 0038 003C 0050 0054 0058
+idx  17.. 30  0420 0424 042C 0430 0434 0438 0440 0460 0464 0468 0470
+              0474 0478 047C
+idx  31.. 38  0890 0894 0898 089C 08A0 08A4 08A8 08AC
+idx  39.. 51  C100 C104 C108 C10C C110 C114 C118 C11C C120 C128 C12C
+              C130 C134
+idx  52       C19C
+idx  53       C200
+idx  54       C2C0
+idx  55.. 70  C300 .. C33C   (16 dwords)
+idx  71.. 74  C380 C384 C388 C38C
+idx  75.. 78  C400 C404 C408 C40C
+idx  79.. 83  C600 C610 C618 C61C C624
+idx  84.. 90  C700 C704 C708 C70C C710 C714 C720
+idx  91..154  C800 .. C8FC   (64 dwords)
+idx 155       CA00
+idx 156..159  D060 D064 D068 D06C
+```
+
+Indices are 0-based table positions, which is exactly what the firmware
+passes to the accessors -- so the "idx" column in the register table below
+can be checked against this listing directly.
+
+Why this is conclusive rather than suggestive:
+
+* DWC3 puts its 32-byte xHCI capability block at 0x0000 with
+  `CAPLENGTH = 0x20`. Table entries 0x0020, 0x0024, 0x0028, 0x0034,
+  0x0038/0x003C, 0x0050/0x0054, 0x0058 are then USBCMD, USBSTS, PAGESIZE,
+  DNCTRL, CRCR lo/hi, DCBAAP lo/hi, CONFIG -- the xHCI operational set,
+  with the spec's reserved holes (0x2C, 0x30, 0x40..0x4C) absent.
+* 0x0420/0x0424/0x042C and 0x0430/0x0434/0x0438 are two port register sets
+  at the spec's `op_base + 0x400 + 0x10*(n-1)`, and 0x0440 + 0x0460..0x047C
+  are MFINDEX followed by interrupter 0 at the spec's IMAN/IMOD/ERSTSZ/
+  (hole)/ERSTBA lo,hi/ERDP lo,hi.
+* 0xC100..0xC134 is GSBUSCFG0, GSBUSCFG1, GTXTHRCFG, GRXTHRCFG, **GCTL**,
+  GPMSTS, GSTS, GUCTL1, GSNPSID, GUID, GUCTL, GBUSERRADDRLO/HI; 0xC19C is
+  GUCTL2; 0xC200 GUSB2PHYCFG(0); 0xC2C0 GUSB3PIPECTL(0); 0xC300+/0xC380+ the
+  16 GTXFIFOSIZ / 16 GRXFIFOSIZ; 0xC400.. the event-buffer quad; 0xC700..
+  the device block; 0xC800..0xC8FC exactly **16 endpoints x
+  {DEPCMDPAR2, DEPCMDPAR1, DEPCMDPAR0, DEPCMD}**; 0xCA00 DEV_IMOD.
+* One register is also reachable as a bare immediate -- `0x1788C110`, GCTL
+  -- in `sub_40C717EC` (0x40C717EC), where bit 11 is pulsed. DWC3 GCTL bit
+  11 is `CoreSoftReset`. The sequence there is set / delay / clear / delay:
+  the databook's core soft reset, letter for letter.
+
+Independent cross-checks from the *values* the driver writes:
+
+* `GCTL = old & 0x60DC7 | 0x30C02000` (`sub_40C70F14`). Bits [13:12] = 2 =
+  `PrtCapDir = Device`. The controller is put in **device mode**.
+* `GUCTL = 0x0A414802` (`sub_40C71110`, 0x40C711AC ff.). GUCTL[31:22] is
+  `REFCLKPER`, the reference-clock period in ns; 0x0A414802 >> 22 = 41,
+  i.e. a ~24 MHz reference clock.
+* `DEVTEN = old & 0xC1A0 | 0x25F` -- bits 0,1,2,3,4,6,9, the DWC3
+  Disconnect / USBReset / ConnectionDone / LinkStateChange / WakeUp /
+  EndOfFrame / ErraticError enable set.
+* `DALEPENA` is manipulated as `1 << ((2 * ep) | dir)` -- the DWC3 physical
+  endpoint numbering.
+* `DEPCMD` is composed as `(param << 16) | cmd[3:0]`, with 0x800
+  (`HIPRI_FORCERM`) forced for command 8, 0x100 (`CMDIOC`) added when
+  `DCTL[31] RUN_STOP` is set, and 0x400 (`CMDACT`) always set. Commands
+  seen: 2 (SETTRANSFRESOURCE), 5 (CLEARSTALL), 6 (STARTTRANSFER), 7
+  (UPDATETRANSFER), 8 (ENDTRANSFER), 9 (DEPSTARTCFG).
+
+Four further rodata tables complete the device-mode map, each 16 x uint32:
+
+```
+0x4185B2B0  DEPCMD(n)      = table index 94 + 4n   -> 0xC80C + 0x10n
+0x4185B2F0  DEPCMDPAR0(n)  = table index 93 + 4n   -> 0xC808 + 0x10n
+0x4185B330  DEPCMDPAR1(n)  = table index 92 + 4n   -> 0xC804 + 0x10n
+0x4185B370  DEPCMDPAR2(n)  = table index 91 + 4n   -> 0xC800 + 0x10n
+```
+
+used by `sub_40C702B4` (0x40C702B4) which writes PAR0/PAR1/PAR2 then calls
+the command issuer. Confirmation that the pairing is right: for
+STARTTRANSFER the DWC3 databook wants PAR0 = TRB address bits [63:32] and
+PAR1 = bits [31:0], and `sub_40C701F0` (0x40C701F0) builds its parameter
+block as `{0, trb_addr_lo, trb_addr_hi, frame}` which `sub_40C702B4` routes
+to PAR2, PAR1, PAR0 respectively.
+
+### Access width
+
+**Uniformly 32-bit.** Every MMIO access in the complex is `LDR Wn` /
+`STR Wn`; verified by listing all load/store instructions of
+`sub_40C71848`, `sub_40C7187C`, `sub_402BB2D8`, `sub_402BB30C`,
+`sub_40C716E4`, `sub_40C717B4`, `sub_40C717EC`, `sub_40C7160C`,
+`sub_40C719E4`. The `LDRH` instructions in those functions (0x402BB300,
+0x402BB334, 0x40C71870, 0x40C718A4) all read the *offset tables* out of
+rodata/.data, and the `LDRB`/`STRB` ones touch BSS flags. There is no
+sub-word MMIO anywhere in the complex, so the model's
+`valid.min_access_size = max_access_size = 4` is a measurement, not a
+convention.
+
+---
+
+## 2. 0x17880000 -- the registers the driver actually names
+
+Everything the 160-entry table can reach is a real register, but a model
+cannot usefully answer an offset no call site mentions. The list below is
+the complete set of offsets **named by a call site**, static or through the
+four per-endpoint tables. Everything else in 0x17880000 is refused by the
+model with its offset and PC. Notably that includes the entire xHCI host
+block (0x0000..0x08AC): this RTOS component never touches it. The Linux
+component's device tree calls the same base `socionext,sc2007-xhci`, so the
+host side is presumably Linux's; nothing in *this* image establishes a
+single host register.
+
+Direction is as the firmware uses it. "R/W" means read-modify-write.
