+| 0xC800 + 0x10n | DEPCMDPAR2(n) | 91+4n | 32 | W | n = 0..15 | `sub_40C702B4` 0x40C7032C; also literal 1 written to EP0/EP1 at 0x40C710C0/0x40C710F4 |
+| 0xC804 + 0x10n | DEPCMDPAR1(n) | 92+4n | 32 | W | n = 0..15 | 0x40C7030C, 0x40C710B4/0x40C710E8 |
+| 0xC808 + 0x10n | DEPCMDPAR0(n) | 93+4n | 32 | W | n = 0..15 | 0x40C702EC, 0x40C710A8/0x40C710DC |
+| 0xC80C + 0x10n | DEPCMD(n) | 94+4n | 32 | R/W | command issued, then polled for bit 10 `CMDACT`; `[31:16]` read back afterwards as the transfer resource index | write 0x40C70498, poll 0x40C704B0, readback `sub_40C701F0` 0x40C70284 |
+
+The driver reads PAR0/PAR1/PAR2 straight back after writing them
+(0x40C70334/0x40C7033C/0x40C70344) and discards the values -- a write
+flush, not a dependency.
+
+---
+
+## 3. The glue blocks
+
+### 3.1 0x17A00000 -- EXS subsystem control (shared with PCIe)
+
+A 68-register indexed file plus a 10-channel interrupt mux. Same shape as
+the DWC3 accessors but with the offset table in **.data** rather than
+rodata:
+
+```
+sub_402BB2D8 @ 0x402BB2D8  (write)
+    if (i <= 0x43) *(u32 *)(*(u16 *)(0xA02808508 + 2*i) + 0x17A00000) = v;
+sub_402BB30C @ 0x402BB30C  (read)
+    if (i > 0x43) return 0;
+    return *(u32 *)(*(u16 *)(0xA02808508 + 2*i) + 0x17A00000);
+```
+
+`0xA02808508` is .data (file offset 0x5099508), 68 x uint16:
+
+```
+idx  0..19  0x000,0x004, ... 0x04C      (20 regs)
+idx 20,21   0x080, 0x084
+idx 22      0x100
+idx 23..34  0x300 .. 0x32C              (12 regs)
+idx 35      0x400
+idx 36..42  0x500 .. 0x518              (7 regs)
+idx 43..49  0x600 .. 0x618              (7 regs)
+idx 50..53  0x700 .. 0x70C
+idx 54..57  0x800 .. 0x80C
+idx 58..67  0x1000 .. 0x1024            (10 regs -- the interrupt mux)
+```
+
+The mux is confirmed by `sub_402BB248` (0x402BB248), which is the block's
+ISR:
+
+```
+for (i = 0; i != 10; ++i) {
+    s = *(u32 *)(0x17A01000 + 4*i) & 3;
+    *(u32 *)(0x17A01000 + 4*i) = <same word, bits[1:0] written back>;
+    if (s) { h = callbacks[i]; if (h) h(0, s); }
+}
+```
+(`LDR/LDR/ANDS/BFXIL/STR` at 0x402BB278..0x402BB288; callback table in BSS
+at 0xA02DBF9F0, registered by the stub at 0x402BB348.) So **0x1000..0x1024
+are ten 2-bit, write-1-to-clear interrupt-status registers**.
+
+What the USB driver uses:
+
+| idx | off | what | established at |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 0x24 | 0x500 | `\|= 0xE` then `\|= 1`: four reset/clock bits [3:0] for USB port 0. `= 0xF` in the port-1 path. Cleared with `&= ~0xF` to shut the block down | `sub_40C7160C` 0x40C7160C, `sub_40C719E4`, `sub_40C715A8`, `sub_40C71494` |
+| 0x25 | 0x504 | `= 0x120030` at bring-up; `\|= 3`; `(old & 0xFFECFFFC) \| 0x120003` at shutdown | same |
+| 0x2B | 0x600 | port 1's equivalent of 0x500: `= 6`, then `\|= 1` | `sub_40C71494` 0x40C71494 |
+| 0x2C | 0x604 | port 1's equivalent of 0x504: `= 0x120030`, then `\|= 0x3000003` | `sub_40C71494` |
+
+What the **PCIe** driver uses in the same block (so the model must not
+assume USB ownership): indices 13, 15, 17, 19 -> offsets 0x034, 0x03C,
+0x044, 0x04C, read in `sub_40289B30` (0x40289B30) as
+`2*instance + 13`, bit 1 tested for "link usable"; and indices 49, 51, 52
+-> 0x618, 0x704, 0x708 in `sub_40287ED0`.
+
+The field meanings of 0x500/0x504/0x600/0x604 beyond "reset and clock gates
+for one USB3 port, four of them in [3:0]" **could not be determined**. The
+block is named by inference from its users, not by any string in the image.
+
+### 3.2 0x17A20000 and 0x17A60000 -- the two USB3 PHY/glue blocks
+
+The only register the port-0 block ever shows is `+0x010`, and only bits
+[17:14]:
+
+```
+sub_40C717EC @ 0x40C717EC   (core reset path, port 0 coming up)
+    *(u32 *)0x17A20010 &= 0xFFFC3FFF;         0x40C71814..0x40C7181C
+sub_40C719E4 @ 0x40C719E4   (port 0 going down)
+    *(u32 *)0x17A20010 |= 0x3C000;            0x40C71AA8..0x40C71AB0
+```
+
+Four bits cleared to bring the port up and set to park it: an isolation or
+power-down quad. Which bit is which **is not established**.
+
+Port 1 is the same register at `0x17A60010` and, uniquely, is given four
+tuning values that port 0 never gets:
+
+```
+sub_40C71494 @ 0x40C71494
+    *(u32 *)0x17A60020 = 0x000FE188;
+    *(u32 *)0x17A60028 = 0x00086800;
+    *(u32 *)0x17A60038 = 0x04C6001A;
+    *(u32 *)0x17A60018 = 0x0000042B;
+    ... EXS idx 0x24/0x2B/0x2C writes ...
+    *(u32 *)0x17A60010 |= 0x3C000;            0x40C71AA8-style RMW
+```
+
+The function ends by *setting* the 0x3C000 quad, i.e. it configures the
+second port's PHY and then leaves it parked. Whether that is "prepare it
+for the Linux host driver" or "keep it off" cannot be told from this image.
+The four magic words are unexplained; nothing reads them back.
+
+### 3.3 0x17A40000 -- the "Type-C assist"
+
+Six registers, all established by one function and its ISR. From the
+disassembly (X22 = 0x17A40004 throughout `sub_40C716E4`, so the
+decompiler's displacements are +4 from the base):
+
+| off | dir | value / behaviour | established at |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 0x004 | R/W | `\|= 3`: interrupt **enable**, two sources | 0x40C71750..0x40C71758 |
+| 0x008 | W1C | `= 0xFFFF` (clear all) at arm time, then `\|= 3`; the ISR clears bits [1:0] the same way: interrupt **status** | 0x40C7174C, 0x40C71768..0x40C71778, ISR 0x40C717D8..0x40C717E0 |
+| 0x010 | R/W | `(old & ~2) \| 0x11`: bit 1 cleared, bits 0 and 4 set. Bit 4 is also set alone when the complex is enabled and cleared when it is disabled | 0x40C71728..0x40C7173C; bit 4 at 0x40C716C8/0x40C716D0 (`sub_40C7160C`) and 0x40C71A84/0x40C71A8C (`sub_40C719E4`) |
+| 0x014 | R/W | `(old & 0xFFFFFFE0) \| mode`, mode = 0x11 when the caller's argument is 0, 0x15 otherwise. **This is the arming write**: the block answers with an interrupt | 0x40C7177C..0x40C71788 |
+| 0x024 | W | `= 93750` (0x16E36) -- a divisor or timeout | 0x40C71720 |
+| 0x040 | W | `= 53` (0x35) | 0x40C71744 |
+
+The ISR is `sub_40C717B4` (0x40C717B4), reached through the thunk
+`sub_40C6A32C`:
+
+```
+*(u32 *)0x17A40008 |= 3;          /* clear both status bits   */
+*(u8  *)0xA02E7B078 = 1;          /* signal the waiter        */
+```
+
+**What this block does is not established.** The name
+`EXS_USB3_TYPE_C_ASSIST` (SPI 109) and its position between the two PHY
+glue blocks are the only clues; 93750 and 53 cannot be turned into a clock
+rate without knowing the input clock. It is plausibly CC-line / Type-C
+orientation detection or a USB-PD BMC engine, but this image does not say
+so and the model asserts nothing about it.
+
+---
+
+## 4. State machines -- what the model has to answer
+
+Four waits exist in the whole complex. Three are bounded polls on a
+self-clearing bit; the fourth is unbounded and needs an interrupt.
+
+### 4.1 DCTL[30] CSFTRST -- core soft reset
+
+`sub_40C71110` @ 0x40C71110, transcribed from the disassembly:
+
+```
+0x40c71164  ORR  W1, W8, #0x40000000      ; DCTL |= CSFTRST
+0x40c71168  BL   sub_40C71848             ; write DCTL (index 0x55 = 85)
+0x40c7116c  STR  WZR, [SP,#var_14]        ; i = 0
+0x40c71170  LDR  W8, [SP,#var_14]
+0x40c71174  CMP  W8, #0x3E7
+0x40c71178  B.HI loc_40C711AC             ; i > 999 -> give up
+0x40c7117c  MOV  W19, #0x55
+0x40c71180  MOV  W0, #1
+0x40c71184  BL   sub_40D33F20             ; delay(1)
+0x40c71188  MOV  W0, W19
+0x40c7118c  BL   sub_40C7187C             ; read DCTL
+0x40c71190  TBZ  W0, #0x1E, loc_40C711AC  ; bit 30 clear -> done
+0x40c71194..0x40c711a8                    ; ++i; loop while i < 1000
+```
+
+Model answer: **a write to DCTL stores the value with bit 30 forced to 0.**
+Timing out is not fatal here -- the function just carries on and writes
+GUCTL -- but 1000 iterations of a scheduler delay is a real stall.
+
+This is preceded by `sub_40C717EC` (GCTL CoreSoftReset pulse) which needs no
+answer: the firmware sets and clears bit 11 itself and never reads it back.
+
+### 4.2 DEPCMD(n)[10] CMDACT -- endpoint command
+
+`sub_40C70378` @ 0x40C70378, the command issuer. Transcribed:
+
+```
+0x40c703b0  LDR  W8, [X24]                ; driver mode word
+0x40c703b8  CMP  W8, #2
+0x40c703bc  B.EQ skip                     ; SS: do not touch the PHY
+0x40c703c8  BL   sub_40C7187C  (idx 0x35) ; read GUSB2PHYCFG0
+0x40c703d4  AND  W1, W0, #0xFFFFFEBF      ; clear SusPHY(6) + EnblSlpM(8)
+0x40c703dc  BL   sub_40C71848
+   ... build the command word:
+       cmd 8  -> |= 0x800   HIPRI_FORCERM
+       cmd 6  -> sub_40C7052C first (see 4.4)
+       cmd 7  -> &= ~0x500  (no CMDIOC, no CMDACT)
+       else   -> if DCTL[31] RUN_STOP then |= 0x100 CMDIOC
+                 |= 0x400 CMDACT
+0x40c70498  BL   sub_40C71848             ; write DEPCMD(n)
+0x40c7049c  STR  WZR, [SP,#var_38]        ; i = 0
+0x40c704ac  MOV  W0, W20                  ; the DEPCMD table index
+0x40c704b0  BL   sub_40C7187C             ; read DEPCMD(n)
+0x40c704bc  TBZ  W8, #0xA, done           ; bit 10 clear -> done
+0x40c704c0..0x40c704d4                    ; ++i; loop while i < 1000
+0x40c704e0  B.NE skip_err
+0x40c704f8  STR  W9, [X8]                 ; i == 1000 -> error 8 at
+                                          ; BSS 0xA02E7A8A0
+0x40c70514  BL   sub_40C71848  (idx 0x35) ; restore GUSB2PHYCFG0
+```
+
+Model answer: **a write to DEPCMD(n) stores the value with bit 10 forced to
+0.** Note that the firmware does *not* inspect CMDSTATUS `[15:12]`
+anywhere, so no status code has to be synthesised -- leaving the written
+bits is enough, and the model does not invent a completion code.
+
+`sub_40C701F0` does read DEPCMD(n) back after STARTTRANSFER and keeps bits
+[31:16] as the transfer resource index, later handed to ENDTRANSFER as its
+parameter. On real hardware those bits come back as the hardware-assigned
+`XferRscIdx`; the model returns what was written. **Unmeasured** -- flagged
+in the header.
+
+### 4.3 DGCMD[10] CMDACT -- device generic command
+
+`sub_40C70580` @ 0x40C70580, same shape, no PHY dance and no delay:
+
+```
+0x40c705e8  ORR  W1, W8, #0x400           ; |= CMDACT
+0x40c705ec  BL   sub_40C71848  (idx 0x59) ; write DGCMD
+0x40c705f0  STR  WZR, [SP,#var_24]        ; i = 0
+0x40c70604  MOV  W0, W19  (0x59)
+0x40c70608  BL   sub_40C7187C             ; read DGCMD
+0x40c7060c  TBZ  W0, #0xA, done           ; bit 10 clear -> done
+0x40c70610..0x40c70624                    ; ++i; loop while i < 1000
+0x40c70630  B.NE ...                      ; i == 1000 -> error 8
+```
+
+Model answer: **a write to DGCMD stores the value with bit 10 forced to 0.**
+
+### 4.4 The two reads that must not lie
+
+* `GEVNTCOUNT(0)` -- `sub_40C6C454` (0x40C6C454) reads it once and then
+  walks the DRAM event buffer with `while (1) { ...; if (count <= consumed)
+  break; }`. The loop's only exit is that comparison, so the value returned
+  here decides whether the firmware parses garbage. **The model returns 0**:
+  nothing is attached, no events were produced. Replayed against the loop
+  (section 6) this consumes one zero word from the zeroed event buffer,
+  dispatches nothing -- `((0 >> 6) & 0xF) - 1` is 0xFFFFFFFF, which fails
+  the `>= 7` guard at the top -- and exits on the first iteration.
+* `DSTS` -- read in two places. `sub_40C7052C` (0x40C7052C), called before
+  every STARTTRANSFER, does
+  `if ((DSTS & 0x3C0000) && (DCTL & 0x80000000)) DCTL |= 0x100;`
+  i.e. "if `USBLNKST` is not U0, request Recovery". **0 means U0** and the
+  recovery request is skipped. The other read, at 0x40C6CF6C, takes
+  `DSTS[2:0]` as a 6-way jump-table index and treats > 5 as a fault, so 0
+  (High-Speed) is both legal and the quietest answer -- but it is only
+  reached from the USB-reset *event*, which cannot arrive while
+  GEVNTCOUNT is 0.
+
+### 4.5 The one wait the model cannot satisfy: 0x17A40014
+
+`sub_40C716E4` @ 0x40C716E4, the Type-C assist bring-up, ends:
+
+```
+0x40c7177c  LDR   W8, [X22,#0x10]         ; read 0x17A40014
+0x40c71780  AND   W8, W8, #0xFFFFFFE0
+0x40c71784  ORR   W8, W9, W8              ; mode = 0x11 or 0x15
+0x40c71788  STR   W8, [X22,#0x10]         ; arm
+0x40c7178c  MOV   W0, W19  (10)
+0x40c71790  BL    sub_40D33F20            ; delay(10)
+0x40c71794  LDRB  W8, [X21]               ; BSS flag 0xA02E7B078
+0x40c71798  CMP   W8, #1
+0x40c7179c  B.NE  loc_40C7178C            ; <-- NO BOUND
+```
+
+The flag is set only by the ISR `sub_40C717B4`, which the model does not
+drive. There is no register read in the loop, so there is no place to hook
+a stop except the arming write itself. The model therefore **stops on the
+write to 0x17A40014** with the offending PC, rather than spinning forever
+with no diagnostic. The header says exactly what to add if a bring-up run
+needs to get past it (raise the block's interrupt -- `EXS_USB3_TYPE_C_ASSIST`
+is SPI 109, or it may instead arrive through the 0x17A01000 mux; which of
+the two is wired **was not determined**, because the handler is installed
+at runtime and neither `sub_40C6A32C` nor `sub_40C717B4` appears as a
+32-bit constant anywhere in the image).
+
+This path runs only when the gadget stack is started: `sub_40C6C7F8` case
+0x4000004 at 0x40C6DAB8/0x40C6DAC0 calls `sub_40C7160C` then
+`sub_40C716E4` then `sub_40C70F14`. A board that never selects USB mode
+never reaches it.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Reset values
+
+**None is measured.** Every register file in the model resets to zero, and
+that is a choice, not hardware knowledge. Where it matters:
+
+* `GCTL`, `GUCTL1`, `DCFG`, `GUSB2PHYCFG0`, `GUSB3PIPECTL0`, `DEVTEN` and
+  `DALEPENA` are all read-modify-written, so the bits the driver preserves
+  come straight from the reset value. Real DWC3 reset values for GCTL and
+  GUSB2PHYCFG are non-zero (GCTL's `PwrDnScale` and `U2EXIT_LFPS`,
+  GUSB2PHYCFG's `USBTRDTIM` and `PHYIF`). Zero is wrong on real silicon;
+  nothing in this image reveals the right value, and the driver never tests
+  a preserved bit, so zero is consistent with everything this firmware can
+  observe.
+* `GSNPSID` (0xC120) and the `GHWPARAMS` block are in the offset table but
+  **never read**, so no DWC3 version or configuration has to be invented.
+* `DSTS` is forced to 0 on read rather than being backed by storage; see
+  4.4.
+* `GEVNTCOUNT` is forced to 0 on read; see 4.4.
+* The ten mux status registers at 0x17A01000..0x17A01024 read 0: no
+  interrupt source is modelled.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Replaying the polls against the model's logic
+
+The loops were transcribed from the disassembly above into C and **run
+against the compiled model** -- `hw/arm/nikon-z63-usb.h` included for real,
+its `MemoryRegionOps` called through their function pointers, with
+`z63_stop_read` / `z63_stop_write` replaced by counters. The harness is not
+checked in (it is a throwaway in the session scratchpad); what it printed
+was:
+
+```
+CSFTRST  (0x40C7118C): 1 iteration(s), DCTL=0x80000000
+DEPCMD   (0x40C704B0): 1 iteration(s), DEPCMD0=0x00000106
+  GUSB2PHYCFG0 0x00001540 -> 0x00001540 (must be unchanged)
+DGCMD    (0x40C70608): 1 iteration(s), DGCMD=0x00000002
+event walk (0x40C6C454): 1 iteration(s), 0 dispatched, GEVNTSIZ=0x00001000
+DSTS: 0x00000000 (CONNECTSPD=0 <= 5, USBLNKST=0)
+xHCI HCSPARAMS1 read: 1 stop(s) [R DWC3 0x17880004/4]
+GSNPSID read:         1 stop(s) [R DWC3 0x1788c120/4]
+DSTS write:           1 stop(s) [W DWC3-RO 0x1788c70c/4=0x1]
+assist arm write:     1 stop(s) [W USB-ASSIST-ARM 0x17a40014/4=0x11]
+EXS mux status read:  0x0, stops=0
+EXS 0x504 write/read: 0x120030, stops=0
+EXS 0x520 (unlisted): 1 stop(s) [R EXS 0x17a00520/4]
+PHY0 +0x10 write:     0x3c000, stops=0
+PHY0 +0x14 (unlisted):1 stop(s) [R USB-PHY0 0x17a20014/4]
+```
+
+Every loop exits on its first read. `DEPCMD0 = 0x106` is command 6 with
+CMDIOC set (because RUN_STOP was set) and CMDACT cleared, which is what the
+`TBZ W8,#0xA` at 0x40C704BC tests. `DCTL = 0x80000000` keeps RUN_STOP and
+drops CSFTRST. The GUSB2PHYCFG0 round trip is the one that would silently
+corrupt later state if the model answered 0 instead of the last write.
+
+The same reasoning in table form:
+
+| loop | model rule | iterations | outcome |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 4.1 DCTL CSFTRST (0x40C71168 / 0x40C7118C) | write stores `v & ~(1<<30)` | 1 | `TBZ W0,#0x1E` taken on the first read; no 1000 x delay(1) stall |
+| 4.2 DEPCMD CMDACT (0x40C70498 / 0x40C704B0) | write stores `v & ~(1<<10)` | 1 | `TBZ W8,#0xA` taken first time; `i != 1000`, so the error-8 store at 0x40C704F8 is skipped; GUSB2PHYCFG0 restored to the saved `v19` at 0x40C70514, which the model returns unchanged because the save at 0x40C703C8 reads back what the previous write stored |
+| 4.3 DGCMD CMDACT (0x40C705EC / 0x40C70608) | write stores `v & ~(1<<10)` | 1 | same |
+| 4.4 event walk (`sub_40C6C454`) | GEVNTCOUNT reads 0 | 1 | first word of the zeroed event buffer has bit 0 clear, `((0>>6)&0xF)-1 = 0xFFFFFFFF >= 7` so nothing is dispatched, `v8 += 4`, `v7 = 4`, `count(0) <= 4` -> writes back `GEVNTCOUNT = 0`, unmasks GEVNTSIZ bit 31, returns. No hang, no spurious gadget event |
+| 4.5 assist wait (0x40C7179C) | write to 0x17A40014 stops the VM | n/a | would otherwise never terminate |
+
+The GUSB2PHYCFG0 save/restore in 4.2 is worth spelling out because it is the
+one place where a wrong read would corrupt later state: the model returns
+the last written value, the firmware clears bits 6 and 8, issues the
+command, and writes the saved word back, so the register ends where it
+started. A model that returned 0 unconditionally would silently clear
+whatever the firmware had configured.
+
+---
+
+## 7. What is modelled, what is refused, and where this stopped
+
+**Modelled** (reads return the last write unless stated; unknown offsets
+stop):
+
+* `0x17880000 + 0x10000` -- the 80 established DWC3 offsets, with the three
+  self-clearing bits and the two forced reads of section 4.
+* `0x17A00000 + 0x2000` -- all 68 enumerated offsets, with 0x1000..0x1024
+  reading 0.
+* `0x17A20000 + 0x1000` -- `+0x010` only.
+* `0x17A40000 + 0x1000` -- the six established offsets; `+0x008` reads 0;
+  `+0x014` stops on write.
+* `0x17A60000 + 0x1000` -- the five established offsets.
+
+**Refused, deliberately:**
+
+* Every offset in 0x17880000 that no call site names -- in particular the
+  whole xHCI host block 0x0000..0x08AC, the GHWPARAMS/GSNPSID block, the
+  FIFO-size arrays 0xC300/0xC380, GUCTL2, DEV_IMOD and 0xD060..0xD06C. They
+  are reachable through the index accessor, so a stop there is a genuine
+  lead: it means either a firmware path this analysis never saw, or the
+  Linux host driver running against the same base.
+* Everything outside the enumerated offsets of the four glue blocks.
+* The whole PCIe quartet (0x1A000000, 0x1A400000, 0x1A800000, 0x1AC00000)
+  and its 4 GiB apertures at 0x400000000..0x7FFFFFFFF. Not mapped at all;
+  the board's `z63_unmapped` trap already names the address and PC. Section
+  0 has everything needed to start on it.
+
+**Not attempted at all** (and out of scope by instruction): any USB device
+model, descriptor handling, TRB execution, event generation, or transfer
+emulation. Nothing in this model walks the DRAM rings at 0xA1AA09410 or
+produces an event in the buffer at 0xA1AA0C000.
+
+**Could not be determined:**
+
+1. What 0x17A40000 *is*. Register roles are solid; function is guesswork.
+   The constants 93750 and 53 are unexplained.
+2. Which interrupt line the 0x17A40000 ISR is attached to (SPI 109 directly,
+   or a channel of the 0x17A01000 mux). The handler is installed at runtime
+   and its address appears nowhere as a constant.
+3. The bit meanings of `0x17A20010[17:14]` / `0x17A60010[17:14]` beyond
+   "clear to run, set to park".
+4. The meaning of the four port-1 tuning words 0x000FE188, 0x00086800,
+   0x04C6001A, 0x0000042B at 0x17A60020/28/38/18.
+5. The field layout of EXS 0x500 / 0x504 / 0x600 / 0x604 beyond "[3:0] are
+   four reset/clock gates".
+6. Every reset value in the complex (section 5).
+7. Whether the second USB3 controller has its own xHCI/DWC3 window. Nothing
+   in this component references one.
+8. What `DEPCMD[31:16]` should read back after STARTTRANSFER. The model
+   returns the written word; real hardware returns `XferRscIdx` in [22:16].
+9. Whether `GUCTL1` bit 24 (driven from a config byte) and `DCFG` bit 22
+   (`LPMCAP`, driven from the same kind of flag) are ever set in practice --
+   the flags live in a descriptor this image does not initialise.
+
+**Where this stopped:** at the register interface. The gadget-side
+behaviour -- `sub_40C6C7F8` (5596 bytes, the message pump),
+`sub_40C6E728` (6504 bytes, the control-transfer engine) and the TRB
+construction in `sub_40C70148` / `sub_40C701F0` -- was read only far enough
+to extract the register semantics above. Those functions contain the whole
+UVC / UAC / iAP2 class logic (the `EF_UVC`, `MTX_UAC` and `IAP2CTLSVC`
+object names the brief mentions belong there) and none of it is modelled,
+because none of it is a register.
+
+---
+
+## 8. The PCIe root complex (out of this model's scope, but settled)
+
+This section exists because the quartet was handed to this work as part of
+the USB complex. It is not, and nothing in `hw/arm/nikon-z63-usb.h`
+touches it. What follows is everything needed by whoever picks it up.
+
+### 8.1 It is a Synopsys DesignWare PCIe
+
+Not inferred from the vendor or from the DWC MIPI DSI host found elsewhere
+in this SoC. The firmware names DWC port-logic registers at their published
+DBI offsets, and uses them the way the DWC databook says to. All of the
+following are built as `base + offset` inside `sub_40287ED0` (0x40287ED0)
+with `base` one of the quartet:
+
+| DBI off | DWC name | what the firmware does | signature value |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 0x8BC | `PCIE_MISC_CONTROL_1_OFF` | written **1** before the read-only config writes and **0** after | bit 0 is `DBI_RO_WR_EN`. This is the single most characteristic DWC idiom there is, and it is exactly why the firmware can write LnkCap at 0x7C and the BARs at 0x10/0x14, which are read-only through normal config access |
+| 0x80C | `PCIE_LINK_WIDTH_SPEED_CONTROL` | `\|= 0x20000` | bit 17 = `PORT_LOGIC_SPEED_CHANGE` |
+| 0x890 | `GEN3_RELATED_OFF` | `&= ~1` | |
+| 0x8A8 | `GEN3_EQ_CONTROL_OFF` | `old & 0xFF0000FF \| 0x7FF00` | the preset-vector field [23:8] |
+| 0x820/0x828/0x82C/0x830 | `MSI_ADDR_LO` / `MSI_INTR0_ENABLE` / `_MASK` / `_STATUS` | programmed and polled; 0x1A000830 and 0x1A800830 also appear as bare absolute addresses | the DWC MSI controller block, verbatim |
+| 0xB40 | -- | `old & 0xFFFFFC00 \| 0x18` | **not identified**; a port-logic register outside the set this analysis could name |
+| 0x300000 + 0x200*n | **unrolled iATU**, region n | see 8.3 | the DWC "iATU unroll" window: 7 registers per region at +0x00 CTRL1, +0x04 CTRL2, +0x08 LOWER_BASE, +0x0C UPPER_BASE, +0x10 LIMIT, +0x14 LOWER_TARGET, +0x18 UPPER_TARGET, stride 0x200 |
+
+The construction of those addresses is visible as plain `ADD` instructions
+at 0x402890A4..0x402890E4 and again per switch case from 0x402892EC:
+`ADD X17, X9, #0xB40`, `ADD X1, X9, #0x890`, `ADD X2, X9, #0x8A8`,
+`ADD X29, X9, #0x300,LSL#12`.
+
+Below 0x700 the same window is the root port's **own type-1 config
+space**, which is what DBI means: `+0x04` Command/Status written 0x147,
+`+0x10`/`+0x14` BAR0/BAR1 written 0x10000/0, `+0x18`..`+0x24` the bridge
+bus-number and base/limit words, `+0x50` a capability, `+0x70`.. the PCI
+Express Capability (`+0x78` DevCtl/Sts, `+0x7C` LnkCap, `+0x80` LnkCtl/Sts,
+`+0x88` SlotCtl, `+0x8C` RootCtl, `+0x90` RootSts, `+0x98` DevCtl2, `+0xA0`
+LnkCtl2), `+0x100`.. the AER capability (`+0x104` Uncorrectable Error
+Status, bit 14 Completion Timeout), plus two extended capabilities at
+`+0x168` and `+0x258`.
+
+### 8.2 How a PCI configuration access is performed
+
+**It is the DWC iATU + outbound CFG0 region scheme. It is not ECAM at a
+fixed base, and there is no config-address/config-data register pair.**
+
+Two separate mechanisms, and they must not be confused:
+
+**(a) The root port's own config space** is read and written *directly in
+the DBI window*, at `quartet_base + config_offset`. That is every
+`MEMORY[0x04]`, `MEMORY[0x78]`, `MEMORY[0x104]` in `sub_40287ED0`. No
+viewport, no translation.
+
+**(b) Downstream config space** goes through an outbound iATU region whose
+type is CFG0. `sub_40287ED0` programs it once, during bring-up:
+
+```
+/* outbound region 0 -- the configuration window */
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x300008) = 0;          /* LOWER_BASE                   */
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x30000C) = 0;          /* UPPER_BASE                   */
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x300010) = 0xFFFF;     /* LIMIT   -> 64 KiB at AXI 0   */
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x300014) = 0;          /* LOWER_TARGET                 */
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x300018) = 0;          /* UPPER_TARGET                 */
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x300000) = old & 0xFF80C000 | 4;
+                                        /* CTRL1: TYPE = 4 = CFG0       */
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x300004) = old & 0x47000000
+                          | ((cfg->shift_mode & 1) << 28)
+                          | 0x80000000; /* CTRL2: bit 31 REGION_EN,
+                                         *        bit 28 CFG_SHIFT_MODE */
+
+/* outbound region 1 -- the memory window */
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x300208) = 0x10000;    /* LOWER_BASE                   */
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x30020C) = 0;
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x300210) = 0xFFFFF;    /* LIMIT   -> AXI 0x10000..0xFFFFF */
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x300214) = 0x10000;    /* LOWER_TARGET (identity)      */
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x300218) = 0;
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x300200) &= 0xFF80C000;/* CTRL1: TYPE = 0 = MEM        */
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x300204) = old & 0x47000000 | 0x80000000;  /* enable   */
+```
+
+`PCIE_ATU_TYPE_CFG0 = 4`, `PCIE_ATU_ENABLE = BIT(31)` and
+`PCIE_ATU_CFG_SHIFT_MODE = BIT(28)` are the published DWC encodings and
+match bit for bit.
+
+So, for a given root port:
+
+* **config window** = the port's AXI outbound aperture, offset 0..0xFFFF,
+  64 KiB. With `CFG_SHIFT_MODE` on, the offset itself carries
+  `(dev << 15) | (fn << 12) | reg` -- i.e. the 64 KiB behaves as ECAM for
+  one bus. With it off, the target bus/dev/fn comes from LOWER_TARGET,
+  which is written 0. The shift-mode bit is taken from a config byte at
+  `*(u8 *)(a3 + 60)`, and this image contains no initialised instance of
+  that structure, so **which of the two modes is used at runtime could not
+  be determined**.
+* **memory window** = the same aperture, offset 0x10000..0xFFFFF, identity
+  mapped. Consistent with the root port's own BAR0 being set to 0x10000
+  and with the bridge memory base/limit writes at +0x20/+0x24.
+
+The actual accesses are plain loads and stores through a family of
+accessors that take `(window descriptor, byte offset, buffer)` -- the shape
+of EDK2's `EFI_PCI_ROOT_BRIDGE_IO_PROTOCOL`, which fits the EDK2 PciBusDxe
+and NvmExpress code in this image:
+
+```
+sub_40289BA0  0x40289BA0   LDRB  W8, [X8,X22]   at 0x40289CA8   (read byte)
+sub_40289DCC  0x40289DCC                                        (read halfword)
+sub_4028A068  0x4028A068                                        (write byte)
+... and the dword/qword members of the same family, 0x40289F18
+    .. 0x4028B1BC
+```
+
+Each resolves `X8` from an 8-case jump table on `descriptor->space_id` and
+then checks for a completion timeout:
+
+```
+sub_40289CE4  0x40289CE4
+    v = *(u32 *)(base + 0x104);          /* AER Uncorrectable Error Status */
+    if (v & 0x4000) *(u32 *)(base + 0x104) = 0x4000;   /* W1C Completion Timeout */
+    return (v & 0x4000) != 0;            /* caller turns this into error 4 */
+```
+
+**Access width is NOT uniformly 32-bit here** -- unlike the USB complex.
+`LDRB` at 0x40289CA8 and the halfword/dword members of the family mean the
+config and memory windows must accept 1, 2 and 4-byte accesses. Any model
+of this must set `valid.min_access_size = 1`.
+
+### 8.3 Instances and the index space
+
+Eight jump-table cases, bases:
+
+```
+0 -> 0x1A000000   1 -> 0x1A400000   2 -> 0x1A800000   3 -> 0x1AC00000
+4 -> 0x400000000  5 -> 0x500000000  6 -> 0x600000000  7 -> 0x700000000
+```
+
+Cases 0..3 are unambiguously the four root ports' DBI windows: they are
+where the config header, the port logic and the iATU live, they are 4 MiB
+apart and every offset used (max 0x300218) fits inside 4 MiB.
+
+Cases 4..7 are the SoC region table's `PCIe_mem_AXI0` (0x400000000) and
+`PCIe_mem_AXI1` (0x600000000) halves. `sub_40287ED0` builds the *same* DBI
+offsets against them (`MOV X9, #0; MOVK X9, #4,LSL#32` at 0x40289240,
+followed by the same `ADD X29, X9, #0x300,LSL#12`), which would only make
+sense if those are 64-bit aliases of the same DBI windows. **This was not
+settled.** Either reading is possible from the code: a 64-bit DBI alias, or
+eight generic "space id" cases of which only four are ever exercised on
+this SoC. Note that `sub_40287ED0`'s case 0 is `MOV X11, XZR` -- a null
+base -- while `sub_40289CE4`'s case 0 is 0x1A000000, which already shows
+the 8-case dispatch is a generic shape, not a tight instance list.
+
+Only instances 0 and 2 appear as bare absolute addresses anywhere
+(0x1A000080, 0x1A000830, 0x1A800080, 0x1A800830 -- LnkCtl/Sts and
+MSI_INTR0_STATUS), which fits `PCIE00` and `PCIE10`, one port per
+controller.
+
+### 8.4 The controller wrappers at 0x17B00000 and 0x17B80000
+
+Outside the DBI entirely, and this is where the link-up state lives. Two
+blocks, one per controller, each with a two-port layout at +0x000 and
++0x200:
+
+| off | what | established at |
+|---|---|---|
+| 0x0B4 / 0x2B4 | `[5:0]` is the **LTSSM state**. The link-up poll waits for it to read `0x11` = L0. Bit 6 is tested separately in the ISR | poll in `sub_40287ED0` (`(*v69 & 0x3F) == 0x11`), bit 6 at pcie.txt-equivalent `MEMORY[0x17B000B4] & 0x40` |
+| 0x0E4 / 0x2E4 | interrupt-related | `sub_40287ED0` |
+| 0x0E8 / 0x2E8 | interrupt status; bit 15 dispatches a callback | the block's ISR |
+| 0x054 / 0x254 | control | `sub_40287ED0` |
+| 0xE4C | shared control; `\|= 7` or `\|= 0x700`, and bits cleared with `~4` / `~0x301` / `~0x513` | five sites in `sub_40287ED0` |
+
+`sub_40287ED0` chooses the wrapper with `((1 << space_id) & 0x33) != 0 ?
+0x17B00000 : 0x17B80000`, i.e. space ids {0,1,4,5} belong to controller 0
+and {2,3,6,7} to controller 1, and the `+0x200` offset picks the port
+within the controller. That is **two controllers x two ports = four root
+ports**, exactly matching `EXS_PCIE00/01/10/11`.
+
+**The link-up state machine**, transcribed:
+
+```
+*(u32 *)(base + 0x258) |= 1;            /* last DBI write before waiting */
+for (n = 500; n; n--) {
+    if ((*(u32 *)(wrapper + port*0x200 + 0xB4) & 0x3F) == 0x11) {
+        host->link_up = 1;              /* *(u8 *)(a1 + 64) = 1 */
+        return 0;
+    }
+    delay(1);
+}
+return 5;                               /* timeout */
+```
+
+and the consequence of the timeout is clean: `sub_40289B30` (0x40289B30)
+gates every later config/memory access on `host->link_up` and on an
+EXS 0x17A00000 status bit, returning error 2 if either is clear. So a model
+that never brings the link up makes the whole PCIe stack report "no
+device" rather than hang or fault. That is the cheapest possible first
+step.
+
+### 8.5 What this means for QEMU
+
+QEMU already has `hw/pci-host/designware.c` (`designware-pcie-host` /
+`designware-pcie-root`), and it is the right starting point -- but it does
+**not** match this firmware out of the box. Four concrete gaps, all checked
+against the tree in this repository:
+
+1. **iATU window.** QEMU implements only the viewport-indexed iATU
+   (`DESIGNWARE_PCIE_ATU_VIEWPORT 0x900`, `designware.c:44`). This firmware
+   uses the **unrolled** iATU at DBI+0x300000 with a 0x200 stride and never
+   touches 0x900. The fix is small -- an alias that maps unrolled region
+   *n* onto QEMU's `viewports[OUTBOUND][n]` state -- but it is required.
+2. **CFG0 outbound regions.** QEMU's `designware_pcie_update_viewport()`
+   only acts when `viewport->cr[0] == DESIGNWARE_PCIE_ATU_TYPE_MEM`
+   (`designware.c:282`). This firmware's region 0 is `TYPE = 4 = CFG0`, and
+   that region *is* the configuration access path. Without CFG0 support
+   there is no config access at all.
+3. **DBI_RO_WR_EN at 0x8BC**, plus 0x890, 0x8A8, 0xB40, 0x258 and the
+   extended capability at 0x168. QEMU implements none of them. 0x8BC
+   matters most: with it unimplemented, the firmware's writes to LnkCap
+   (0x7C) and to BAR0 (0x10) are either dropped or mis-handled.
+4. **The wrapper at 0x17B00000 / 0x17B80000 is not a DWC block at all** and
+   QEMU's model will never answer it. The LTSSM register at +0x0B4 / +0x2B4
+   has to be modelled separately, or the 500-iteration timeout taken.
+
+The payoff is real: with (1), (2) and (4) in place, a QEMU `nvme` device
+behind `designware-pcie-host` would be enumerated by the firmware's own
+EDK2 `PciBusDxe` / `NvmExpress.c`, which is the camera's CFexpress path.
+That is a much better trade than modelling NVMe by hand, and nothing in
+this document should be read as a recommendation to do the latter.
+
+**Not attempted here, by instruction and because it would be the wrong
+file:** no PCIe model was written. The register-level facts above are the
+deliverable. In particular no NVMe and no PCI device model exists anywhere
+in this work.
